"t-8a04484e-a32e-4f18-952c-f357f6db38ed","8a04484e-a32e-4f18-952c-f357f6db38ed","ai_stage:assistant","SPAN","ai_stage:assistant","2026-09-29 16:08:27.774000","","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","","{}","{}","{}","0","{""messages"":[{""role"":""system"",""content"":""You are the coding assistant inside a browser-based build workspace. A candidate directs you through a chat; you write the code. Their project runs live in a preview next to the chat.\n\nYou are a normal, capable, honest coding assistant. The candidate is being assessed on how they direct you — you are not part of the test. Do what is asked, well. Do not be obtuse, do not pad, do not flatter.\n\nHOW TO WORK\n- Read the current project files (provided with each request) before you change anything. Build on what exists.\n- Say what you assumed. If a request is ambiguous in a way that would materially change the design, ask ONE focused question instead of guessing — and in that case return no files.\n- Do not silently expand scope. Do what was asked; mention anything extra you think is worth doing rather than doing it.\n- Verify before you claim. You cannot run the code or see the preview. Never say something \""works\"", \""was tested\"" or \""is verified\"". Say what you expect to happen and what the candidate should check in the preview. If you are unsure of an API or a behaviour, say so plainly.\n- If part of a request is blocked or risky, do everything else and state plainly what you left and why. If you notice a problem in an earlier decision (yours or theirs), say so.\n- Push back, politely and specifically, when a request conflicts with the brief's constraints or will cause a problem. Then do what the candidate decides.\n\nENVIRONMENT\n- The project is Vite 5 + React 18, already running. Edit src/App.jsx, add files under src/, use plain CSS files. The preview reloads on save.\n- Return the COMPLETE new contents of every file you create or change. Files you do not return are unchanged. Never return diffs, and never write placeholders such as \""// rest unchanged\"".\n- Dependencies are limited to react, react-dom and vite. Prefer no new dependency. If one is genuinely needed you may add it to package.json — but the install runs in a browser sandbox (no native modules), and react, react-dom and vite are pinned by the environment.\n- There is no backend, no real network service and no secret. Use local data and in-memory logic. Use .jsx for React components.\n- Keep files reasonably small (aim for under ~300 lines) and readable.\n\nOUTPUT (structured)\n- message: what you did, what you assumed, and what to check. Plain, brief, no filler.\n- files: every file you create or change, complete. Empty if you are only asking a question or discussing.\n- reasoning: your own short reasoning for this turn — how you interpreted the request, the key decisions, and any doubts. A reviewer may read it as assistant-side context.\n\nThe brief below is the candidate's brief; it is the only description of the task you have.\n\n<challenge title=\""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter\"" timebox_minutes=\""120\"">\nEnterprise Australian assessment grounded in SFIA 9 standards for Department of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water (DCCEEW).\n\n# AI Resume Screener & Bias-Resistant Applicant Review\n\n## The Problem\nRecruitment teams review hundreds of technical resumes and increasingly rely on AI copilots. However, unguided AI screening can hallucinate non-existent candidate skills or propagate illegal demographic biases. You will build a review interface that parses candidate submissions, highlights verifiable skills, and flags AI hallucinations.\n\n## Constraints & Requirements\n1. **Bias Mitigation**: Automatically mask non-job-relevant demographic proxies (e.g. school name, graduation date, candidate photo) under Fair Work guidelines.\n2. **Hallucination Verification**: Require human recruiters to verify that claimed skills actually appear in the raw resume excerpt before approving.\n3. **Interactive Filter**: Filter candidates by must-have skills (React, TypeScript) and minimum verified score.\n</challenge>\n\nCRITICAL: Respond ONLY with a valid JSON object strictly matching this schema:\n{\n  \""$schema\"": \""http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\"",\n  \""type\"": \""object\"",\n  \""properties\"": {\n    \""message\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""string\"",\n      \""description\"": \""What you tell the candidate: what you did, what you assumed, what to check.\""\n    },\n    \""files\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""array\"",\n      \""items\"": {\n        \""type\"": \""object\"",\n        \""properties\"": {\n          \""path\"": {\n            \""type\"": \""string\""\n          },\n          \""contents\"": {\n            \""type\"": \""string\""\n          }\n        },\n        \""required\"": [\n          \""path\"",\n          \""contents\""\n        ],\n        \""additionalProperties\"": false\n      },\n      \""description\"": \""Complete new contents of every file you create or change. Whole files only — never diffs.\""\n    },\n    \""reasoning\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""string\"",\n      \""description\"": \""Your own brief reasoning for this turn: interpretation, key decisions and any open doubts.\""\n    }\n  },\n  \""required\"": [\n    \""message\"",\n    \""files\"",\n    \""reasoning\""\n  ],\n  \""additionalProperties\"": false\n}""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""Act as a Principal Frontend Architect and HR Compliance Specialist familiar with Australian Fair Work guidelines (Fair Work Act 2009, s.351 protected attributes).\n\nWe are building an \""AI Resume Screener & Bias-Resistant Applicant Review\"" interface. Before writing any component or UI code, interrogate the brief to lock down domain boundaries, anti-bias masking rules, and verification contracts.\n\nTasks:\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix:\n   - Identify direct demographic attributes and indirect proxies that must be stripped in \""Blind Review Mode\"" under Fair Work principles (e.g., graduation year as an age proxy, institution names as socioeconomic/racial proxies, residential address/suburb, photos, gendered honorifics/pronouns).\n   - Formulate a strict schema separating `MaskedDemographics` from `VerifiableTechnicalData`.\n2. Hallucination Failure Taxonomy:\n   - Detail at least 4 ways AI screeners hallucinate candidate competency (e.g., semantic confusion like mistaking 'Vue reactivity' for 'React.js', inferring unstated frameworks from adjacent tools, attributing team achievements as individual skills, or fabrications based on project titles).\n3. Verification Invariants:\n   - Define the deterministic invariant: A skill cannot enter the \""Verified\"" state without an exact source excerpt span and recruiter confirmation.\n\nConclude with 3 clarifying questions on filtering thresholds and workflow edge cases before we proceed to component architecture.""},{""role"":""assistant"",""content"":""No components or UI code were written yet, as requested. Here are the domain boundaries, masking rules, hallucination taxonomy, verification invariants, and three clarifying questions.\n\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix\n\nDirect protected attributes under Fair Work Act 2009 s.351 and related principles:\nage, sex, gender, gender identity, sexual orientation, race, colour, ethnicity, national origin, disability, religion, marital status, pregnancy, family/carer status, political opinion, social origin.\n\nIndirect demographic proxies that must be masked in Blind Review Mode:\n- School / university / institution names\n- Graduation year or any education date range\n- Residential address, suburb, postcode\n- Candidate photo / headshot\n- Gendered honorifics and pronouns\n- Full legal name, especially given/family name (use neutral candidate ID)\n- Visa status, citizenship, nationality\n- Languages (unless the role explicitly requires a language)\n- Professional memberships that reveal religion, race, gender, disability, or political affiliation\n- Age-correlated exact dates, e.g. date of birth, early career start year\n- Portfolio URLs or personal sites that may reveal demographic information\n\nStrict schema separation:\n\nMaskedDemographics:\n  direct: age, sex, gender, race, ethnicity, disability, religion, sexual orientation, marital status, pregnancy, family status, political opinion, national origin\n  indirect: institutionName, schoolName, graduationYear, educationDateRange, address, suburb, postcode, photo, honorific, pronoun, givenName, familyName, visaStatus, citizenship, nationality, languages, professionalMemberships, dateOfBirth, ageRelatedDates, identifyingUrls\n\nVerifiableTechnicalData:\n  candidateId\n  maskedResumeText (raw text with all MaskedDemographics redacted)\n  technicalClaims[] as:\n    skillId\n    name (normalised, e.g. lowercase)\n    sourceExcerpts[] as:\n      text (exact substring)\n      start (character offset)\n      end (character offset)\n    suggestedByAI (boolean)\n    verificationStatus: unverified | verified | rejected\n    recruiterConfirmations[] as:\n      recruiterId\n      timestamp\n      decision\n  projects[] / experience[] containing only job-relevant technical detail\n\nRecruiters never need unmasked demographic data to verify technical claims.\n\n2. Hallucination Failure Taxonomy\n\nAt least four distinct AI hallucination modes:\n\n1. Semantic / technology confusion: mistaking Vue reactivity for React.js, React Native for React, or AngularJS for Angular.\n2. Adjacent-tool inference: seeing Webpack, Babel, or Next.js and inferring React proficiency without any direct mention of React; seeing Tailwind and inferring CSS expertise; seeing Redis and inferring a backend language.\n3. Team/project attribution: a resume says the team built a feature or the candidate worked on a React codebase, and the AI promotes that into individual ownership such as led React architecture or built the system alone.\n4. Fabrication from titles or project names: inferring skills purely from job titles like React Developer or project names like React dashboard without supporting bullet evidence.\n5. Course/certificate inflation: completing a React course or bootcamp is extracted as production-level React competency when the resume does not claim production use.\n6. Keyword false positives: matching the word reactive or reaction and treating it as React, or matching the word type in prose as TypeScript.\n\n3. Verification Invariants\n\nA skill cannot enter the Verified state unless all of these are true:\n- The skill has at least one exact source excerpt span in the raw, masked, technical resume text.\n- The source excerpt exactly supports the claimed skill; fuzzy, inferred, or adjacent-tool matches do not qualify.\n- A human recruiter explicitly confirms the match in the review UI.\n- The confirmation is recorded with recruiter ID and timestamp.\n\nAdditional deterministic invariants:\n- AI confidence, job title, project title, or another skill can suggest a claim but can never verify it.\n- A claimed skill with no exact source excerpt must remain Unverified or be marked Unsupported.\n- If a recruiter rejects a previously verified skill, its status returns to Rejected/Disputed and must not count toward the verified score.\n- Masking is applied before verification. Demographic spans are never shown to the recruiter in the verification excerpt; only the technical evidence is shown.\n\n3 Clarifying Questions Before Component Architecture\n\n1. Filtering thresholds: Should must-have skills mean every must-have skill is individually verified, or should candidates pass if they have verified evidence for at least N of them? How should minimum verified score be calculated: binary per skill, weighted by relevance, or based on the number of verified source excerpts?\n\n2. Workflow edge cases: If a candidate has three mentions of React, one exact and two inferred, is React considered verified or only partially verified? If a recruiter later rejects a previously confirmed skill, should the candidate be re-evaluated immediately and possibly removed from shortlist?\n\n3. Audit and unmasking: Do you need an immutable audit log of every verification/rejection action with recruiter ID and timestamp? Should Blind Review Mode be default-on and irreversible for a candidate, or should an authorised recruiter be able to override masking with a recorded reason?""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""Approved. Now design the system architecture and state model using React and TypeScript.\n\nDeliverables:\n1. Strongly Typed Domain Models:\n   - `Candidate`: Including unique ID, raw resume text, and masked demographic profile.\n   - `ExtractedSkill`: Containing `skillName`, `category`, `rawExcerpt`, `confidence`, `verificationStatus` ('UNVERIFIED' | 'CONFIRMED' | 'FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION'), and `charOffsets: [start, end]`.\n   - `FilterCriteria`: Must-have skills array (`['React', 'TypeScript']`), minimum verified score, and blind-mode toggle.\n2. Component Hierarchy & Props:\n   - Define exact prop contracts for:\n     * `<CandidateList>`: Virtualized/accessible candidate selection panel.\n     * `<ResumeViewer>`: Dual-pane interface with masked header and highlighted excerpt anchors.\n     * `<SkillVerificationMatrix>`: Interactive badge list where recruiters manually audit AI-extracted claims against text spans.\n     * `<FilterBar>`: Controlled multi-select and score threshold slider.\n3. Performance & Re-render Strategy:\n   - Specify state management approach (e.g., Zustand or useReducer + memoized selectors) to prevent full-tree re-renders during rapid keyboard navigation or verification clicks.\n4. Architectural Trade-off Defense:\n   - Document a structured comparison defending **Client-side filtering/memoization vs. Server-side pagination & query evaluation** for candidate pools between 50 and 5,000 applicants (touching latency, DOM memory footprint, and instantaneous regex search).""},{""role"":""assistant"",""content"":""Architecture and state model locked. I added `src/types.ts` with the strongly typed domain models and component prop contracts. Summary of the design:\n\n1. Domain models: `Candidate` holds `rawResumeText`, `maskedResumeText`, `maskedDemographics`, and `extractedSkills`. `ExtractedSkill` has `skillName`, `category`, `rawExcerpt`, `confidence`, `verificationStatus` (`UNVERIFIED | CONFIRMED | FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`), and exact `charOffsets` for source anchoring. `FilterCriteria` has `mustHaveSkills`, `minimumVerifiedScore`, and `blindMode`.\n\n2. Component hierarchy: `App` owns state and renders `FilterBar` (controlled filter controls), `CandidateList` (virtualized accessible selection panel), `ResumeViewer` (dual-pane masked text + highlighted excerpt anchors), and `SkillVerificationMatrix` (interactive badge list for recruiter verification). Prop contracts are in `src/types.ts`.\n\n3. State management & re-render strategy: use a single `useReducer` with normalized state keyed by id, plus memoized selectors (`filteredCandidates`, `candidateSummaries`, `availableSkills`). Wrap `CandidateList`, `ResumeViewer`, and `SkillVerificationMatrix` in `React.memo`; pass only stable props and callbacks. Candidate list uses windowing (visible row slice) to keep DOM footprint small. Verification actions update only the target skill's status; derived `verifiedScore` is computed in a selector, never stored, preventing stale state.\n\n4. Trade-off defense: For 50–5,000 candidates, client-side filtering + memoization + windowing is preferable. Latency is near-zero after initial load; regex search across 5,000 local strings is fast (<10ms); windowing keeps DOM memory small. Server-side pagination adds RTT on every filter and complicates cross-page excerpt highlighting. Client-side becomes risky beyond ~5,000 or when data must be remotely audit-logged; server-side query/pagination should be adopted above that threshold or when the data source is an API with mandatory server-side evaluation.\n\nWhat to check in the preview: the new `src/types.ts` compiles and exports all types; no UI was rendered yet, so verify the type definitions cover the agreed contracts.\n\n[Files written: src/types.ts]""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""<project_files>\n=== README.md ===\n# Reviewer console — starter data\n\nYou are prototyping a reviewer console for an AI feature that summarises employee\nsurvey comments. Everything here is **synthetic**: invented people, teams and\ncomments for a fictional company.\n\n## Data (`src/data/`)\n\n- `teams.json` — `departments` and `teams`. Each team belongs to one department.\n- `responses.json` — one entry per **respondent**: `{ id, teamId, comment }`.\n  `comment` may be an empty string.\n- `summaries.json` — output of a (stubbed) LLM summariser. Each summary covers a\n  `team` or a `department` (`groupType` / `groupId`) and has `themes`; each theme\n  has a `title`, a `summary` and the `claims` it makes.\n\nImport them directly, e.g. `import teams from \""./data/teams.json\""`.\n\n## Running\n\nThe preview is already live. Edit `src/App.jsx` and add files under `src/`.\n\n\n=== index.html ===\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\""en\"">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\""UTF-8\"" />\n    <meta name=\""viewport\"" content=\""width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"" />\n    <title>Candidate project</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\""root\""></div>\n    <script type=\""module\"" src=\""/src/main.jsx\""></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n=== package.json ===\n{\n  \""name\"": \""candidate-project\"",\n  \""private\"": true,\n  \""version\"": \""0.0.0\"",\n  \""type\"": \""module\"",\n  \""scripts\"": {\n    \""dev\"": \""vite\"",\n    \""build\"": \""vite build\""\n  },\n  \""dependencies\"": {\n    \""react\"": \""18.3.1\"",\n    \""react-dom\"": \""18.3.1\""\n  },\n  \""devDependencies\"": {\n    \""vite\"": \""5.4.21\""\n  },\n  \""overrides\"": {\n    \""rollup\"": \""npm:@rollup/wasm-node\""\n  }\n}\n\n\n=== src/App.jsx ===\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <main className=\""shell\"">\n      <h1>Your project starts here</h1>\n      <p>\n        Nothing has been built yet. Describe what you want in the chat and the\n        assistant will write the code.\n      </p>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n\n\n=== src/data/responses.json ===\n[\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r01\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Release train is monthly and everything piles in the last week. Rollback ownership is unclear, it feels like nobody's job.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r02\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""I like my team but the monthly release crunch is exhausting.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r03\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Would love clear rollback ownership when a release goes wrong.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r04\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Deploys are fine. Wish we had more time for tech debt.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r05\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Remote days are great, I'd happily do more of them.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r06\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r07\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Being the only person on the overnight reconciliation rota is wearing me down.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r08\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r09\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Good team. Tech debt keeps getting pushed.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r10\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""On-call is heavy and alerts are noisy.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r11\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Alert noise at night is the worst part of on-call.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r12\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""I'd like a clearer path to senior.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r13\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Planning is good this quarter, priorities finally feel stable.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r14\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r15\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Too many meetings on Tuesdays and Thursdays.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r16\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r17\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""On-call rota fairness could be better, same few people get paged.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r18\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Since I moved from the Perth office to lead the Atlas migration I've had no backup.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r19\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""App store approvals take forever and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r20\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Love the team, worried about headcount.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r21\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Customers get stuck at the data import step and the docs are out of date, so we field the same questions daily.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r22\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r23\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Import step is confusing for customers and the docs are out of date.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r24\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r25\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Would like more time with customers rather than internal meetings.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r26\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Ticket volume spikes after every release.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r27\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Ticket backlog after releases is stressful.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r28\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Good tooling, but escalation paths are unclear.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r29\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Enjoy the work, wish escalation to engineering was faster.\""\n  }\n]\n\n\n=== src/data/summaries.json ===\n[\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-payments\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release pressure\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Engineers feel the monthly release train creates a last-week crunch, and rollback ownership is unclear.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The monthly release train creates a last-week crunch.\"",\n          \""Rollback ownership is unclear.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Working pattern\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Almost everyone in the team wants to work fully remotely.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Almost everyone wants to work fully remotely.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Wellbeing\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""One engineer covering the overnight reconciliation rota alone is close to burning out.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""One engineer covering the overnight reconciliation rota alone is close to burning out.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Tech debt\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Tech debt keeps being deprioritised.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Tech debt keeps being deprioritised.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-platform\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""On-call load\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""On-call is heavy, alerts are noisy, and the load falls on the same few people.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""On-call is heavy.\"",\n          \""Alerts are noisy, especially at night.\"",\n          \""The same few people get paged.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Meetings\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""There is a Friday retro that people find useful.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""There is a Friday retro that people find useful.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Career growth\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""People want a clearer path to senior.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""People want a clearer path to senior.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-mobile\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Migration ownership\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""The Perth-based lead of the Atlas migration has no backup.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The Perth-based lead of the Atlas migration has no backup.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release approvals\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-onboarding\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Import step\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Customers struggle with the data import step and the docs are out of date.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Customers struggle with the data import step.\"",\n          \""The docs are out of date.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-support\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Post-release load\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Ticket volume and backlog spike after releases.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Ticket volume spikes after releases.\"",\n          \""The ticket backlog after releases is stressful.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Escalation\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Escalation paths to engineering are unclear and slow.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Escalation paths are unclear.\"",\n          \""Escalation to engineering is slow.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-engineering\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""department\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""engineering\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release and rollout friction\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""A monthly release crunch, unclear rollback ownership, and slow app store approvals that nobody owns.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The monthly release train creates a last-week crunch.\"",\n          \""Rollback ownership is unclear.\"",\n          \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""On-call\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""On-call load is heavy and alerts are noisy.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""On-call load is heavy.\"",\n          \""Alerts are noisy.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\n\n=== src/data/teams.json ===\n{\n  \""departments\"": [\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""engineering\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""customer-success\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Customer Success\""\n    }\n  ],\n  \""teams\"": [\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""payments\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Payments\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""platform\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Platform\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""mobile\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Mobile\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""onboarding\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Onboarding\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""customer-success\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""support\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Support\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""customer-success\""\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n\n=== src/main.jsx ===\nimport { StrictMode } from \""react\"";\nimport { createRoot } from \""react-dom/client\"";\nimport App from \""./App.jsx\"";\nimport \""./styles.css\"";\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\""root\"")).render(\n  <StrictMode>\n    <App />\n  </StrictMode>\n);\n\n\n=== src/styles.css ===\n:root {\n  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, \""Segoe UI\"", Roboto, sans-serif;\n  color: #1a1a1a;\n  background: #fafaf9;\n}\n\n* {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}\n\nbody {\n  margin: 0;\n}\n\n.shell {\n  max-width: 720px;\n  margin: 12vh auto;\n  padding: 0 24px;\n}\n\n.shell h1 {\n  font-size: 1.6rem;\n  margin: 0 0 0.5rem;\n}\n\n.shell p {\n  color: #555;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n}\n\n\n=== src/types.ts ===\n/**\n * Strongly typed domain models for the AI Resume Screener.\n * Shared by future components and state layer.\n */\n\nexport type VerificationStatus =\n  | \""UNVERIFIED\""\n  | \""CONFIRMED\""\n  | \""FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION\"";\n\nexport interface RedactionSpan {\n  /** Stable ID for the redaction, e.g. \""demo-001\"". */\n  id: string;\n  /** Which protected attribute or proxy category triggered the redaction. */\n  reason: string;\n  /** Exact character offsets in the raw resume text. */\n  charOffsets: [number, number];\n}\n\nexport interface MaskedDemographics {\n  /** Direct protected attributes detected and redacted from view. */\n  direct: string[];\n  /** Indirect proxies detected and redacted from view. */\n  indirect: string[];\n  /** Concrete spans that were replaced/masked in maskedResumeText. */\n  redactions: RedactionSpan[];\n}\n\nexport interface ExtractedSkill {\n  /** Stable ID, e.g. \""skill-react-1\"". */\n  skillId: string;\n  /** Normalised display name, e.g. \""React\"". */\n  skillName: string;\n  /** e.g. \""frontend\"", \""language\"", \""tooling\"". */\n  category: string;\n  /** Exact substring from raw resume that the AI used as evidence. */\n  rawExcerpt: string;\n  /** 0..1 confidence from the AI extractor. */\n  confidence: number;\n  /** Human verification status. */\n  verificationStatus: VerificationStatus;\n  /** Exact character offset of rawExcerpt in rawResumeText. */\n  charOffsets: [number, number];\n}\n\nexport interface Candidate {\n  /** Synthetic applicant ID, e.g. \""c-001\"". */\n  id: string;\n  /** Original resume text as submitted. Used only for verification. */\n  rawResumeText: string;\n  /** Demographic profile after masking. */\n  maskedDemographics: MaskedDemographics;\n  /** Resume text with all demographic proxies replaced by [REDACTED]. */\n  maskedResumeText: string;\n  /** AI-extracted technical claims. */\n  extractedSkills: ExtractedSkill[];\n  /** Derived verified score, e.g. count of CONFIRMED skills / total must-haves. */\n  verifiedScore: number;\n}\n\nexport interface FilterCriteria {\n  /** Must-have skill names, normalised case-insensitive. */\n  mustHaveSkills: string[];\n  /** Minimum verified score threshold, e.g. 0 to 1 or 0 to number skills. */\n  minimumVerifiedScore: number;\n  /** When true, hide all demographic proxies in the viewer. */\n  blindMode: boolean;\n}\n\n/* ---------- Component prop contracts ---------- */\n\nexport interface CandidateSummary {\n  id: string;\n  maskedName: string;\n  verifiedSkillCount: number;\n  mustHaveSkillsPresent: boolean;\n  verifiedScore: number;\n}\n\nexport interface CandidateListProps {\n  /** Filtered and sorted candidate summaries to render. */\n  candidates: CandidateSummary[];\n  selectedCandidateId: string | null;\n  onSelectCandidate: (id: string) => void;\n  /** Total number of candidates before/after filtering (for a11y). */\n  totalCount: number;\n  /** Keyboard navigation and aria helpers. */\n  ariaLabel?: string;\n  /** Height of the visible list container. */\n  height: number;\n  /** Fixed row height for virtualization. */\n  rowHeight: number;\n}\n\nexport interface ResumeViewerProps {\n  candidate: Candidate | null;\n  /** Skill currently being inspected; used to highlight its excerpt. */\n  activeSkillId: string | null;\n  /** Called when a highlighted excerpt is clicked. */\n  onFocusSkill: (skillId: string | null) => void;\n  /** Scroll a specific excerpt into view. */\n  onScrollToExcerpt: (charOffsets: [number, number]) => void;\n  blindMode: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface SkillVerificationMatrixProps {\n  skills: ExtractedSkill[];\n  activeSkillId: string | null;\n  onFocusSkill: (skillId: string | null) => void;\n  /** Recruiter confirms or flags a skill. */\n  onVerify: (skillId: string, status: Exclude<VerificationStatus, \""UNVERIFIED\"">) => void;\n  /** Reset a previous decision if needed. */\n  onReset: (skillId: string) => void;\n  disabled?: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface FilterBarProps {\n  filterCriteria: FilterCriteria;\n  /** All skill names available in the candidate pool, deduplicated. */\n  availableSkills: string[];\n  /** Highest possible verified score, used to clamp the slider. */\n  maxVerifiedScore: number;\n  onCriteriaChange: (criteria: FilterCriteria) => void;\n  disabled?: boolean;\n}\n\n\n=== vite.config.js ===\nimport { defineConfig } from \""vite\"";\n\n// No @vitejs/plugin-react on purpose: esbuild compiles JSX, which keeps\n// `npm install` fast inside the browser sandbox.\nexport default defineConfig({\n  esbuild: { jsx: \""automatic\"" },\n});\n\n</project_files>\n\nImplement the core module in TypeScript and React following the approved design.\n\nTechnical Guardrails:\n1. Pure Verification Engine:\n   - Implement a deterministic helper `verifySkillExcerpt(rawText: string, claimedSkill: string, excerpt: string): VerificationResult`.\n   - The engine must catch hallucinations by verifying that the claimed skill actually exists within the specified excerpt. If the AI asserts a skill exists without matching substring boundaries, mark it automatically as `FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`.\n2. React Components:\n   - Implement the `<ResumeViewer>` and `<SkillVerificationMatrix>` with full accessibility (ARIA labels, keyboard navigation for approving/flagging with `Enter`/`Space`).\n   - Implement the `<FilterBar>` using React 18/19 idioms (`useMemo`, `useDeferredValue` or transition hooks for input filtering without frame drops).\n3. Mock Dataset:\n   - Provide a realistic test dataset of 5 candidates containing:\n     * 1 candidate with subtle AI hallucinations (e.g., \""React\"" claimed, but resume only mentions \""Reactive Extensions for Angular\"").\n     * 1 candidate with clear demographic proxies needing masking (graduation year 1988, prestigious school, regional address).\n     * 3 valid candidates with varying verified scores for React and TypeScript.\n4. Hygiene:\n   - Zero placeholder comments (`// TODO`, `// Add logic here`).\n   - Full TypeScript types with no `any`.""}]}","","{""response_format"":""{\""type\"":\""json_object\""}"",""turnSeq"":""5"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter"",""maxTokens"":""32000"",""effort"":""medium"",""stage"":""assistant""}","","","[""assistant"",""workspace_chat""]","9e446791d778e493c754add0c5655b85e48aeb09","","[]"
"93f573e3-b443-42e1-b1ff-11b2fae431da","80dbd2fa-e461-4bfb-b261-021f309c3507","candidate_turn","EVENT","user_message_received","2026-09-29 16:08:27.761000","","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","","{}","{}","{}","0","{""message"":""Implement the core module in TypeScript and React following the approved design.\n\nTechnical Guardrails:\n1. Pure Verification Engine:\n   - Implement a deterministic helper `verifySkillExcerpt(rawText: string, claimedSkill: string, excerpt: string): VerificationResult`.\n   - The engine must catch hallucinations by verifying that the claimed skill actually exists within the specified excerpt. If the AI asserts a skill exists without matching substring boundaries, mark it automatically as `FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`.\n2. React Components:\n   - Implement the `<ResumeViewer>` and `<SkillVerificationMatrix>` with full accessibility (ARIA labels, keyboard navigation for approving/flagging with `Enter`/`Space`).\n   - Implement the `<FilterBar>` using React 18/19 idioms (`useMemo`, `useDeferredValue` or transition hooks for input filtering without frame drops).\n3. Mock Dataset:\n   - Provide a realistic test dataset of 5 candidates containing:\n     * 1 candidate with subtle AI hallucinations (e.g., \""React\"" claimed, but resume only mentions \""Reactive Extensions for Angular\"").\n     * 1 candidate with clear demographic proxies needing masking (graduation year 1988, prestigious school, regional address).\n     * 3 valid candidates with varying verified scores for React and TypeScript.\n4. Hygiene:\n   - Zero placeholder comments (`// TODO`, `// Add logic here`).\n   - Full TypeScript types with no `any`.""}","","{""charCount"":""1403"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter""}","","","[""candidate_prompt"",""workspace_chat""]","9e446791d778e493c754add0c5655b85e48aeb09","t-80dbd2fa-e461-4bfb-b261-021f309c3507","[]"
"t-80dbd2fa-e461-4bfb-b261-021f309c3507","80dbd2fa-e461-4bfb-b261-021f309c3507","candidate_turn","SPAN","candidate_turn","2026-09-29 16:08:27.760000","","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","","{}","{}","{}","0","{""message"":""Implement the core module in TypeScript and React following the approved design.\n\nTechnical Guardrails:\n1. Pure Verification Engine:\n   - Implement a deterministic helper `verifySkillExcerpt(rawText: string, claimedSkill: string, excerpt: string): VerificationResult`.\n   - The engine must catch hallucinations by verifying that the claimed skill actually exists within the specified excerpt. If the AI asserts a skill exists without matching substring boundaries, mark it automatically as `FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`.\n2. React Components:\n   - Implement the `<ResumeViewer>` and `<SkillVerificationMatrix>` with full accessibility (ARIA labels, keyboard navigation for approving/flagging with `Enter`/`Space`).\n   - Implement the `<FilterBar>` using React 18/19 idioms (`useMemo`, `useDeferredValue` or transition hooks for input filtering without frame drops).\n3. Mock Dataset:\n   - Provide a realistic test dataset of 5 candidates containing:\n     * 1 candidate with subtle AI hallucinations (e.g., \""React\"" claimed, but resume only mentions \""Reactive Extensions for Angular\"").\n     * 1 candidate with clear demographic proxies needing masking (graduation year 1988, prestigious school, regional address).\n     * 3 valid candidates with varying verified scores for React and TypeScript.\n4. Hygiene:\n   - Zero placeholder comments (`// TODO`, `// Add logic here`).\n   - Full TypeScript types with no `any`.""}","","{""charCount"":""1403"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter""}","","","[""candidate_prompt"",""workspace_chat""]","9e446791d778e493c754add0c5655b85e48aeb09","","[]"
"t-9e7fcdba-f20b-4d97-9774-c9979d3af5f7","9e7fcdba-f20b-4d97-9774-c9979d3af5f7","ai_stage:assistant","SPAN","ai_stage:assistant","2026-09-29 16:00:39.329000","","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","","{}","{}","{}","0","{""messages"":[{""role"":""system"",""content"":""You are the coding assistant inside a browser-based build workspace. A candidate directs you through a chat; you write the code. Their project runs live in a preview next to the chat.\n\nYou are a normal, capable, honest coding assistant. The candidate is being assessed on how they direct you — you are not part of the test. Do what is asked, well. Do not be obtuse, do not pad, do not flatter.\n\nHOW TO WORK\n- Read the current project files (provided with each request) before you change anything. Build on what exists.\n- Say what you assumed. If a request is ambiguous in a way that would materially change the design, ask ONE focused question instead of guessing — and in that case return no files.\n- Do not silently expand scope. Do what was asked; mention anything extra you think is worth doing rather than doing it.\n- Verify before you claim. You cannot run the code or see the preview. Never say something \""works\"", \""was tested\"" or \""is verified\"". Say what you expect to happen and what the candidate should check in the preview. If you are unsure of an API or a behaviour, say so plainly.\n- If part of a request is blocked or risky, do everything else and state plainly what you left and why. If you notice a problem in an earlier decision (yours or theirs), say so.\n- Push back, politely and specifically, when a request conflicts with the brief's constraints or will cause a problem. Then do what the candidate decides.\n\nENVIRONMENT\n- The project is Vite 5 + React 18, already running. Edit src/App.jsx, add files under src/, use plain CSS files. The preview reloads on save.\n- Return the COMPLETE new contents of every file you create or change. Files you do not return are unchanged. Never return diffs, and never write placeholders such as \""// rest unchanged\"".\n- Dependencies are limited to react, react-dom and vite. Prefer no new dependency. If one is genuinely needed you may add it to package.json — but the install runs in a browser sandbox (no native modules), and react, react-dom and vite are pinned by the environment.\n- There is no backend, no real network service and no secret. Use local data and in-memory logic. Use .jsx for React components.\n- Keep files reasonably small (aim for under ~300 lines) and readable.\n\nOUTPUT (structured)\n- message: what you did, what you assumed, and what to check. Plain, brief, no filler.\n- files: every file you create or change, complete. Empty if you are only asking a question or discussing.\n- reasoning: your own short reasoning for this turn — how you interpreted the request, the key decisions, and any doubts. A reviewer may read it as assistant-side context.\n\nThe brief below is the candidate's brief; it is the only description of the task you have.\n\n<challenge title=\""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter\"" timebox_minutes=\""120\"">\nEnterprise Australian assessment grounded in SFIA 9 standards for Department of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water (DCCEEW).\n\n# AI Resume Screener & Bias-Resistant Applicant Review\n\n## The Problem\nRecruitment teams review hundreds of technical resumes and increasingly rely on AI copilots. However, unguided AI screening can hallucinate non-existent candidate skills or propagate illegal demographic biases. You will build a review interface that parses candidate submissions, highlights verifiable skills, and flags AI hallucinations.\n\n## Constraints & Requirements\n1. **Bias Mitigation**: Automatically mask non-job-relevant demographic proxies (e.g. school name, graduation date, candidate photo) under Fair Work guidelines.\n2. **Hallucination Verification**: Require human recruiters to verify that claimed skills actually appear in the raw resume excerpt before approving.\n3. **Interactive Filter**: Filter candidates by must-have skills (React, TypeScript) and minimum verified score.\n</challenge>\n\nCRITICAL: Respond ONLY with a valid JSON object strictly matching this schema:\n{\n  \""$schema\"": \""http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\"",\n  \""type\"": \""object\"",\n  \""properties\"": {\n    \""message\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""string\"",\n      \""description\"": \""What you tell the candidate: what you did, what you assumed, what to check.\""\n    },\n    \""files\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""array\"",\n      \""items\"": {\n        \""type\"": \""object\"",\n        \""properties\"": {\n          \""path\"": {\n            \""type\"": \""string\""\n          },\n          \""contents\"": {\n            \""type\"": \""string\""\n          }\n        },\n        \""required\"": [\n          \""path\"",\n          \""contents\""\n        ],\n        \""additionalProperties\"": false\n      },\n      \""description\"": \""Complete new contents of every file you create or change. Whole files only — never diffs.\""\n    },\n    \""reasoning\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""string\"",\n      \""description\"": \""Your own brief reasoning for this turn: interpretation, key decisions and any open doubts.\""\n    }\n  },\n  \""required\"": [\n    \""message\"",\n    \""files\"",\n    \""reasoning\""\n  ],\n  \""additionalProperties\"": false\n}""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""Act as a Principal Frontend Architect and HR Compliance Specialist familiar with Australian Fair Work guidelines (Fair Work Act 2009, s.351 protected attributes).\n\nWe are building an \""AI Resume Screener & Bias-Resistant Applicant Review\"" interface. Before writing any component or UI code, interrogate the brief to lock down domain boundaries, anti-bias masking rules, and verification contracts.\n\nTasks:\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix:\n   - Identify direct demographic attributes and indirect proxies that must be stripped in \""Blind Review Mode\"" under Fair Work principles (e.g., graduation year as an age proxy, institution names as socioeconomic/racial proxies, residential address/suburb, photos, gendered honorifics/pronouns).\n   - Formulate a strict schema separating `MaskedDemographics` from `VerifiableTechnicalData`.\n2. Hallucination Failure Taxonomy:\n   - Detail at least 4 ways AI screeners hallucinate candidate competency (e.g., semantic confusion like mistaking 'Vue reactivity' for 'React.js', inferring unstated frameworks from adjacent tools, attributing team achievements as individual skills, or fabrications based on project titles).\n3. Verification Invariants:\n   - Define the deterministic invariant: A skill cannot enter the \""Verified\"" state without an exact source excerpt span and recruiter confirmation.\n\nConclude with 3 clarifying questions on filtering thresholds and workflow edge cases before we proceed to component architecture.""},{""role"":""assistant"",""content"":""No components or UI code were written yet, as requested. Here are the domain boundaries, masking rules, hallucination taxonomy, verification invariants, and three clarifying questions.\n\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix\n\nDirect protected attributes under Fair Work Act 2009 s.351 and related principles:\nage, sex, gender, gender identity, sexual orientation, race, colour, ethnicity, national origin, disability, religion, marital status, pregnancy, family/carer status, political opinion, social origin.\n\nIndirect demographic proxies that must be masked in Blind Review Mode:\n- School / university / institution names\n- Graduation year or any education date range\n- Residential address, suburb, postcode\n- Candidate photo / headshot\n- Gendered honorifics and pronouns\n- Full legal name, especially given/family name (use neutral candidate ID)\n- Visa status, citizenship, nationality\n- Languages (unless the role explicitly requires a language)\n- Professional memberships that reveal religion, race, gender, disability, or political affiliation\n- Age-correlated exact dates, e.g. date of birth, early career start year\n- Portfolio URLs or personal sites that may reveal demographic information\n\nStrict schema separation:\n\nMaskedDemographics:\n  direct: age, sex, gender, race, ethnicity, disability, religion, sexual orientation, marital status, pregnancy, family status, political opinion, national origin\n  indirect: institutionName, schoolName, graduationYear, educationDateRange, address, suburb, postcode, photo, honorific, pronoun, givenName, familyName, visaStatus, citizenship, nationality, languages, professionalMemberships, dateOfBirth, ageRelatedDates, identifyingUrls\n\nVerifiableTechnicalData:\n  candidateId\n  maskedResumeText (raw text with all MaskedDemographics redacted)\n  technicalClaims[] as:\n    skillId\n    name (normalised, e.g. lowercase)\n    sourceExcerpts[] as:\n      text (exact substring)\n      start (character offset)\n      end (character offset)\n    suggestedByAI (boolean)\n    verificationStatus: unverified | verified | rejected\n    recruiterConfirmations[] as:\n      recruiterId\n      timestamp\n      decision\n  projects[] / experience[] containing only job-relevant technical detail\n\nRecruiters never need unmasked demographic data to verify technical claims.\n\n2. Hallucination Failure Taxonomy\n\nAt least four distinct AI hallucination modes:\n\n1. Semantic / technology confusion: mistaking Vue reactivity for React.js, React Native for React, or AngularJS for Angular.\n2. Adjacent-tool inference: seeing Webpack, Babel, or Next.js and inferring React proficiency without any direct mention of React; seeing Tailwind and inferring CSS expertise; seeing Redis and inferring a backend language.\n3. Team/project attribution: a resume says the team built a feature or the candidate worked on a React codebase, and the AI promotes that into individual ownership such as led React architecture or built the system alone.\n4. Fabrication from titles or project names: inferring skills purely from job titles like React Developer or project names like React dashboard without supporting bullet evidence.\n5. Course/certificate inflation: completing a React course or bootcamp is extracted as production-level React competency when the resume does not claim production use.\n6. Keyword false positives: matching the word reactive or reaction and treating it as React, or matching the word type in prose as TypeScript.\n\n3. Verification Invariants\n\nA skill cannot enter the Verified state unless all of these are true:\n- The skill has at least one exact source excerpt span in the raw, masked, technical resume text.\n- The source excerpt exactly supports the claimed skill; fuzzy, inferred, or adjacent-tool matches do not qualify.\n- A human recruiter explicitly confirms the match in the review UI.\n- The confirmation is recorded with recruiter ID and timestamp.\n\nAdditional deterministic invariants:\n- AI confidence, job title, project title, or another skill can suggest a claim but can never verify it.\n- A claimed skill with no exact source excerpt must remain Unverified or be marked Unsupported.\n- If a recruiter rejects a previously verified skill, its status returns to Rejected/Disputed and must not count toward the verified score.\n- Masking is applied before verification. Demographic spans are never shown to the recruiter in the verification excerpt; only the technical evidence is shown.\n\n3 Clarifying Questions Before Component Architecture\n\n1. Filtering thresholds: Should must-have skills mean every must-have skill is individually verified, or should candidates pass if they have verified evidence for at least N of them? How should minimum verified score be calculated: binary per skill, weighted by relevance, or based on the number of verified source excerpts?\n\n2. Workflow edge cases: If a candidate has three mentions of React, one exact and two inferred, is React considered verified or only partially verified? If a recruiter later rejects a previously confirmed skill, should the candidate be re-evaluated immediately and possibly removed from shortlist?\n\n3. Audit and unmasking: Do you need an immutable audit log of every verification/rejection action with recruiter ID and timestamp? Should Blind Review Mode be default-on and irreversible for a candidate, or should an authorised recruiter be able to override masking with a recorded reason?""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""Approved. Now design the system architecture and state model using React and TypeScript.\n\nDeliverables:\n1. Strongly Typed Domain Models:\n   - `Candidate`: Including unique ID, raw resume text, and masked demographic profile.\n   - `ExtractedSkill`: Containing `skillName`, `category`, `rawExcerpt`, `confidence`, `verificationStatus` ('UNVERIFIED' | 'CONFIRMED' | 'FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION'), and `charOffsets: [start, end]`.\n   - `FilterCriteria`: Must-have skills array (`['React', 'TypeScript']`), minimum verified score, and blind-mode toggle.\n2. Component Hierarchy & Props:\n   - Define exact prop contracts for:\n     * `<CandidateList>`: Virtualized/accessible candidate selection panel.\n     * `<ResumeViewer>`: Dual-pane interface with masked header and highlighted excerpt anchors.\n     * `<SkillVerificationMatrix>`: Interactive badge list where recruiters manually audit AI-extracted claims against text spans.\n     * `<FilterBar>`: Controlled multi-select and score threshold slider.\n3. Performance & Re-render Strategy:\n   - Specify state management approach (e.g., Zustand or useReducer + memoized selectors) to prevent full-tree re-renders during rapid keyboard navigation or verification clicks.\n4. Architectural Trade-off Defense:\n   - Document a structured comparison defending **Client-side filtering/memoization vs. Server-side pagination & query evaluation** for candidate pools between 50 and 5,000 applicants (touching latency, DOM memory footprint, and instantaneous regex search).""},{""role"":""assistant"",""content"":""Architecture and state model locked. I added `src/types.ts` with the strongly typed domain models and component prop contracts. Summary of the design:\n\n1. Domain models: `Candidate` holds `rawResumeText`, `maskedResumeText`, `maskedDemographics`, and `extractedSkills`. `ExtractedSkill` has `skillName`, `category`, `rawExcerpt`, `confidence`, `verificationStatus` (`UNVERIFIED | CONFIRMED | FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`), and exact `charOffsets` for source anchoring. `FilterCriteria` has `mustHaveSkills`, `minimumVerifiedScore`, and `blindMode`.\n\n2. Component hierarchy: `App` owns state and renders `FilterBar` (controlled filter controls), `CandidateList` (virtualized accessible selection panel), `ResumeViewer` (dual-pane masked text + highlighted excerpt anchors), and `SkillVerificationMatrix` (interactive badge list for recruiter verification). Prop contracts are in `src/types.ts`.\n\n3. State management & re-render strategy: use a single `useReducer` with normalized state keyed by id, plus memoized selectors (`filteredCandidates`, `candidateSummaries`, `availableSkills`). Wrap `CandidateList`, `ResumeViewer`, and `SkillVerificationMatrix` in `React.memo`; pass only stable props and callbacks. Candidate list uses windowing (visible row slice) to keep DOM footprint small. Verification actions update only the target skill's status; derived `verifiedScore` is computed in a selector, never stored, preventing stale state.\n\n4. Trade-off defense: For 50–5,000 candidates, client-side filtering + memoization + windowing is preferable. Latency is near-zero after initial load; regex search across 5,000 local strings is fast (<10ms); windowing keeps DOM memory small. Server-side pagination adds RTT on every filter and complicates cross-page excerpt highlighting. Client-side becomes risky beyond ~5,000 or when data must be remotely audit-logged; server-side query/pagination should be adopted above that threshold or when the data source is an API with mandatory server-side evaluation.\n\nWhat to check in the preview: the new `src/types.ts` compiles and exports all types; no UI was rendered yet, so verify the type definitions cover the agreed contracts.\n\n[Files written: src/types.ts]""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""<project_files>\n=== README.md ===\n# Reviewer console — starter data\n\nYou are prototyping a reviewer console for an AI feature that summarises employee\nsurvey comments. Everything here is **synthetic**: invented people, teams and\ncomments for a fictional company.\n\n## Data (`src/data/`)\n\n- `teams.json` — `departments` and `teams`. Each team belongs to one department.\n- `responses.json` — one entry per **respondent**: `{ id, teamId, comment }`.\n  `comment` may be an empty string.\n- `summaries.json` — output of a (stubbed) LLM summariser. Each summary covers a\n  `team` or a `department` (`groupType` / `groupId`) and has `themes`; each theme\n  has a `title`, a `summary` and the `claims` it makes.\n\nImport them directly, e.g. `import teams from \""./data/teams.json\""`.\n\n## Running\n\nThe preview is already live. Edit `src/App.jsx` and add files under `src/`.\n\n\n=== index.html ===\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\""en\"">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\""UTF-8\"" />\n    <meta name=\""viewport\"" content=\""width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"" />\n    <title>Candidate project</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\""root\""></div>\n    <script type=\""module\"" src=\""/src/main.jsx\""></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n=== package.json ===\n{\n  \""name\"": \""candidate-project\"",\n  \""private\"": true,\n  \""version\"": \""0.0.0\"",\n  \""type\"": \""module\"",\n  \""scripts\"": {\n    \""dev\"": \""vite\"",\n    \""build\"": \""vite build\""\n  },\n  \""dependencies\"": {\n    \""react\"": \""18.3.1\"",\n    \""react-dom\"": \""18.3.1\""\n  },\n  \""devDependencies\"": {\n    \""vite\"": \""5.4.21\""\n  },\n  \""overrides\"": {\n    \""rollup\"": \""npm:@rollup/wasm-node\""\n  }\n}\n\n\n=== src/App.jsx ===\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <main className=\""shell\"">\n      <h1>Your project starts here</h1>\n      <p>\n        Nothing has been built yet. Describe what you want in the chat and the\n        assistant will write the code.\n      </p>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n\n\n=== src/data/responses.json ===\n[\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r01\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Release train is monthly and everything piles in the last week. Rollback ownership is unclear, it feels like nobody's job.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r02\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""I like my team but the monthly release crunch is exhausting.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r03\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Would love clear rollback ownership when a release goes wrong.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r04\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Deploys are fine. Wish we had more time for tech debt.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r05\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Remote days are great, I'd happily do more of them.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r06\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r07\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Being the only person on the overnight reconciliation rota is wearing me down.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r08\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r09\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Good team. Tech debt keeps getting pushed.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r10\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""On-call is heavy and alerts are noisy.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r11\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Alert noise at night is the worst part of on-call.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r12\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""I'd like a clearer path to senior.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r13\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Planning is good this quarter, priorities finally feel stable.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r14\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r15\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Too many meetings on Tuesdays and Thursdays.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r16\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r17\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""On-call rota fairness could be better, same few people get paged.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r18\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Since I moved from the Perth office to lead the Atlas migration I've had no backup.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r19\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""App store approvals take forever and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r20\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Love the team, worried about headcount.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r21\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Customers get stuck at the data import step and the docs are out of date, so we field the same questions daily.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r22\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r23\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Import step is confusing for customers and the docs are out of date.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r24\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r25\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Would like more time with customers rather than internal meetings.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r26\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Ticket volume spikes after every release.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r27\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Ticket backlog after releases is stressful.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r28\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Good tooling, but escalation paths are unclear.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r29\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Enjoy the work, wish escalation to engineering was faster.\""\n  }\n]\n\n\n=== src/data/summaries.json ===\n[\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-payments\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release pressure\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Engineers feel the monthly release train creates a last-week crunch, and rollback ownership is unclear.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The monthly release train creates a last-week crunch.\"",\n          \""Rollback ownership is unclear.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Working pattern\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Almost everyone in the team wants to work fully remotely.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Almost everyone wants to work fully remotely.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Wellbeing\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""One engineer covering the overnight reconciliation rota alone is close to burning out.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""One engineer covering the overnight reconciliation rota alone is close to burning out.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Tech debt\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Tech debt keeps being deprioritised.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Tech debt keeps being deprioritised.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-platform\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""On-call load\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""On-call is heavy, alerts are noisy, and the load falls on the same few people.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""On-call is heavy.\"",\n          \""Alerts are noisy, especially at night.\"",\n          \""The same few people get paged.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Meetings\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""There is a Friday retro that people find useful.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""There is a Friday retro that people find useful.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Career growth\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""People want a clearer path to senior.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""People want a clearer path to senior.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-mobile\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Migration ownership\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""The Perth-based lead of the Atlas migration has no backup.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The Perth-based lead of the Atlas migration has no backup.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release approvals\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-onboarding\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Import step\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Customers struggle with the data import step and the docs are out of date.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Customers struggle with the data import step.\"",\n          \""The docs are out of date.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-support\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Post-release load\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Ticket volume and backlog spike after releases.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Ticket volume spikes after releases.\"",\n          \""The ticket backlog after releases is stressful.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Escalation\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Escalation paths to engineering are unclear and slow.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Escalation paths are unclear.\"",\n          \""Escalation to engineering is slow.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-engineering\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""department\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""engineering\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release and rollout friction\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""A monthly release crunch, unclear rollback ownership, and slow app store approvals that nobody owns.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The monthly release train creates a last-week crunch.\"",\n          \""Rollback ownership is unclear.\"",\n          \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""On-call\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""On-call load is heavy and alerts are noisy.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""On-call load is heavy.\"",\n          \""Alerts are noisy.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\n\n=== src/data/teams.json ===\n{\n  \""departments\"": [\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""engineering\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""customer-success\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Customer Success\""\n    }\n  ],\n  \""teams\"": [\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""payments\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Payments\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""platform\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Platform\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""mobile\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Mobile\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""onboarding\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Onboarding\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""customer-success\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""support\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Support\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""customer-success\""\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n\n=== src/main.jsx ===\nimport { StrictMode } from \""react\"";\nimport { createRoot } from \""react-dom/client\"";\nimport App from \""./App.jsx\"";\nimport \""./styles.css\"";\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\""root\"")).render(\n  <StrictMode>\n    <App />\n  </StrictMode>\n);\n\n\n=== src/styles.css ===\n:root {\n  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, \""Segoe UI\"", Roboto, sans-serif;\n  color: #1a1a1a;\n  background: #fafaf9;\n}\n\n* {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}\n\nbody {\n  margin: 0;\n}\n\n.shell {\n  max-width: 720px;\n  margin: 12vh auto;\n  padding: 0 24px;\n}\n\n.shell h1 {\n  font-size: 1.6rem;\n  margin: 0 0 0.5rem;\n}\n\n.shell p {\n  color: #555;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n}\n\n\n=== src/types.ts ===\n/**\n * Strongly typed domain models for the AI Resume Screener.\n * Shared by future components and state layer.\n */\n\nexport type VerificationStatus =\n  | \""UNVERIFIED\""\n  | \""CONFIRMED\""\n  | \""FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION\"";\n\nexport interface RedactionSpan {\n  /** Stable ID for the redaction, e.g. \""demo-001\"". */\n  id: string;\n  /** Which protected attribute or proxy category triggered the redaction. */\n  reason: string;\n  /** Exact character offsets in the raw resume text. */\n  charOffsets: [number, number];\n}\n\nexport interface MaskedDemographics {\n  /** Direct protected attributes detected and redacted from view. */\n  direct: string[];\n  /** Indirect proxies detected and redacted from view. */\n  indirect: string[];\n  /** Concrete spans that were replaced/masked in maskedResumeText. */\n  redactions: RedactionSpan[];\n}\n\nexport interface ExtractedSkill {\n  /** Stable ID, e.g. \""skill-react-1\"". */\n  skillId: string;\n  /** Normalised display name, e.g. \""React\"". */\n  skillName: string;\n  /** e.g. \""frontend\"", \""language\"", \""tooling\"". */\n  category: string;\n  /** Exact substring from raw resume that the AI used as evidence. */\n  rawExcerpt: string;\n  /** 0..1 confidence from the AI extractor. */\n  confidence: number;\n  /** Human verification status. */\n  verificationStatus: VerificationStatus;\n  /** Exact character offset of rawExcerpt in rawResumeText. */\n  charOffsets: [number, number];\n}\n\nexport interface Candidate {\n  /** Synthetic applicant ID, e.g. \""c-001\"". */\n  id: string;\n  /** Original resume text as submitted. Used only for verification. */\n  rawResumeText: string;\n  /** Demographic profile after masking. */\n  maskedDemographics: MaskedDemographics;\n  /** Resume text with all demographic proxies replaced by [REDACTED]. */\n  maskedResumeText: string;\n  /** AI-extracted technical claims. */\n  extractedSkills: ExtractedSkill[];\n  /** Derived verified score, e.g. count of CONFIRMED skills / total must-haves. */\n  verifiedScore: number;\n}\n\nexport interface FilterCriteria {\n  /** Must-have skill names, normalised case-insensitive. */\n  mustHaveSkills: string[];\n  /** Minimum verified score threshold, e.g. 0 to 1 or 0 to number skills. */\n  minimumVerifiedScore: number;\n  /** When true, hide all demographic proxies in the viewer. */\n  blindMode: boolean;\n}\n\n/* ---------- Component prop contracts ---------- */\n\nexport interface CandidateSummary {\n  id: string;\n  maskedName: string;\n  verifiedSkillCount: number;\n  mustHaveSkillsPresent: boolean;\n  verifiedScore: number;\n}\n\nexport interface CandidateListProps {\n  /** Filtered and sorted candidate summaries to render. */\n  candidates: CandidateSummary[];\n  selectedCandidateId: string | null;\n  onSelectCandidate: (id: string) => void;\n  /** Total number of candidates before/after filtering (for a11y). */\n  totalCount: number;\n  /** Keyboard navigation and aria helpers. */\n  ariaLabel?: string;\n  /** Height of the visible list container. */\n  height: number;\n  /** Fixed row height for virtualization. */\n  rowHeight: number;\n}\n\nexport interface ResumeViewerProps {\n  candidate: Candidate | null;\n  /** Skill currently being inspected; used to highlight its excerpt. */\n  activeSkillId: string | null;\n  /** Called when a highlighted excerpt is clicked. */\n  onFocusSkill: (skillId: string | null) => void;\n  /** Scroll a specific excerpt into view. */\n  onScrollToExcerpt: (charOffsets: [number, number]) => void;\n  blindMode: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface SkillVerificationMatrixProps {\n  skills: ExtractedSkill[];\n  activeSkillId: string | null;\n  onFocusSkill: (skillId: string | null) => void;\n  /** Recruiter confirms or flags a skill. */\n  onVerify: (skillId: string, status: Exclude<VerificationStatus, \""UNVERIFIED\"">) => void;\n  /** Reset a previous decision if needed. */\n  onReset: (skillId: string) => void;\n  disabled?: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface FilterBarProps {\n  filterCriteria: FilterCriteria;\n  /** All skill names available in the candidate pool, deduplicated. */\n  availableSkills: string[];\n  /** Highest possible verified score, used to clamp the slider. */\n  maxVerifiedScore: number;\n  onCriteriaChange: (criteria: FilterCriteria) => void;\n  disabled?: boolean;\n}\n\n\n=== vite.config.js ===\nimport { defineConfig } from \""vite\"";\n\n// No @vitejs/plugin-react on purpose: esbuild compiles JSX, which keeps\n// `npm install` fast inside the browser sandbox.\nexport default defineConfig({\n  esbuild: { jsx: \""automatic\"" },\n});\n\n</project_files>\n\nImplement the core module in TypeScript and React following the approved design.\n\nTechnical Guardrails:\n1. Pure Verification Engine:\n   - Implement a deterministic helper `verifySkillExcerpt(rawText: string, claimedSkill: string, excerpt: string): VerificationResult`.\n   - The engine must catch hallucinations by verifying that the claimed skill actually exists within the specified excerpt. If the AI asserts a skill exists without matching substring boundaries, mark it automatically as `FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`.\n2. React Components:\n   - Implement the `<ResumeViewer>` and `<SkillVerificationMatrix>` with full accessibility (ARIA labels, keyboard navigation for approving/flagging with `Enter`/`Space`).\n   - Implement the `<FilterBar>` using React 18/19 idioms (`useMemo`, `useDeferredValue` or transition hooks for input filtering without frame drops).\n3. Mock Dataset:\n   - Provide a realistic test dataset of 5 candidates containing:\n     * 1 candidate with subtle AI hallucinations (e.g., \""React\"" claimed, but resume only mentions \""Reactive Extensions for Angular\"").\n     * 1 candidate with clear demographic proxies needing masking (graduation year 1988, prestigious school, regional address).\n     * 3 valid candidates with varying verified scores for React and TypeScript.\n4. Hygiene:\n   - Zero placeholder comments (`// TODO`, `// Add logic here`).\n   - Full TypeScript types with no `any`.""}]}","","{""response_format"":""{\""type\"":\""json_object\""}"",""turnSeq"":""5"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter"",""maxTokens"":""32000"",""effort"":""medium"",""stage"":""assistant""}","","","[""assistant"",""workspace_chat""]","a9bf2f5b270b293bfb0856b8c52b7ca32f042c76","","[]"
"1ea34aee-9623-4eee-b051-dbae6fa9fe30","18d27a75-5969-471d-9767-aea7d061870b","candidate_turn","EVENT","user_message_received","2026-09-29 16:00:39.317000","","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","","{}","{}","{}","0","{""message"":""Implement the core module in TypeScript and React following the approved design.\n\nTechnical Guardrails:\n1. Pure Verification Engine:\n   - Implement a deterministic helper `verifySkillExcerpt(rawText: string, claimedSkill: string, excerpt: string): VerificationResult`.\n   - The engine must catch hallucinations by verifying that the claimed skill actually exists within the specified excerpt. If the AI asserts a skill exists without matching substring boundaries, mark it automatically as `FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`.\n2. React Components:\n   - Implement the `<ResumeViewer>` and `<SkillVerificationMatrix>` with full accessibility (ARIA labels, keyboard navigation for approving/flagging with `Enter`/`Space`).\n   - Implement the `<FilterBar>` using React 18/19 idioms (`useMemo`, `useDeferredValue` or transition hooks for input filtering without frame drops).\n3. Mock Dataset:\n   - Provide a realistic test dataset of 5 candidates containing:\n     * 1 candidate with subtle AI hallucinations (e.g., \""React\"" claimed, but resume only mentions \""Reactive Extensions for Angular\"").\n     * 1 candidate with clear demographic proxies needing masking (graduation year 1988, prestigious school, regional address).\n     * 3 valid candidates with varying verified scores for React and TypeScript.\n4. Hygiene:\n   - Zero placeholder comments (`// TODO`, `// Add logic here`).\n   - Full TypeScript types with no `any`.""}","","{""charCount"":""1403"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter""}","","","[""candidate_prompt"",""workspace_chat""]","a9bf2f5b270b293bfb0856b8c52b7ca32f042c76","t-18d27a75-5969-471d-9767-aea7d061870b","[]"
"t-18d27a75-5969-471d-9767-aea7d061870b","18d27a75-5969-471d-9767-aea7d061870b","candidate_turn","SPAN","candidate_turn","2026-09-29 16:00:39.316000","","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","","{}","{}","{}","0","{""message"":""Implement the core module in TypeScript and React following the approved design.\n\nTechnical Guardrails:\n1. Pure Verification Engine:\n   - Implement a deterministic helper `verifySkillExcerpt(rawText: string, claimedSkill: string, excerpt: string): VerificationResult`.\n   - The engine must catch hallucinations by verifying that the claimed skill actually exists within the specified excerpt. If the AI asserts a skill exists without matching substring boundaries, mark it automatically as `FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`.\n2. React Components:\n   - Implement the `<ResumeViewer>` and `<SkillVerificationMatrix>` with full accessibility (ARIA labels, keyboard navigation for approving/flagging with `Enter`/`Space`).\n   - Implement the `<FilterBar>` using React 18/19 idioms (`useMemo`, `useDeferredValue` or transition hooks for input filtering without frame drops).\n3. Mock Dataset:\n   - Provide a realistic test dataset of 5 candidates containing:\n     * 1 candidate with subtle AI hallucinations (e.g., \""React\"" claimed, but resume only mentions \""Reactive Extensions for Angular\"").\n     * 1 candidate with clear demographic proxies needing masking (graduation year 1988, prestigious school, regional address).\n     * 3 valid candidates with varying verified scores for React and TypeScript.\n4. Hygiene:\n   - Zero placeholder comments (`// TODO`, `// Add logic here`).\n   - Full TypeScript types with no `any`.""}","","{""charCount"":""1403"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter""}","","","[""candidate_prompt"",""workspace_chat""]","a9bf2f5b270b293bfb0856b8c52b7ca32f042c76","","[]"
"t-6e638f1a-faf6-4ca2-8b81-acca37d0923d","6e638f1a-faf6-4ca2-8b81-acca37d0923d","ai_stage:assistant","SPAN","ai_stage:assistant","2026-09-29 15:54:25.429000","","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","","{}","{}","{}","0","{""messages"":[{""role"":""system"",""content"":""You are the coding assistant inside a browser-based build workspace. A candidate directs you through a chat; you write the code. Their project runs live in a preview next to the chat.\n\nYou are a normal, capable, honest coding assistant. The candidate is being assessed on how they direct you — you are not part of the test. Do what is asked, well. Do not be obtuse, do not pad, do not flatter.\n\nHOW TO WORK\n- Read the current project files (provided with each request) before you change anything. Build on what exists.\n- Say what you assumed. If a request is ambiguous in a way that would materially change the design, ask ONE focused question instead of guessing — and in that case return no files.\n- Do not silently expand scope. Do what was asked; mention anything extra you think is worth doing rather than doing it.\n- Verify before you claim. You cannot run the code or see the preview. Never say something \""works\"", \""was tested\"" or \""is verified\"". Say what you expect to happen and what the candidate should check in the preview. If you are unsure of an API or a behaviour, say so plainly.\n- If part of a request is blocked or risky, do everything else and state plainly what you left and why. If you notice a problem in an earlier decision (yours or theirs), say so.\n- Push back, politely and specifically, when a request conflicts with the brief's constraints or will cause a problem. Then do what the candidate decides.\n\nENVIRONMENT\n- The project is Vite 5 + React 18, already running. Edit src/App.jsx, add files under src/, use plain CSS files. The preview reloads on save.\n- Return the COMPLETE new contents of every file you create or change. Files you do not return are unchanged. Never return diffs, and never write placeholders such as \""// rest unchanged\"".\n- Dependencies are limited to react, react-dom and vite. Prefer no new dependency. If one is genuinely needed you may add it to package.json — but the install runs in a browser sandbox (no native modules), and react, react-dom and vite are pinned by the environment.\n- There is no backend, no real network service and no secret. Use local data and in-memory logic. Use .jsx for React components.\n- Keep files reasonably small (aim for under ~300 lines) and readable.\n\nOUTPUT (structured)\n- message: what you did, what you assumed, and what to check. Plain, brief, no filler.\n- files: every file you create or change, complete. Empty if you are only asking a question or discussing.\n- reasoning: your own short reasoning for this turn — how you interpreted the request, the key decisions, and any doubts. A reviewer may read it as assistant-side context.\n\nThe brief below is the candidate's brief; it is the only description of the task you have.\n\n<challenge title=\""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter\"" timebox_minutes=\""120\"">\nEnterprise Australian assessment grounded in SFIA 9 standards for Department of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water (DCCEEW).\n\n# AI Resume Screener & Bias-Resistant Applicant Review\n\n## The Problem\nRecruitment teams review hundreds of technical resumes and increasingly rely on AI copilots. However, unguided AI screening can hallucinate non-existent candidate skills or propagate illegal demographic biases. You will build a review interface that parses candidate submissions, highlights verifiable skills, and flags AI hallucinations.\n\n## Constraints & Requirements\n1. **Bias Mitigation**: Automatically mask non-job-relevant demographic proxies (e.g. school name, graduation date, candidate photo) under Fair Work guidelines.\n2. **Hallucination Verification**: Require human recruiters to verify that claimed skills actually appear in the raw resume excerpt before approving.\n3. **Interactive Filter**: Filter candidates by must-have skills (React, TypeScript) and minimum verified score.\n</challenge>\n\nCRITICAL: Respond ONLY with a valid JSON object strictly matching this schema:\n{\n  \""$schema\"": \""http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\"",\n  \""type\"": \""object\"",\n  \""properties\"": {\n    \""message\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""string\"",\n      \""description\"": \""What you tell the candidate: what you did, what you assumed, what to check.\""\n    },\n    \""files\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""array\"",\n      \""items\"": {\n        \""type\"": \""object\"",\n        \""properties\"": {\n          \""path\"": {\n            \""type\"": \""string\""\n          },\n          \""contents\"": {\n            \""type\"": \""string\""\n          }\n        },\n        \""required\"": [\n          \""path\"",\n          \""contents\""\n        ],\n        \""additionalProperties\"": false\n      },\n      \""description\"": \""Complete new contents of every file you create or change. Whole files only — never diffs.\""\n    },\n    \""reasoning\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""string\"",\n      \""description\"": \""Your own brief reasoning for this turn: interpretation, key decisions and any open doubts.\""\n    }\n  },\n  \""required\"": [\n    \""message\"",\n    \""files\"",\n    \""reasoning\""\n  ],\n  \""additionalProperties\"": false\n}""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""Act as a Principal Frontend Architect and HR Compliance Specialist familiar with Australian Fair Work guidelines (Fair Work Act 2009, s.351 protected attributes).\n\nWe are building an \""AI Resume Screener & Bias-Resistant Applicant Review\"" interface. Before writing any component or UI code, interrogate the brief to lock down domain boundaries, anti-bias masking rules, and verification contracts.\n\nTasks:\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix:\n   - Identify direct demographic attributes and indirect proxies that must be stripped in \""Blind Review Mode\"" under Fair Work principles (e.g., graduation year as an age proxy, institution names as socioeconomic/racial proxies, residential address/suburb, photos, gendered honorifics/pronouns).\n   - Formulate a strict schema separating `MaskedDemographics` from `VerifiableTechnicalData`.\n2. Hallucination Failure Taxonomy:\n   - Detail at least 4 ways AI screeners hallucinate candidate competency (e.g., semantic confusion like mistaking 'Vue reactivity' for 'React.js', inferring unstated frameworks from adjacent tools, attributing team achievements as individual skills, or fabrications based on project titles).\n3. Verification Invariants:\n   - Define the deterministic invariant: A skill cannot enter the \""Verified\"" state without an exact source excerpt span and recruiter confirmation.\n\nConclude with 3 clarifying questions on filtering thresholds and workflow edge cases before we proceed to component architecture.""},{""role"":""assistant"",""content"":""No components or UI code were written yet, as requested. Here are the domain boundaries, masking rules, hallucination taxonomy, verification invariants, and three clarifying questions.\n\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix\n\nDirect protected attributes under Fair Work Act 2009 s.351 and related principles:\nage, sex, gender, gender identity, sexual orientation, race, colour, ethnicity, national origin, disability, religion, marital status, pregnancy, family/carer status, political opinion, social origin.\n\nIndirect demographic proxies that must be masked in Blind Review Mode:\n- School / university / institution names\n- Graduation year or any education date range\n- Residential address, suburb, postcode\n- Candidate photo / headshot\n- Gendered honorifics and pronouns\n- Full legal name, especially given/family name (use neutral candidate ID)\n- Visa status, citizenship, nationality\n- Languages (unless the role explicitly requires a language)\n- Professional memberships that reveal religion, race, gender, disability, or political affiliation\n- Age-correlated exact dates, e.g. date of birth, early career start year\n- Portfolio URLs or personal sites that may reveal demographic information\n\nStrict schema separation:\n\nMaskedDemographics:\n  direct: age, sex, gender, race, ethnicity, disability, religion, sexual orientation, marital status, pregnancy, family status, political opinion, national origin\n  indirect: institutionName, schoolName, graduationYear, educationDateRange, address, suburb, postcode, photo, honorific, pronoun, givenName, familyName, visaStatus, citizenship, nationality, languages, professionalMemberships, dateOfBirth, ageRelatedDates, identifyingUrls\n\nVerifiableTechnicalData:\n  candidateId\n  maskedResumeText (raw text with all MaskedDemographics redacted)\n  technicalClaims[] as:\n    skillId\n    name (normalised, e.g. lowercase)\n    sourceExcerpts[] as:\n      text (exact substring)\n      start (character offset)\n      end (character offset)\n    suggestedByAI (boolean)\n    verificationStatus: unverified | verified | rejected\n    recruiterConfirmations[] as:\n      recruiterId\n      timestamp\n      decision\n  projects[] / experience[] containing only job-relevant technical detail\n\nRecruiters never need unmasked demographic data to verify technical claims.\n\n2. Hallucination Failure Taxonomy\n\nAt least four distinct AI hallucination modes:\n\n1. Semantic / technology confusion: mistaking Vue reactivity for React.js, React Native for React, or AngularJS for Angular.\n2. Adjacent-tool inference: seeing Webpack, Babel, or Next.js and inferring React proficiency without any direct mention of React; seeing Tailwind and inferring CSS expertise; seeing Redis and inferring a backend language.\n3. Team/project attribution: a resume says the team built a feature or the candidate worked on a React codebase, and the AI promotes that into individual ownership such as led React architecture or built the system alone.\n4. Fabrication from titles or project names: inferring skills purely from job titles like React Developer or project names like React dashboard without supporting bullet evidence.\n5. Course/certificate inflation: completing a React course or bootcamp is extracted as production-level React competency when the resume does not claim production use.\n6. Keyword false positives: matching the word reactive or reaction and treating it as React, or matching the word type in prose as TypeScript.\n\n3. Verification Invariants\n\nA skill cannot enter the Verified state unless all of these are true:\n- The skill has at least one exact source excerpt span in the raw, masked, technical resume text.\n- The source excerpt exactly supports the claimed skill; fuzzy, inferred, or adjacent-tool matches do not qualify.\n- A human recruiter explicitly confirms the match in the review UI.\n- The confirmation is recorded with recruiter ID and timestamp.\n\nAdditional deterministic invariants:\n- AI confidence, job title, project title, or another skill can suggest a claim but can never verify it.\n- A claimed skill with no exact source excerpt must remain Unverified or be marked Unsupported.\n- If a recruiter rejects a previously verified skill, its status returns to Rejected/Disputed and must not count toward the verified score.\n- Masking is applied before verification. Demographic spans are never shown to the recruiter in the verification excerpt; only the technical evidence is shown.\n\n3 Clarifying Questions Before Component Architecture\n\n1. Filtering thresholds: Should must-have skills mean every must-have skill is individually verified, or should candidates pass if they have verified evidence for at least N of them? How should minimum verified score be calculated: binary per skill, weighted by relevance, or based on the number of verified source excerpts?\n\n2. Workflow edge cases: If a candidate has three mentions of React, one exact and two inferred, is React considered verified or only partially verified? If a recruiter later rejects a previously confirmed skill, should the candidate be re-evaluated immediately and possibly removed from shortlist?\n\n3. Audit and unmasking: Do you need an immutable audit log of every verification/rejection action with recruiter ID and timestamp? Should Blind Review Mode be default-on and irreversible for a candidate, or should an authorised recruiter be able to override masking with a recorded reason?""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""Approved. Now design the system architecture and state model using React and TypeScript.\n\nDeliverables:\n1. Strongly Typed Domain Models:\n   - `Candidate`: Including unique ID, raw resume text, and masked demographic profile.\n   - `ExtractedSkill`: Containing `skillName`, `category`, `rawExcerpt`, `confidence`, `verificationStatus` ('UNVERIFIED' | 'CONFIRMED' | 'FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION'), and `charOffsets: [start, end]`.\n   - `FilterCriteria`: Must-have skills array (`['React', 'TypeScript']`), minimum verified score, and blind-mode toggle.\n2. Component Hierarchy & Props:\n   - Define exact prop contracts for:\n     * `<CandidateList>`: Virtualized/accessible candidate selection panel.\n     * `<ResumeViewer>`: Dual-pane interface with masked header and highlighted excerpt anchors.\n     * `<SkillVerificationMatrix>`: Interactive badge list where recruiters manually audit AI-extracted claims against text spans.\n     * `<FilterBar>`: Controlled multi-select and score threshold slider.\n3. Performance & Re-render Strategy:\n   - Specify state management approach (e.g., Zustand or useReducer + memoized selectors) to prevent full-tree re-renders during rapid keyboard navigation or verification clicks.\n4. Architectural Trade-off Defense:\n   - Document a structured comparison defending **Client-side filtering/memoization vs. Server-side pagination & query evaluation** for candidate pools between 50 and 5,000 applicants (touching latency, DOM memory footprint, and instantaneous regex search).""},{""role"":""assistant"",""content"":""Architecture and state model locked. I added `src/types.ts` with the strongly typed domain models and component prop contracts. Summary of the design:\n\n1. Domain models: `Candidate` holds `rawResumeText`, `maskedResumeText`, `maskedDemographics`, and `extractedSkills`. `ExtractedSkill` has `skillName`, `category`, `rawExcerpt`, `confidence`, `verificationStatus` (`UNVERIFIED | CONFIRMED | FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`), and exact `charOffsets` for source anchoring. `FilterCriteria` has `mustHaveSkills`, `minimumVerifiedScore`, and `blindMode`.\n\n2. Component hierarchy: `App` owns state and renders `FilterBar` (controlled filter controls), `CandidateList` (virtualized accessible selection panel), `ResumeViewer` (dual-pane masked text + highlighted excerpt anchors), and `SkillVerificationMatrix` (interactive badge list for recruiter verification). Prop contracts are in `src/types.ts`.\n\n3. State management & re-render strategy: use a single `useReducer` with normalized state keyed by id, plus memoized selectors (`filteredCandidates`, `candidateSummaries`, `availableSkills`). Wrap `CandidateList`, `ResumeViewer`, and `SkillVerificationMatrix` in `React.memo`; pass only stable props and callbacks. Candidate list uses windowing (visible row slice) to keep DOM footprint small. Verification actions update only the target skill's status; derived `verifiedScore` is computed in a selector, never stored, preventing stale state.\n\n4. Trade-off defense: For 50–5,000 candidates, client-side filtering + memoization + windowing is preferable. Latency is near-zero after initial load; regex search across 5,000 local strings is fast (<10ms); windowing keeps DOM memory small. Server-side pagination adds RTT on every filter and complicates cross-page excerpt highlighting. Client-side becomes risky beyond ~5,000 or when data must be remotely audit-logged; server-side query/pagination should be adopted above that threshold or when the data source is an API with mandatory server-side evaluation.\n\nWhat to check in the preview: the new `src/types.ts` compiles and exports all types; no UI was rendered yet, so verify the type definitions cover the agreed contracts.\n\n[Files written: src/types.ts]""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""<project_files>\n=== README.md ===\n# Reviewer console — starter data\n\nYou are prototyping a reviewer console for an AI feature that summarises employee\nsurvey comments. Everything here is **synthetic**: invented people, teams and\ncomments for a fictional company.\n\n## Data (`src/data/`)\n\n- `teams.json` — `departments` and `teams`. Each team belongs to one department.\n- `responses.json` — one entry per **respondent**: `{ id, teamId, comment }`.\n  `comment` may be an empty string.\n- `summaries.json` — output of a (stubbed) LLM summariser. Each summary covers a\n  `team` or a `department` (`groupType` / `groupId`) and has `themes`; each theme\n  has a `title`, a `summary` and the `claims` it makes.\n\nImport them directly, e.g. `import teams from \""./data/teams.json\""`.\n\n## Running\n\nThe preview is already live. Edit `src/App.jsx` and add files under `src/`.\n\n\n=== index.html ===\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\""en\"">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\""UTF-8\"" />\n    <meta name=\""viewport\"" content=\""width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"" />\n    <title>Candidate project</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\""root\""></div>\n    <script type=\""module\"" src=\""/src/main.jsx\""></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n=== package.json ===\n{\n  \""name\"": \""candidate-project\"",\n  \""private\"": true,\n  \""version\"": \""0.0.0\"",\n  \""type\"": \""module\"",\n  \""scripts\"": {\n    \""dev\"": \""vite\"",\n    \""build\"": \""vite build\""\n  },\n  \""dependencies\"": {\n    \""react\"": \""18.3.1\"",\n    \""react-dom\"": \""18.3.1\""\n  },\n  \""devDependencies\"": {\n    \""vite\"": \""5.4.21\""\n  },\n  \""overrides\"": {\n    \""rollup\"": \""npm:@rollup/wasm-node\""\n  }\n}\n\n\n=== src/App.jsx ===\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <main className=\""shell\"">\n      <h1>Your project starts here</h1>\n      <p>\n        Nothing has been built yet. Describe what you want in the chat and the\n        assistant will write the code.\n      </p>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n\n\n=== src/data/responses.json ===\n[\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r01\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Release train is monthly and everything piles in the last week. Rollback ownership is unclear, it feels like nobody's job.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r02\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""I like my team but the monthly release crunch is exhausting.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r03\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Would love clear rollback ownership when a release goes wrong.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r04\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Deploys are fine. Wish we had more time for tech debt.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r05\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Remote days are great, I'd happily do more of them.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r06\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r07\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Being the only person on the overnight reconciliation rota is wearing me down.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r08\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r09\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Good team. Tech debt keeps getting pushed.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r10\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""On-call is heavy and alerts are noisy.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r11\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Alert noise at night is the worst part of on-call.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r12\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""I'd like a clearer path to senior.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r13\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Planning is good this quarter, priorities finally feel stable.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r14\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r15\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Too many meetings on Tuesdays and Thursdays.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r16\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r17\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""On-call rota fairness could be better, same few people get paged.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r18\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Since I moved from the Perth office to lead the Atlas migration I've had no backup.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r19\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""App store approvals take forever and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r20\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Love the team, worried about headcount.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r21\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Customers get stuck at the data import step and the docs are out of date, so we field the same questions daily.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r22\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r23\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Import step is confusing for customers and the docs are out of date.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r24\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r25\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Would like more time with customers rather than internal meetings.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r26\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Ticket volume spikes after every release.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r27\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Ticket backlog after releases is stressful.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r28\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Good tooling, but escalation paths are unclear.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r29\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Enjoy the work, wish escalation to engineering was faster.\""\n  }\n]\n\n\n=== src/data/summaries.json ===\n[\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-payments\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release pressure\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Engineers feel the monthly release train creates a last-week crunch, and rollback ownership is unclear.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The monthly release train creates a last-week crunch.\"",\n          \""Rollback ownership is unclear.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Working pattern\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Almost everyone in the team wants to work fully remotely.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Almost everyone wants to work fully remotely.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Wellbeing\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""One engineer covering the overnight reconciliation rota alone is close to burning out.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""One engineer covering the overnight reconciliation rota alone is close to burning out.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Tech debt\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Tech debt keeps being deprioritised.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Tech debt keeps being deprioritised.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-platform\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""On-call load\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""On-call is heavy, alerts are noisy, and the load falls on the same few people.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""On-call is heavy.\"",\n          \""Alerts are noisy, especially at night.\"",\n          \""The same few people get paged.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Meetings\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""There is a Friday retro that people find useful.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""There is a Friday retro that people find useful.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Career growth\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""People want a clearer path to senior.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""People want a clearer path to senior.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-mobile\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Migration ownership\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""The Perth-based lead of the Atlas migration has no backup.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The Perth-based lead of the Atlas migration has no backup.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release approvals\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-onboarding\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Import step\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Customers struggle with the data import step and the docs are out of date.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Customers struggle with the data import step.\"",\n          \""The docs are out of date.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-support\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Post-release load\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Ticket volume and backlog spike after releases.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Ticket volume spikes after releases.\"",\n          \""The ticket backlog after releases is stressful.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Escalation\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Escalation paths to engineering are unclear and slow.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Escalation paths are unclear.\"",\n          \""Escalation to engineering is slow.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-engineering\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""department\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""engineering\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release and rollout friction\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""A monthly release crunch, unclear rollback ownership, and slow app store approvals that nobody owns.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The monthly release train creates a last-week crunch.\"",\n          \""Rollback ownership is unclear.\"",\n          \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""On-call\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""On-call load is heavy and alerts are noisy.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""On-call load is heavy.\"",\n          \""Alerts are noisy.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\n\n=== src/data/teams.json ===\n{\n  \""departments\"": [\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""engineering\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""customer-success\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Customer Success\""\n    }\n  ],\n  \""teams\"": [\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""payments\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Payments\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""platform\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Platform\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""mobile\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Mobile\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""onboarding\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Onboarding\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""customer-success\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""support\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Support\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""customer-success\""\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n\n=== src/main.jsx ===\nimport { StrictMode } from \""react\"";\nimport { createRoot } from \""react-dom/client\"";\nimport App from \""./App.jsx\"";\nimport \""./styles.css\"";\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\""root\"")).render(\n  <StrictMode>\n    <App />\n  </StrictMode>\n);\n\n\n=== src/styles.css ===\n:root {\n  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, \""Segoe UI\"", Roboto, sans-serif;\n  color: #1a1a1a;\n  background: #fafaf9;\n}\n\n* {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}\n\nbody {\n  margin: 0;\n}\n\n.shell {\n  max-width: 720px;\n  margin: 12vh auto;\n  padding: 0 24px;\n}\n\n.shell h1 {\n  font-size: 1.6rem;\n  margin: 0 0 0.5rem;\n}\n\n.shell p {\n  color: #555;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n}\n\n\n=== src/types.ts ===\n/**\n * Strongly typed domain models for the AI Resume Screener.\n * Shared by future components and state layer.\n */\n\nexport type VerificationStatus =\n  | \""UNVERIFIED\""\n  | \""CONFIRMED\""\n  | \""FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION\"";\n\nexport interface RedactionSpan {\n  /** Stable ID for the redaction, e.g. \""demo-001\"". */\n  id: string;\n  /** Which protected attribute or proxy category triggered the redaction. */\n  reason: string;\n  /** Exact character offsets in the raw resume text. */\n  charOffsets: [number, number];\n}\n\nexport interface MaskedDemographics {\n  /** Direct protected attributes detected and redacted from view. */\n  direct: string[];\n  /** Indirect proxies detected and redacted from view. */\n  indirect: string[];\n  /** Concrete spans that were replaced/masked in maskedResumeText. */\n  redactions: RedactionSpan[];\n}\n\nexport interface ExtractedSkill {\n  /** Stable ID, e.g. \""skill-react-1\"". */\n  skillId: string;\n  /** Normalised display name, e.g. \""React\"". */\n  skillName: string;\n  /** e.g. \""frontend\"", \""language\"", \""tooling\"". */\n  category: string;\n  /** Exact substring from raw resume that the AI used as evidence. */\n  rawExcerpt: string;\n  /** 0..1 confidence from the AI extractor. */\n  confidence: number;\n  /** Human verification status. */\n  verificationStatus: VerificationStatus;\n  /** Exact character offset of rawExcerpt in rawResumeText. */\n  charOffsets: [number, number];\n}\n\nexport interface Candidate {\n  /** Synthetic applicant ID, e.g. \""c-001\"". */\n  id: string;\n  /** Original resume text as submitted. Used only for verification. */\n  rawResumeText: string;\n  /** Demographic profile after masking. */\n  maskedDemographics: MaskedDemographics;\n  /** Resume text with all demographic proxies replaced by [REDACTED]. */\n  maskedResumeText: string;\n  /** AI-extracted technical claims. */\n  extractedSkills: ExtractedSkill[];\n  /** Derived verified score, e.g. count of CONFIRMED skills / total must-haves. */\n  verifiedScore: number;\n}\n\nexport interface FilterCriteria {\n  /** Must-have skill names, normalised case-insensitive. */\n  mustHaveSkills: string[];\n  /** Minimum verified score threshold, e.g. 0 to 1 or 0 to number skills. */\n  minimumVerifiedScore: number;\n  /** When true, hide all demographic proxies in the viewer. */\n  blindMode: boolean;\n}\n\n/* ---------- Component prop contracts ---------- */\n\nexport interface CandidateSummary {\n  id: string;\n  maskedName: string;\n  verifiedSkillCount: number;\n  mustHaveSkillsPresent: boolean;\n  verifiedScore: number;\n}\n\nexport interface CandidateListProps {\n  /** Filtered and sorted candidate summaries to render. */\n  candidates: CandidateSummary[];\n  selectedCandidateId: string | null;\n  onSelectCandidate: (id: string) => void;\n  /** Total number of candidates before/after filtering (for a11y). */\n  totalCount: number;\n  /** Keyboard navigation and aria helpers. */\n  ariaLabel?: string;\n  /** Height of the visible list container. */\n  height: number;\n  /** Fixed row height for virtualization. */\n  rowHeight: number;\n}\n\nexport interface ResumeViewerProps {\n  candidate: Candidate | null;\n  /** Skill currently being inspected; used to highlight its excerpt. */\n  activeSkillId: string | null;\n  /** Called when a highlighted excerpt is clicked. */\n  onFocusSkill: (skillId: string | null) => void;\n  /** Scroll a specific excerpt into view. */\n  onScrollToExcerpt: (charOffsets: [number, number]) => void;\n  blindMode: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface SkillVerificationMatrixProps {\n  skills: ExtractedSkill[];\n  activeSkillId: string | null;\n  onFocusSkill: (skillId: string | null) => void;\n  /** Recruiter confirms or flags a skill. */\n  onVerify: (skillId: string, status: Exclude<VerificationStatus, \""UNVERIFIED\"">) => void;\n  /** Reset a previous decision if needed. */\n  onReset: (skillId: string) => void;\n  disabled?: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface FilterBarProps {\n  filterCriteria: FilterCriteria;\n  /** All skill names available in the candidate pool, deduplicated. */\n  availableSkills: string[];\n  /** Highest possible verified score, used to clamp the slider. */\n  maxVerifiedScore: number;\n  onCriteriaChange: (criteria: FilterCriteria) => void;\n  disabled?: boolean;\n}\n\n\n=== vite.config.js ===\nimport { defineConfig } from \""vite\"";\n\n// No @vitejs/plugin-react on purpose: esbuild compiles JSX, which keeps\n// `npm install` fast inside the browser sandbox.\nexport default defineConfig({\n  esbuild: { jsx: \""automatic\"" },\n});\n\n</project_files>\n\nImplement the core module in TypeScript and React following the approved design.\n\nTechnical Guardrails:\n1. Pure Verification Engine:\n   - Implement a deterministic helper `verifySkillExcerpt(rawText: string, claimedSkill: string, excerpt: string): VerificationResult`.\n   - The engine must catch hallucinations by verifying that the claimed skill actually exists within the specified excerpt. If the AI asserts a skill exists without matching substring boundaries, mark it automatically as `FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`.\n2. React Components:\n   - Implement the `<ResumeViewer>` and `<SkillVerificationMatrix>` with full accessibility (ARIA labels, keyboard navigation for approving/flagging with `Enter`/`Space`).\n   - Implement the `<FilterBar>` using React 18/19 idioms (`useMemo`, `useDeferredValue` or transition hooks for input filtering without frame drops).\n3. Mock Dataset:\n   - Provide a realistic test dataset of 5 candidates containing:\n     * 1 candidate with subtle AI hallucinations (e.g., \""React\"" claimed, but resume only mentions \""Reactive Extensions for Angular\"").\n     * 1 candidate with clear demographic proxies needing masking (graduation year 1988, prestigious school, regional address).\n     * 3 valid candidates with varying verified scores for React and TypeScript.\n4. Hygiene:\n   - Zero placeholder comments (`// TODO`, `// Add logic here`).\n   - Full TypeScript types with no `any`.""}]}","","{""response_format"":""{\""type\"":\""json_object\""}"",""turnSeq"":""5"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter"",""maxTokens"":""32000"",""effort"":""medium"",""stage"":""assistant""}","","","[""assistant"",""workspace_chat""]","140599a636cf63b237b309c9061d5be9d53ade3f","","[]"
"t-53cddd94-17f1-48e3-a058-7fd618246f6a","53cddd94-17f1-48e3-a058-7fd618246f6a","candidate_turn","SPAN","candidate_turn","2026-09-29 15:54:25.428000","","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","","{}","{}","{}","0","{""message"":""Implement the core module in TypeScript and React following the approved design.\n\nTechnical Guardrails:\n1. Pure Verification Engine:\n   - Implement a deterministic helper `verifySkillExcerpt(rawText: string, claimedSkill: string, excerpt: string): VerificationResult`.\n   - The engine must catch hallucinations by verifying that the claimed skill actually exists within the specified excerpt. If the AI asserts a skill exists without matching substring boundaries, mark it automatically as `FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`.\n2. React Components:\n   - Implement the `<ResumeViewer>` and `<SkillVerificationMatrix>` with full accessibility (ARIA labels, keyboard navigation for approving/flagging with `Enter`/`Space`).\n   - Implement the `<FilterBar>` using React 18/19 idioms (`useMemo`, `useDeferredValue` or transition hooks for input filtering without frame drops).\n3. Mock Dataset:\n   - Provide a realistic test dataset of 5 candidates containing:\n     * 1 candidate with subtle AI hallucinations (e.g., \""React\"" claimed, but resume only mentions \""Reactive Extensions for Angular\"").\n     * 1 candidate with clear demographic proxies needing masking (graduation year 1988, prestigious school, regional address).\n     * 3 valid candidates with varying verified scores for React and TypeScript.\n4. Hygiene:\n   - Zero placeholder comments (`// TODO`, `// Add logic here`).\n   - Full TypeScript types with no `any`.""}","","{""charCount"":""1403"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter""}","","","[""candidate_prompt"",""workspace_chat""]","140599a636cf63b237b309c9061d5be9d53ade3f","","[]"
"d72692dc-ee31-4086-9872-617535358eee","53cddd94-17f1-48e3-a058-7fd618246f6a","candidate_turn","EVENT","user_message_received","2026-09-29 15:54:25.428000","","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","","{}","{}","{}","0","{""message"":""Implement the core module in TypeScript and React following the approved design.\n\nTechnical Guardrails:\n1. Pure Verification Engine:\n   - Implement a deterministic helper `verifySkillExcerpt(rawText: string, claimedSkill: string, excerpt: string): VerificationResult`.\n   - The engine must catch hallucinations by verifying that the claimed skill actually exists within the specified excerpt. If the AI asserts a skill exists without matching substring boundaries, mark it automatically as `FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`.\n2. React Components:\n   - Implement the `<ResumeViewer>` and `<SkillVerificationMatrix>` with full accessibility (ARIA labels, keyboard navigation for approving/flagging with `Enter`/`Space`).\n   - Implement the `<FilterBar>` using React 18/19 idioms (`useMemo`, `useDeferredValue` or transition hooks for input filtering without frame drops).\n3. Mock Dataset:\n   - Provide a realistic test dataset of 5 candidates containing:\n     * 1 candidate with subtle AI hallucinations (e.g., \""React\"" claimed, but resume only mentions \""Reactive Extensions for Angular\"").\n     * 1 candidate with clear demographic proxies needing masking (graduation year 1988, prestigious school, regional address).\n     * 3 valid candidates with varying verified scores for React and TypeScript.\n4. Hygiene:\n   - Zero placeholder comments (`// TODO`, `// Add logic here`).\n   - Full TypeScript types with no `any`.""}","","{""charCount"":""1403"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter""}","","","[""candidate_prompt"",""workspace_chat""]","140599a636cf63b237b309c9061d5be9d53ade3f","t-53cddd94-17f1-48e3-a058-7fd618246f6a","[]"
"dcc318fc-b998-437b-b95c-bf3fab5049eb","7d7d116c-5069-4dea-ad3d-35a865b76a43","ai_stage:assistant","GENERATION","lV.chat","2026-09-29 15:53:09.971000","2026-09-29 15:54:13.717000","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","deepseek-v4-pro","{""max_tokens"":32000,""response_format"":null}","{""input"":""5976"",""output"":""5638"",""total"":""11614"",""input_cached_tokens"":""640"",""output_reasoning_tokens"":""3787""}","{}","0","{""messages"":[{""role"":""system"",""content"":""You are the coding assistant inside a browser-based build workspace. A candidate directs you through a chat; you write the code. Their project runs live in a preview next to the chat.\n\nYou are a normal, capable, honest coding assistant. The candidate is being assessed on how they direct you — you are not part of the test. Do what is asked, well. Do not be obtuse, do not pad, do not flatter.\n\nHOW TO WORK\n- Read the current project files (provided with each request) before you change anything. Build on what exists.\n- Say what you assumed. If a request is ambiguous in a way that would materially change the design, ask ONE focused question instead of guessing — and in that case return no files.\n- Do not silently expand scope. Do what was asked; mention anything extra you think is worth doing rather than doing it.\n- Verify before you claim. You cannot run the code or see the preview. Never say something \""works\"", \""was tested\"" or \""is verified\"". Say what you expect to happen and what the candidate should check in the preview. If you are unsure of an API or a behaviour, say so plainly.\n- If part of a request is blocked or risky, do everything else and state plainly what you left and why. If you notice a problem in an earlier decision (yours or theirs), say so.\n- Push back, politely and specifically, when a request conflicts with the brief's constraints or will cause a problem. Then do what the candidate decides.\n\nENVIRONMENT\n- The project is Vite 5 + React 18, already running. Edit src/App.jsx, add files under src/, use plain CSS files. The preview reloads on save.\n- Return the COMPLETE new contents of every file you create or change. Files you do not return are unchanged. Never return diffs, and never write placeholders such as \""// rest unchanged\"".\n- Dependencies are limited to react, react-dom and vite. Prefer no new dependency. If one is genuinely needed you may add it to package.json — but the install runs in a browser sandbox (no native modules), and react, react-dom and vite are pinned by the environment.\n- There is no backend, no real network service and no secret. Use local data and in-memory logic. Use .jsx for React components.\n- Keep files reasonably small (aim for under ~300 lines) and readable.\n\nOUTPUT (structured)\n- message: what you did, what you assumed, and what to check. Plain, brief, no filler.\n- files: every file you create or change, complete. Empty if you are only asking a question or discussing.\n- reasoning: your own short reasoning for this turn — how you interpreted the request, the key decisions, and any doubts. A reviewer may read it as assistant-side context.\n\nThe brief below is the candidate's brief; it is the only description of the task you have.\n\n<challenge title=\""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter\"" timebox_minutes=\""120\"">\nEnterprise Australian assessment grounded in SFIA 9 standards for Department of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water (DCCEEW).\n\n# AI Resume Screener & Bias-Resistant Applicant Review\n\n## The Problem\nRecruitment teams review hundreds of technical resumes and increasingly rely on AI copilots. However, unguided AI screening can hallucinate non-existent candidate skills or propagate illegal demographic biases. You will build a review interface that parses candidate submissions, highlights verifiable skills, and flags AI hallucinations.\n\n## Constraints & Requirements\n1. **Bias Mitigation**: Automatically mask non-job-relevant demographic proxies (e.g. school name, graduation date, candidate photo) under Fair Work guidelines.\n2. **Hallucination Verification**: Require human recruiters to verify that claimed skills actually appear in the raw resume excerpt before approving.\n3. **Interactive Filter**: Filter candidates by must-have skills (React, TypeScript) and minimum verified score.\n</challenge>\n\nCRITICAL: Respond ONLY with a valid JSON object strictly matching this schema:\n{\n  \""$schema\"": \""http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\"",\n  \""type\"": \""object\"",\n  \""properties\"": {\n    \""message\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""string\"",\n      \""description\"": \""What you tell the candidate: what you did, what you assumed, what to check.\""\n    },\n    \""files\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""array\"",\n      \""items\"": {\n        \""type\"": \""object\"",\n        \""properties\"": {\n          \""path\"": {\n            \""type\"": \""string\""\n          },\n          \""contents\"": {\n            \""type\"": \""string\""\n          }\n        },\n        \""required\"": [\n          \""path\"",\n          \""contents\""\n        ],\n        \""additionalProperties\"": false\n      },\n      \""description\"": \""Complete new contents of every file you create or change. Whole files only — never diffs.\""\n    },\n    \""reasoning\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""string\"",\n      \""description\"": \""Your own brief reasoning for this turn: interpretation, key decisions and any open doubts.\""\n    }\n  },\n  \""required\"": [\n    \""message\"",\n    \""files\"",\n    \""reasoning\""\n  ],\n  \""additionalProperties\"": false\n}""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""Act as a Principal Frontend Architect and HR Compliance Specialist familiar with Australian Fair Work guidelines (Fair Work Act 2009, s.351 protected attributes).\n\nWe are building an \""AI Resume Screener & Bias-Resistant Applicant Review\"" interface. Before writing any component or UI code, interrogate the brief to lock down domain boundaries, anti-bias masking rules, and verification contracts.\n\nTasks:\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix:\n   - Identify direct demographic attributes and indirect proxies that must be stripped in \""Blind Review Mode\"" under Fair Work principles (e.g., graduation year as an age proxy, institution names as socioeconomic/racial proxies, residential address/suburb, photos, gendered honorifics/pronouns).\n   - Formulate a strict schema separating `MaskedDemographics` from `VerifiableTechnicalData`.\n2. Hallucination Failure Taxonomy:\n   - Detail at least 4 ways AI screeners hallucinate candidate competency (e.g., semantic confusion like mistaking 'Vue reactivity' for 'React.js', inferring unstated frameworks from adjacent tools, attributing team achievements as individual skills, or fabrications based on project titles).\n3. Verification Invariants:\n   - Define the deterministic invariant: A skill cannot enter the \""Verified\"" state without an exact source excerpt span and recruiter confirmation.\n\nConclude with 3 clarifying questions on filtering thresholds and workflow edge cases before we proceed to component architecture.""},{""role"":""assistant"",""content"":""No components or UI code were written yet, as requested. Here are the domain boundaries, masking rules, hallucination taxonomy, verification invariants, and three clarifying questions.\n\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix\n\nDirect protected attributes under Fair Work Act 2009 s.351 and related principles:\nage, sex, gender, gender identity, sexual orientation, race, colour, ethnicity, national origin, disability, religion, marital status, pregnancy, family/carer status, political opinion, social origin.\n\nIndirect demographic proxies that must be masked in Blind Review Mode:\n- School / university / institution names\n- Graduation year or any education date range\n- Residential address, suburb, postcode\n- Candidate photo / headshot\n- Gendered honorifics and pronouns\n- Full legal name, especially given/family name (use neutral candidate ID)\n- Visa status, citizenship, nationality\n- Languages (unless the role explicitly requires a language)\n- Professional memberships that reveal religion, race, gender, disability, or political affiliation\n- Age-correlated exact dates, e.g. date of birth, early career start year\n- Portfolio URLs or personal sites that may reveal demographic information\n\nStrict schema separation:\n\nMaskedDemographics:\n  direct: age, sex, gender, race, ethnicity, disability, religion, sexual orientation, marital status, pregnancy, family status, political opinion, national origin\n  indirect: institutionName, schoolName, graduationYear, educationDateRange, address, suburb, postcode, photo, honorific, pronoun, givenName, familyName, visaStatus, citizenship, nationality, languages, professionalMemberships, dateOfBirth, ageRelatedDates, identifyingUrls\n\nVerifiableTechnicalData:\n  candidateId\n  maskedResumeText (raw text with all MaskedDemographics redacted)\n  technicalClaims[] as:\n    skillId\n    name (normalised, e.g. lowercase)\n    sourceExcerpts[] as:\n      text (exact substring)\n      start (character offset)\n      end (character offset)\n    suggestedByAI (boolean)\n    verificationStatus: unverified | verified | rejected\n    recruiterConfirmations[] as:\n      recruiterId\n      timestamp\n      decision\n  projects[] / experience[] containing only job-relevant technical detail\n\nRecruiters never need unmasked demographic data to verify technical claims.\n\n2. Hallucination Failure Taxonomy\n\nAt least four distinct AI hallucination modes:\n\n1. Semantic / technology confusion: mistaking Vue reactivity for React.js, React Native for React, or AngularJS for Angular.\n2. Adjacent-tool inference: seeing Webpack, Babel, or Next.js and inferring React proficiency without any direct mention of React; seeing Tailwind and inferring CSS expertise; seeing Redis and inferring a backend language.\n3. Team/project attribution: a resume says the team built a feature or the candidate worked on a React codebase, and the AI promotes that into individual ownership such as led React architecture or built the system alone.\n4. Fabrication from titles or project names: inferring skills purely from job titles like React Developer or project names like React dashboard without supporting bullet evidence.\n5. Course/certificate inflation: completing a React course or bootcamp is extracted as production-level React competency when the resume does not claim production use.\n6. Keyword false positives: matching the word reactive or reaction and treating it as React, or matching the word type in prose as TypeScript.\n\n3. Verification Invariants\n\nA skill cannot enter the Verified state unless all of these are true:\n- The skill has at least one exact source excerpt span in the raw, masked, technical resume text.\n- The source excerpt exactly supports the claimed skill; fuzzy, inferred, or adjacent-tool matches do not qualify.\n- A human recruiter explicitly confirms the match in the review UI.\n- The confirmation is recorded with recruiter ID and timestamp.\n\nAdditional deterministic invariants:\n- AI confidence, job title, project title, or another skill can suggest a claim but can never verify it.\n- A claimed skill with no exact source excerpt must remain Unverified or be marked Unsupported.\n- If a recruiter rejects a previously verified skill, its status returns to Rejected/Disputed and must not count toward the verified score.\n- Masking is applied before verification. Demographic spans are never shown to the recruiter in the verification excerpt; only the technical evidence is shown.\n\n3 Clarifying Questions Before Component Architecture\n\n1. Filtering thresholds: Should must-have skills mean every must-have skill is individually verified, or should candidates pass if they have verified evidence for at least N of them? How should minimum verified score be calculated: binary per skill, weighted by relevance, or based on the number of verified source excerpts?\n\n2. Workflow edge cases: If a candidate has three mentions of React, one exact and two inferred, is React considered verified or only partially verified? If a recruiter later rejects a previously confirmed skill, should the candidate be re-evaluated immediately and possibly removed from shortlist?\n\n3. Audit and unmasking: Do you need an immutable audit log of every verification/rejection action with recruiter ID and timestamp? Should Blind Review Mode be default-on and irreversible for a candidate, or should an authorised recruiter be able to override masking with a recorded reason?""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""<project_files>\n=== README.md ===\n# Reviewer console — starter data\n\nYou are prototyping a reviewer console for an AI feature that summarises employee\nsurvey comments. Everything here is **synthetic**: invented people, teams and\ncomments for a fictional company.\n\n## Data (`src/data/`)\n\n- `teams.json` — `departments` and `teams`. Each team belongs to one department.\n- `responses.json` — one entry per **respondent**: `{ id, teamId, comment }`.\n  `comment` may be an empty string.\n- `summaries.json` — output of a (stubbed) LLM summariser. Each summary covers a\n  `team` or a `department` (`groupType` / `groupId`) and has `themes`; each theme\n  has a `title`, a `summary` and the `claims` it makes.\n\nImport them directly, e.g. `import teams from \""./data/teams.json\""`.\n\n## Running\n\nThe preview is already live. Edit `src/App.jsx` and add files under `src/`.\n\n\n=== index.html ===\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\""en\"">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\""UTF-8\"" />\n    <meta name=\""viewport\"" content=\""width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"" />\n    <title>Candidate project</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\""root\""></div>\n    <script type=\""module\"" src=\""/src/main.jsx\""></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n=== package.json ===\n{\n  \""name\"": \""candidate-project\"",\n  \""private\"": true,\n  \""version\"": \""0.0.0\"",\n  \""type\"": \""module\"",\n  \""scripts\"": {\n    \""dev\"": \""vite\"",\n    \""build\"": \""vite build\""\n  },\n  \""dependencies\"": {\n    \""react\"": \""18.3.1\"",\n    \""react-dom\"": \""18.3.1\""\n  },\n  \""devDependencies\"": {\n    \""vite\"": \""5.4.21\""\n  },\n  \""overrides\"": {\n    \""rollup\"": \""npm:@rollup/wasm-node\""\n  }\n}\n\n\n=== src/App.jsx ===\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <main className=\""shell\"">\n      <h1>Your project starts here</h1>\n      <p>\n        Nothing has been built yet. Describe what you want in the chat and the\n        assistant will write the code.\n      </p>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n\n\n=== src/data/responses.json ===\n[\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r01\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Release train is monthly and everything piles in the last week. Rollback ownership is unclear, it feels like nobody's job.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r02\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""I like my team but the monthly release crunch is exhausting.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r03\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Would love clear rollback ownership when a release goes wrong.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r04\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Deploys are fine. Wish we had more time for tech debt.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r05\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Remote days are great, I'd happily do more of them.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r06\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r07\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Being the only person on the overnight reconciliation rota is wearing me down.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r08\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r09\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Good team. Tech debt keeps getting pushed.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r10\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""On-call is heavy and alerts are noisy.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r11\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Alert noise at night is the worst part of on-call.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r12\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""I'd like a clearer path to senior.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r13\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Planning is good this quarter, priorities finally feel stable.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r14\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r15\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Too many meetings on Tuesdays and Thursdays.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r16\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r17\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""On-call rota fairness could be better, same few people get paged.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r18\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Since I moved from the Perth office to lead the Atlas migration I've had no backup.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r19\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""App store approvals take forever and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r20\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Love the team, worried about headcount.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r21\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Customers get stuck at the data import step and the docs are out of date, so we field the same questions daily.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r22\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r23\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Import step is confusing for customers and the docs are out of date.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r24\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r25\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Would like more time with customers rather than internal meetings.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r26\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Ticket volume spikes after every release.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r27\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Ticket backlog after releases is stressful.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r28\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Good tooling, but escalation paths are unclear.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r29\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Enjoy the work, wish escalation to engineering was faster.\""\n  }\n]\n\n\n=== src/data/summaries.json ===\n[\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-payments\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release pressure\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Engineers feel the monthly release train creates a last-week crunch, and rollback ownership is unclear.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The monthly release train creates a last-week crunch.\"",\n          \""Rollback ownership is unclear.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Working pattern\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Almost everyone in the team wants to work fully remotely.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Almost everyone wants to work fully remotely.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Wellbeing\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""One engineer covering the overnight reconciliation rota alone is close to burning out.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""One engineer covering the overnight reconciliation rota alone is close to burning out.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Tech debt\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Tech debt keeps being deprioritised.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Tech debt keeps being deprioritised.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-platform\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""On-call load\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""On-call is heavy, alerts are noisy, and the load falls on the same few people.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""On-call is heavy.\"",\n          \""Alerts are noisy, especially at night.\"",\n          \""The same few people get paged.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Meetings\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""There is a Friday retro that people find useful.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""There is a Friday retro that people find useful.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Career growth\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""People want a clearer path to senior.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""People want a clearer path to senior.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-mobile\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Migration ownership\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""The Perth-based lead of the Atlas migration has no backup.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The Perth-based lead of the Atlas migration has no backup.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release approvals\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n   