p\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Customers struggle with the data import step and the docs are out of date.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Customers struggle with the data import step.\"",\n          \""The docs are out of date.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-support\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Post-release load\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Ticket volume and backlog spike after releases.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Ticket volume spikes after releases.\"",\n          \""The ticket backlog after releases is stressful.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Escalation\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Escalation paths to engineering are unclear and slow.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Escalation paths are unclear.\"",\n          \""Escalation to engineering is slow.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-engineering\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""department\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""engineering\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release and rollout friction\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""A monthly release crunch, unclear rollback ownership, and slow app store approvals that nobody owns.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The monthly release train creates a last-week crunch.\"",\n          \""Rollback ownership is unclear.\"",\n          \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""On-call\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""On-call load is heavy and alerts are noisy.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""On-call load is heavy.\"",\n          \""Alerts are noisy.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\n\n=== src/data/teams.json ===\n{\n  \""departments\"": [\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""engineering\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""customer-success\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Customer Success\""\n    }\n  ],\n  \""teams\"": [\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""payments\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Payments\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""platform\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Platform\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""mobile\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Mobile\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""onboarding\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Onboarding\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""customer-success\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""support\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Support\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""customer-success\""\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n\n=== src/main.jsx ===\nimport { StrictMode } from \""react\"";\nimport { createRoot } from \""react-dom/client\"";\nimport App from \""./App.jsx\"";\nimport \""./styles.css\"";\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\""root\"")).render(\n  <StrictMode>\n    <App />\n  </StrictMode>\n);\n\n\n=== src/styles.css ===\n:root {\n  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, \""Segoe UI\"", Roboto, sans-serif;\n  color: #1a1a1a;\n  background: #fafaf9;\n}\n\n* {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}\n\nbody {\n  margin: 0;\n}\n\n.shell {\n  max-width: 720px;\n  margin: 12vh auto;\n  padding: 0 24px;\n}\n\n.shell h1 {\n  font-size: 1.6rem;\n  margin: 0 0 0.5rem;\n}\n\n.shell p {\n  color: #555;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n}\n\n\n=== vite.config.js ===\nimport { defineConfig } from \""vite\"";\n\n// No @vitejs/plugin-react on purpose: esbuild compiles JSX, which keeps\n// `npm install` fast inside the browser sandbox.\nexport default defineConfig({\n  esbuild: { jsx: \""automatic\"" },\n});\n\n</project_files>\n\nApproved. Now design the system architecture and state model using React and TypeScript.\n\nDeliverables:\n1. Strongly Typed Domain Models:\n   - `Candidate`: Including unique ID, raw resume text, and masked demographic profile.\n   - `ExtractedSkill`: Containing `skillName`, `category`, `rawExcerpt`, `confidence`, `verificationStatus` ('UNVERIFIED' | 'CONFIRMED' | 'FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION'), and `charOffsets: [start, end]`.\n   - `FilterCriteria`: Must-have skills array (`['React', 'TypeScript']`), minimum verified score, and blind-mode toggle.\n2. Component Hierarchy & Props:\n   - Define exact prop contracts for:\n     * `<CandidateList>`: Virtualized/accessible candidate selection panel.\n     * `<ResumeViewer>`: Dual-pane interface with masked header and highlighted excerpt anchors.\n     * `<SkillVerificationMatrix>`: Interactive badge list where recruiters manually audit AI-extracted claims against text spans.\n     * `<FilterBar>`: Controlled multi-select and score threshold slider.\n3. Performance & Re-render Strategy:\n   - Specify state management approach (e.g., Zustand or useReducer + memoized selectors) to prevent full-tree re-renders during rapid keyboard navigation or verification clicks.\n4. Architectural Trade-off Defense:\n   - Document a structured comparison defending **Client-side filtering/memoization vs. Server-side pagination & query evaluation** for candidate pools between 50 and 5,000 applicants (touching latency, DOM memory footprint, and instantaneous regex search).""}]}","{""role"":""assistant"",""content"":""{\n  \""message\"": \""Architecture and state model locked. I added `src/types.ts` with the strongly typed domain models and component prop contracts. Summary of the design:\\n\\n1. Domain models: `Candidate` holds `rawResumeText`, `maskedResumeText`, `maskedDemographics`, and `extractedSkills`. `ExtractedSkill` has `skillName`, `category`, `rawExcerpt`, `confidence`, `verificationStatus` (`UNVERIFIED | CONFIRMED | FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION`), and exact `charOffsets` for source anchoring. `FilterCriteria` has `mustHaveSkills`, `minimumVerifiedScore`, and `blindMode`.\\n\\n2. Component hierarchy: `App` owns state and renders `FilterBar` (controlled filter controls), `CandidateList` (virtualized accessible selection panel), `ResumeViewer` (dual-pane masked text + highlighted excerpt anchors), and `SkillVerificationMatrix` (interactive badge list for recruiter verification). Prop contracts are in `src/types.ts`.\\n\\n3. State management & re-render strategy: use a single `useReducer` with normalized state keyed by id, plus memoized selectors (`filteredCandidates`, `candidateSummaries`, `availableSkills`). Wrap `CandidateList`, `ResumeViewer`, and `SkillVerificationMatrix` in `React.memo`; pass only stable props and callbacks. Candidate list uses windowing (visible row slice) to keep DOM footprint small. Verification actions update only the target skill's status; derived `verifiedScore` is computed in a selector, never stored, preventing stale state.\\n\\n4. Trade-off defense: For 50–5,000 candidates, client-side filtering + memoization + windowing is preferable. Latency is near-zero after initial load; regex search across 5,000 local strings is fast (<10ms); windowing keeps DOM memory small. Server-side pagination adds RTT on every filter and complicates cross-page excerpt highlighting. Client-side becomes risky beyond ~5,000 or when data must be remotely audit-logged; server-side query/pagination should be adopted above that threshold or when the data source is an API with mandatory server-side evaluation.\\n\\nWhat to check in the preview: the new `src/types.ts` compiles and exports all types; no UI was rendered yet, so verify the type definitions cover the agreed contracts.\"",\n  \""files\"": [\n    {\n      \""path\"": \""src/types.ts\"",\n      \""contents\"": \""/**\\n * Strongly typed domain models for the AI Resume Screener.\\n * Shared by future components and state layer.\\n */\\n\\nexport type VerificationStatus =\\n  | \\\""UNVERIFIED\\\""\\n  | \\\""CONFIRMED\\\""\\n  | \\\""FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION\\\"";\\n\\nexport interface RedactionSpan {\\n  /** Stable ID for the redaction, e.g. \\\""demo-001\\\"". */\\n  id: string;\\n  /** Which protected attribute or proxy category triggered the redaction. */\\n  reason: string;\\n  /** Exact character offsets in the raw resume text. */\\n  charOffsets: [number, number];\\n}\\n\\nexport interface MaskedDemographics {\\n  /** Direct protected attributes detected and redacted from view. */\\n  direct: string[];\\n  /** Indirect proxies detected and redacted from view. */\\n  indirect: string[];\\n  /** Concrete spans that were replaced/masked in maskedResumeText. */\\n  redactions: RedactionSpan[];\\n}\\n\\nexport interface ExtractedSkill {\\n  /** Stable ID, e.g. \\\""skill-react-1\\\"". */\\n  skillId: string;\\n  /** Normalised display name, e.g. \\\""React\\\"". */\\n  skillName: string;\\n  /** e.g. \\\""frontend\\\"", \\\""language\\\"", \\\""tooling\\\"". */\\n  category: string;\\n  /** Exact substring from raw resume that the AI used as evidence. */\\n  rawExcerpt: string;\\n  /** 0..1 confidence from the AI extractor. */\\n  confidence: number;\\n  /** Human verification status. */\\n  verificationStatus: VerificationStatus;\\n  /** Exact character offset of rawExcerpt in rawResumeText. */\\n  charOffsets: [number, number];\\n}\\n\\nexport interface Candidate {\\n  /** Synthetic applicant ID, e.g. \\\""c-001\\\"". */\\n  id: string;\\n  /** Original resume text as submitted. Used only for verification. */\\n  rawResumeText: string;\\n  /** Demographic profile after masking. */\\n  maskedDemographics: MaskedDemographics;\\n  /** Resume text with all demographic proxies replaced by [REDACTED]. */\\n  maskedResumeText: string;\\n  /** AI-extracted technical claims. */\\n  extractedSkills: ExtractedSkill[];\\n  /** Derived verified score, e.g. count of CONFIRMED skills / total must-haves. */\\n  verifiedScore: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface FilterCriteria {\\n  /** Must-have skill names, normalised case-insensitive. */\\n  mustHaveSkills: string[];\\n  /** Minimum verified score threshold, e.g. 0 to 1 or 0 to number skills. */\\n  minimumVerifiedScore: number;\\n  /** When true, hide all demographic proxies in the viewer. */\\n  blindMode: boolean;\\n}\\n\\n/* ---------- Component prop contracts ---------- */\\n\\nexport interface CandidateSummary {\\n  id: string;\\n  maskedName: string;\\n  verifiedSkillCount: number;\\n  mustHaveSkillsPresent: boolean;\\n  verifiedScore: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface CandidateListProps {\\n  /** Filtered and sorted candidate summaries to render. */\\n  candidates: CandidateSummary[];\\n  selectedCandidateId: string | null;\\n  onSelectCandidate: (id: string) => void;\\n  /** Total number of candidates before/after filtering (for a11y). */\\n  totalCount: number;\\n  /** Keyboard navigation and aria helpers. */\\n  ariaLabel?: string;\\n  /** Height of the visible list container. */\\n  height: number;\\n  /** Fixed row height for virtualization. */\\n  rowHeight: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface ResumeViewerProps {\\n  candidate: Candidate | null;\\n  /** Skill currently being inspected; used to highlight its excerpt. */\\n  activeSkillId: string | null;\\n  /** Called when a highlighted excerpt is clicked. */\\n  onFocusSkill: (skillId: string | null) => void;\\n  /** Scroll a specific excerpt into view. */\\n  onScrollToExcerpt: (charOffsets: [number, number]) => void;\\n  blindMode: boolean;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface SkillVerificationMatrixProps {\\n  skills: ExtractedSkill[];\\n  activeSkillId: string | null;\\n  onFocusSkill: (skillId: string | null) => void;\\n  /** Recruiter confirms or flags a skill. */\\n  onVerify: (skillId: string, status: Exclude<VerificationStatus, \\\""UNVERIFIED\\\"">) => void;\\n  /** Reset a previous decision if needed. */\\n  onReset: (skillId: string) => void;\\n  disabled?: boolean;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface FilterBarProps {\\n  filterCriteria: FilterCriteria;\\n  /** All skill names available in the candidate pool, deduplicated. */\\n  availableSkills: string[];\\n  /** Highest possible verified score, used to clamp the slider. */\\n  maxVerifiedScore: number;\\n  onCriteriaChange: (criteria: FilterCriteria) => void;\\n  disabled?: boolean;\\n}\\n\""\n    }\n  ],\n  \""reasoning\"": \""The candidate asked for a design-only architecture and state model after the previous domain-boundary request. I interpreted the deliverables as needing TypeScript domain definitions and component prop contracts, which I produced in `src/types.ts`, while keeping the rest of the design in the message. No UI code was added. The state management and client-vs-server trade-off are described in the message rather than in files, since they are architectural decisions, not code. The types file is compatible with Vite/JSX compilation and stays under 300 lines.\""\n}"",""reasoning_content"":""We need respond to user. They ask: \""Approved. Now design the system architecture and state model using React and TypeScript.\""\n\nThey want deliverables: 1 strongly typed domain models including Candidate, ExtractedSkill, FilterCriteria. 2 Component hierarchy & props exact contracts for CandidateList, ResumeViewer, SkillVerificationMatrix, FilterBar. 3 performance & re-render strategy state management approach. 4 architectural trade-off defense client-side filtering/memoization vs server-side pagination & query evaluation for 50-5000 applicants.\n\nWe need produce JSON with message, files, reasoning. We are in project environment with existing starter data unrelated. But this task is design only, no component code. They said \""Now design the system architecture and state model using React and TypeScript.\"" Deliverables are design. So likely no files needed? The output schema requires files array every file create or change. If no files, empty array. But maybe we should create a design document file? They asked design system architecture and state model. We could produce a Markdown file? However they might expect in chat. But output must JSON. We can include files if we create a design doc under src maybe. But environment says \""Return the COMPLETE new contents of every file you create or change.\"" If only design in message, files empty. But deliverables include TypeScript domain models; we could create a types.ts file. But the brief says \""Before writing any component or UI code\"" previous. Now approved and ask design architecture and state model using React and TypeScript. They ask for deliverables not necessarily code: strongly typed domain models, component hierarchy & props, performance strategy, trade-off defense. Could output as message. But maybe better to also create a `src/domain/types.ts` with actual TypeScript definitions and perhaps `src/architecture.md`? The environment says use plain CSS, JSX, add files under src/. It doesn't forbid TS. But package only has JSX; vite config no plugin react but esbuild handles JSX, TypeScript? Vite supports TS via esbuild, but no type checking. We can create .ts file? Under src maybe imported later. However the task specifically asks design, not code. \""Deliverables: 1. Strongly Typed Domain Models\"" implies they want the model definitions. We can present them in message; creating a types.ts file is a valid deliverable, and would be useful for later implementation. The system asked to return files if create/change. We could create `src/types.ts` with domain models. But need to ensure it's complete and matches design. Also could create `src/architecture.md` but not necessary. Since candidate wants to lock architecture before component code, a types file is reasonable. But we need be cautious: \""Before writing any component or UI code\"" earlier; now approved design system architecture and state model using React and TypeScript. They are asking for type definitions, not UI. Creating a `src/types.ts` is not UI code. It is an architecture artifact. That seems appropriate.\n\nWe need also maybe create `src/architecture.md`? Not necessary. But message can contain the design details. The files array could include `src/types.ts` containing the TypeScript domain models as requested. Component hierarchy & props can be described in message as code snippets (maybe not files). Performance strategy and trade-off defense in message.\n\nNeed ensure TypeScript types are consistent with brief: Candidate includes unique ID, raw resume text, masked demographic profile. ExtractedSkill: skillName, category, rawExcerpt, confidence, verificationStatus ('UNVERIFIED'|'CONFIRMED'|'FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION'), charOffsets:[start,end]. FilterCriteria: mustHaveSkills array, minVerifiedScore, blindModeToggle? They said must-have skills array (['React', 'TypeScript']), minimum verified score, and blind-mode toggle. We can define types.\n\nAlso we need define MaskedDemographics and VerifiableTechnicalData from previous? We can include.\n\nComponent props: exact prop contracts for CandidateList, ResumeViewer, SkillVerificationMatrix, FilterBar. Could include in message or a separate `src/components/types.ts`? We can include in `src/types.ts` as component prop interfaces? That might be good. But we need keep file small. Could include domain types and component prop types in one file. But the file path could be `src/types.ts`. It is okay.\n\nNeed decide state management: useReducer + context + memoized selectors, no external Zustand because dependencies limited. We can specify useReducer at App level with context and selectors using useMemo, React.memo, and maybe useSyncExternalStore? Simpler: useReducer + custom context with stable dispatch. For virtualized list, use `react-window`? No dependency. So we can implement simple virtualization manually or just mention. The environment doesn't include react-window. But the task is design; we can propose useReducer with refs and manual virtualization, but not code yet.\n\nTrade-off defense: client-side filtering/memoization vs server-side. We need structured comparison for 50-5000 candidates. Touch latency, DOM memory footprint, instantaneous regex search. We'll produce a table/description in message.\n\nNeed output JSON with message, files, reasoning. We'll include file `src/types.ts` with domain models and prop contracts. Maybe also `src/architecture.md`? Not needed; message can hold design. However the system output message is string, can be formatted. Could be long but okay. We need keep under token limits. We'll produce concise but comprehensive.\n\nLet's craft `src/types.ts`. Use TypeScript interfaces/types. Need ensure import path later if they use. We'll define:\n\nexport type VerificationStatus = \""UNVERIFIED\"" | \""CONFIRMED\"" | \""FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION\"";\n\nexport interface MaskedDemographics {\n  direct: string[];\n  indirect: string[];\n  redactions: RedactionSpan[]; // maybe include spans\n}\n\nexport interface RedactionSpan {\n  type: \""direct\"" | \""indirect\"";\n  reason: string;\n  charOffsets: [number, number];\n}\n\nexport interface ExtractedSkill {\n  skillId: string;\n  skillName: string;\n  category: string;\n  rawExcerpt: string;\n  confidence: number; // 0..1\n  verificationStatus: VerificationStatus;\n  charOffsets: [number, number];\n  // extra?\n}\n\nexport interface Candidate {\n  id: string;\n  rawResumeText: string;\n  maskedDemographics: MaskedDemographics;\n  maskedResumeText: string;\n  extractedSkills: ExtractedSkill[];\n  verifiedScore: number; // maybe derived\n}\n\nexport interface FilterCriteria {\n  mustHaveSkills: string[];\n  minimumVerifiedScore: number;\n  blindMode: boolean;\n}\n\nComponent props:\n\nCandidateListProps:\n  candidates: CandidateSummary[]; // summary to avoid full objects\n  selectedCandidateId: string | null;\n  onSelectCandidate: (id: string) => void;\n  filterCriteria: FilterCriteria; // maybe already applied?\n  visibleCount: number;\n  totalCount: number;\n  loadMore? no if all local.\n  // virtualization info\n  height: number;\n  rowHeight: number;\n  disabled?: boolean;\n  ariaLabel?: string;\n  // maybe ordered candidate ids\n\nCandidateSummary:\n  id, maskedName/identifier, score, mustHaveSkillsMatch: boolean, number of verified skills, etc.\n\nResumeViewerProps:\n  candidate: Candidate;\n  highlightedSpanIds?: string[]; // anchors to scroll/highlight\n  activeSkillId?: string | null;\n  onHighlightSkill: (skillId: string | null) => void;\n  onScrollToExcerpt: (charOffsets: [number, number]) => void;\n  blindMode: boolean;\n}\n\nSkillVerificationMatrixProps:\n  skills: ExtractedSkill[];\n  onVerify: (skillId: string, verificationStatus: VerificationStatus) => void;\n  onReject: (skillId: string) => void; // or use onVerify\n  activeSkillId: string | null;\n  onFocusSkill: (skillId: string | null) => void;\n  selectedSkillIds: string[];\n  // maybe disabledStatuses\n  disabled?: boolean;\n}\n\nFilterBarProps:\n  filterCriteria: FilterCriteria;\n  availableSkills: string[];\n  onCriteriaChange: (criteria: FilterCriteria) => void;\n  minScoreRange? maybe 0..max\n  maxVerifiedScore: number;\n  disabled?: boolean;\n}\n\nWe'll include these in file maybe.\n\nNeed ensure TypeScript file is valid. It will be under src. No imports needed.\n\nNow message: we need provide architecture and state model design. We'll structure:\n\n- Domain model summary referencing types.ts\n- Component hierarchy: App -> FilterBar, CandidateList, ResumeViewer + SkillVerificationMatrix. Define props and responsibilities.\n- State management: useReducer with context, selectors, React.memo, useMemo, manual virtualization (or mention if using react-window not allowed, but architecture can suggest it). We'll specify a reducer with actions.\n- Performance: normalization of state (candidates by ID, skills by ID), memoized selectors for filtered candidate list, virtualized list, stable callbacks, avoiding full-tree re-render.\n- Trade-off defense: compare client-side vs server-side for 50-5000. Provide table with columns: latency, DOM memory, regex search, total cost, etc. Conclusion: for <=5000, client-side is fine if normalized and virtualized; server-side better >5000 or for remote data.\n\nNeed include that we created `src/types.ts`.\n\nMessage should not be too huge but enough. We can maybe write as bullet points and short paragraphs.\n\nNow output JSON. Ensure no markdown in message? The message can include markdown; JSON string fine. But avoid newlines? We can include newlines escaped. We'll include bullet list.\n\nNeed also reasoning: short.\n\nLet's craft the types file carefully, complete, no placeholders.\n\nPotential issue: The project currently has only .jsx and .json; adding .ts may not be imported but it's fine. Vite supports TS. We'll create `src/types.ts`.\n\nCould also create a `src/architecture.md` with the full design? But message can carry that. If we create only types file, the file array has one file. The message contains architecture design. That satisfies deliverables.\n\nLet's write.\n\nFile contents:\n\n/**\n * Strongly typed domain models for the AI Resume Screener.\n * Shared by future components and state layer.\n */\n\nexport type VerificationStatus =\n  | \""UNVERIFIED\""\n  | \""CONFIRMED\""\n  | \""FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION\"";\n\nexport interface RedactionSpan {\n  /** Stable ID for the redaction, e.g. \""demo-001\"". */\n  id: string;\n  /** Which protected attribute or proxy category triggered the redaction. */\n  reason: string;\n  /** Exact character offsets in the raw resume text. */\n  charOffsets: [number, number];\n}\n\nexport interface MaskedDemographics {\n  /** Direct protected attributes detected and redacted from view. */\n  direct: string[];\n  /** Indirect proxies detected and redacted from view. */\n  indirect: string[];\n  /** Concrete spans that were replaced/masked in maskedResumeText. */\n  redactions: RedactionSpan[];\n}\n\nexport interface ExtractedSkill {\n  /** Stable ID, e.g. \""skill-react-1\"". */\n  skillId: string;\n  /** Normalised display name, e.g. \""React\"". */\n  skillName: string;\n  /** e.g. \""frontend\"", \""language\"", \""tooling\"". */\n  category: string;\n  /** Exact substring from raw resume that the AI used as evidence. */\n  rawExcerpt: string;\n  /** 0..1 confidence from the AI extractor. */\n  confidence: number;\n  /** Human verification status. */\n  verificationStatus: VerificationStatus;\n  /** Exact character offset of rawExcerpt in rawResumeText. */\n  charOffsets: [number, number];\n}\n\nexport interface Candidate {\n  /** Synthetic applicant ID, e.g. \""c-001\"". */\n  id: string;\n  /** Original resume text as submitted. Used only for verification. */\n  rawResumeText: string;\n  /** Demographic profile after masking. */\n  maskedDemographics: MaskedDemographics;\n  /** Resume text with all demographic proxies replaced by [REDACTED]. */\n  maskedResumeText: string;\n  /** AI-extracted technical claims. */\n  extractedSkills: ExtractedSkill[];\n  /** Derived verified score, e.g. count of CONFIRMED skills / total must-haves. */\n  verifiedScore: number;\n}\n\nexport interface FilterCriteria {\n  /** Must-have skill names, normalised case-insensitive. */\n  mustHaveSkills: string[];\n  /** Minimum verified score threshold, e.g. 0 to 1 or 0 to number skills. */\n  minimumVerifiedScore: number;\n  /** When true, hide all demographic proxies in the viewer. */\n  blindMode: boolean;\n}\n\n/* ---------- Component prop contracts ---------- */\n\nexport interface CandidateSummary {\n  id: string;\n  maskedName: string;\n  verifiedSkillCount: number;\n  mustHaveSkillsPresent: boolean;\n  verifiedScore: number;\n}\n\nexport interface CandidateListProps {\n  /** Filtered and sorted candidate summaries to render. */\n  candidates: CandidateSummary[];\n  selectedCandidateId: string | null;\n  onSelectCandidate: (id: string) => void;\n  /** Total number of candidates before/after filtering (for a11y). */\n  totalCount: number;\n  /** Keyboard navigation and aria helpers. */\n  ariaLabel?: string;\n  /** Height of the visible list container. */\n  height: number;\n  /** Fixed row height for virtualization. */\n  rowHeight: number;\n}\n\nexport interface ResumeViewerProps {\n  candidate: Candidate | null;\n  /** Skill currently being inspected; used to highlight its excerpt. */\n  activeSkillId: string | null;\n  /** Called when a highlighted excerpt is clicked. */\n  onFocusSkill: (skillId: string | null) => void;\n  /** Scroll a specific excerpt into view. */\n  onScrollToExcerpt: (charOffsets: [number, number]) => void;\n  blindMode: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface SkillVerificationMatrixProps {\n  skills: ExtractedSkill[];\n  activeSkillId: string | null;\n  onFocusSkill: (skillId: string | null) => void;\n  /** Recruiter confirms or flags a skill. */\n  onVerify: (skillId: string, status: Exclude<VerificationStatus, \""UNVERIFIED\"">) => void;\n  /** Reset a previous decision if needed. */\n  onReset: (skillId: string) => void;\n  disabled?: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface FilterBarProps {\n  filterCriteria: FilterCriteria;\n  /** All skill names available in the candidate pool, deduplicated. */\n  availableSkills: string[];\n  /** Highest possible verified score, used to clamp the slider. */\n  maxVerifiedScore: number;\n  onCriteriaChange: (criteria: FilterCriteria) => void;\n  disabled?: boolean;\n}\n\nNeed ensure Exclude<VerificationStatus, \""UNVERIFIED\""> is valid TS. Yes.\n\nNow message. We'll provide concise but thorough.\n\nMessage content:\n\n\""Design complete. I created src/types.ts with the strongly typed domain models and component prop contracts. Below is the architecture summary.\n\n1. Domain models: as in file; Candidate has raw and masked text plus extracted skills; ExtractedSkill has charOffsets and three-state verification; FilterCriteria includes mustHaveSkills, minVerifiedScore, blindMode.\n\n2. Component hierarchy:\nApp (owns reducer/context) -> FilterBar (controlled), CandidateList (virtualized), ResumeViewer (dual-pane with highlighted spans), SkillVerificationMatrix (badge list). Props as defined in file.\n\n3. State management & re-render strategy:\n- useReducer + context; state normalized with candidates by id, skills by id, filterCriteria, selectedCandidateId, activeSkillId.\n- Memoized selectors via useMemo: filteredAndSortedCandidates, candidateSummariesForList, availableSkillNames, etc.\n- CandidateList wrapped in React.memo and receives only CandidateSummary[]; row components are also memoized; list implements simple windowing (slice visible range) or uses a lightweight custom virtualization hook (no external dependency).\n- Verification clicks dispatch actions that update only the affected ExtractedSkill's verificationStatus; Candidate derived verifiedScore is recomputed in selector, not stored, to avoid stale state.\n- ResumeViewer is memoized and only re-renders when selected candidate, activeSkillId, or blindMode changes.\n- All callbacks are stable via useCallback with functional setState/dispatch.\n\n4. Trade-off defense: client-side vs server-side for 50–5,000 candidates.\nTable outline:\n- Latency: client-side near-instant after initial load, even 5,000 items in memory; server adds network RTT per filter, slower for regex highlighting and badge toggles.\n- DOM memory: client-side can keep DOM small with windowing (only visible rows); server pagination also small but adds complexity for cross-page highlighting.\n- Instant regex search: client-side can search local string in <10ms for 5,000 items of ~2KB text; server requires debounced endpoint and still needs excerpt mapping.\n- Cons: client-side cannot scale beyond ~10k without risk; server-side is better for remote/audited data and CSRF-safe mutation trails.\nConclusion: for 50–5,000, use client-side filtering + memoization + windowing. Move to server-side pagination/query when candidate pool exceeds ~5,000 or when data comes from an API with audit requirements.\""\n\nWe need output JSON with message, files, reasoning. Ensure message string properly escaped.\n\nWe'll include the file in files array.\n\nLet's produce final JSON.""}","{""response_format"":""{\""type\"":\""json_object\""}"",""turnSeq"":""3"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter"",""maxTokens"":""32000"",""effort"":""medium"",""stage"":""assistant""}","","","[""assistant"",""workspace_chat""]","140599a636cf63b237b309c9061d5be9d53ade3f","","[]"
"c473595e-1cd1-4409-ba89-14e3a519efe9","fe60b3ad-0630-446b-b3d6-3188a13fbde1","candidate_turn","EVENT","user_message_received","2026-09-29 15:53:09.969000","","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","","{}","{}","{}","0","{""message"":""Approved. Now design the system architecture and state model using React and TypeScript.\n\nDeliverables:\n1. Strongly Typed Domain Models:\n   - `Candidate`: Including unique ID, raw resume text, and masked demographic profile.\n   - `ExtractedSkill`: Containing `skillName`, `category`, `rawExcerpt`, `confidence`, `verificationStatus` ('UNVERIFIED' | 'CONFIRMED' | 'FLAGGED_HALLUCINATION'), and `charOffsets: [start, end]`.\n   - `FilterCriteria`: Must-have skills array (`['React', 'TypeScript']`), minimum verified score, and blind-mode toggle.\n2. Component Hierarchy & Props:\n   - Define exact prop contracts for:\n     * `<CandidateList>`: Virtualized/accessible candidate selection panel.\n     * `<ResumeViewer>`: Dual-pane interface with masked header and highlighted excerpt anchors.\n     * `<SkillVerificationMatrix>`: Interactive badge list where recruiters manually audit AI-extracted claims against text spans.\n     * `<FilterBar>`: Controlled multi-select and score threshold slider.\n3. Performance & Re-render Strategy:\n   - Specify state management approach (e.g., Zustand or useReducer + memoized selectors) to prevent full-tree re-renders during rapid keyboard navigation or verification clicks.\n4. Architectural Trade-off Defense:\n   - Document a structured comparison defending **Client-side filtering/memoization vs. Server-side pagination & query evaluation** for candidate pools between 50 and 5,000 applicants (touching latency, DOM memory footprint, and instantaneous regex search).""}","","{""charCount"":""1501"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter""}","","","[""candidate_prompt"",""workspace_chat""]","140599a636cf63b237b309c9061d5be9d53ade3f