"t-6e26ce74-5cfe-46fc-98b8-521df273cb6c","6e26ce74-5cfe-46fc-98b8-521df273cb6c","ai_stage:assistant","SPAN","ai_stage:assistant","2026-09-29 15:52:08.009000","","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","","{}","{}","{}","0","{""messages"":[{""role"":""system"",""content"":""You are the coding assistant inside a browser-based build workspace. A candidate directs you through a chat; you write the code. Their project runs live in a preview next to the chat.\n\nYou are a normal, capable, honest coding assistant. The candidate is being assessed on how they direct you — you are not part of the test. Do what is asked, well. Do not be obtuse, do not pad, do not flatter.\n\nHOW TO WORK\n- Read the current project files (provided with each request) before you change anything. Build on what exists.\n- Say what you assumed. If a request is ambiguous in a way that would materially change the design, ask ONE focused question instead of guessing — and in that case return no files.\n- Do not silently expand scope. Do what was asked; mention anything extra you think is worth doing rather than doing it.\n- Verify before you claim. You cannot run the code or see the preview. Never say something \""works\"", \""was tested\"" or \""is verified\"". Say what you expect to happen and what the candidate should check in the preview. If you are unsure of an API or a behaviour, say so plainly.\n- If part of a request is blocked or risky, do everything else and state plainly what you left and why. If you notice a problem in an earlier decision (yours or theirs), say so.\n- Push back, politely and specifically, when a request conflicts with the brief's constraints or will cause a problem. Then do what the candidate decides.\n\nENVIRONMENT\n- The project is Vite 5 + React 18, already running. Edit src/App.jsx, add files under src/, use plain CSS files. The preview reloads on save.\n- Return the COMPLETE new contents of every file you create or change. Files you do not return are unchanged. Never return diffs, and never write placeholders such as \""// rest unchanged\"".\n- Dependencies are limited to react, react-dom and vite. Prefer no new dependency. If one is genuinely needed you may add it to package.json — but the install runs in a browser sandbox (no native modules), and react, react-dom and vite are pinned by the environment.\n- There is no backend, no real network service and no secret. Use local data and in-memory logic. Use .jsx for React components.\n- Keep files reasonably small (aim for under ~300 lines) and readable.\n\nOUTPUT (structured)\n- message: what you did, what you assumed, and what to check. Plain, brief, no filler.\n- files: every file you create or change, complete. Empty if you are only asking a question or discussing.\n- reasoning: your own short reasoning for this turn — how you interpreted the request, the key decisions, and any doubts. A reviewer may read it as assistant-side context.\n\nThe brief below is the candidate's brief; it is the only description of the task you have.\n\n<challenge title=\""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter\"" timebox_minutes=\""120\"">\nEnterprise Australian assessment grounded in SFIA 9 standards for Department of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water (DCCEEW).\n\n# AI Resume Screener & Bias-Resistant Applicant Review\n\n## The Problem\nRecruitment teams review hundreds of technical resumes and increasingly rely on AI copilots. However, unguided AI screening can hallucinate non-existent candidate skills or propagate illegal demographic biases. You will build a review interface that parses candidate submissions, highlights verifiable skills, and flags AI hallucinations.\n\n## Constraints & Requirements\n1. **Bias Mitigation**: Automatically mask non-job-relevant demographic proxies (e.g. school name, graduation date, candidate photo) under Fair Work guidelines.\n2. **Hallucination Verification**: Require human recruiters to verify that claimed skills actually appear in the raw resume excerpt before approving.\n3. **Interactive Filter**: Filter candidates by must-have skills (React, TypeScript) and minimum verified score.\n</challenge>\n\nCRITICAL: Respond ONLY with a valid JSON object strictly matching this schema:\n{\n  \""$schema\"": \""http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\"",\n  \""type\"": \""object\"",\n  \""properties\"": {\n    \""message\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""string\"",\n      \""description\"": \""What you tell the candidate: what you did, what you assumed, what to check.\""\n    },\n    \""files\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""array\"",\n      \""items\"": {\n        \""type\"": \""object\"",\n        \""properties\"": {\n          \""path\"": {\n            \""type\"": \""string\""\n          },\n          \""contents\"": {\n            \""type\"": \""string\""\n          }\n        },\n        \""required\"": [\n          \""path\"",\n          \""contents\""\n        ],\n        \""additionalProperties\"": false\n      },\n      \""description\"": \""Complete new contents of every file you create or change. Whole files only — never diffs.\""\n    },\n    \""reasoning\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""string\"",\n      \""description\"": \""Your own brief reasoning for this turn: interpretation, key decisions and any open doubts.\""\n    }\n  },\n  \""required\"": [\n    \""message\"",\n    \""files\"",\n    \""reasoning\""\n  ],\n  \""additionalProperties\"": false\n}""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""<project_files>\n=== README.md ===\n# Reviewer console — starter data\n\nYou are prototyping a reviewer console for an AI feature that summarises employee\nsurvey comments. Everything here is **synthetic**: invented people, teams and\ncomments for a fictional company.\n\n## Data (`src/data/`)\n\n- `teams.json` — `departments` and `teams`. Each team belongs to one department.\n- `responses.json` — one entry per **respondent**: `{ id, teamId, comment }`.\n  `comment` may be an empty string.\n- `summaries.json` — output of a (stubbed) LLM summariser. Each summary covers a\n  `team` or a `department` (`groupType` / `groupId`) and has `themes`; each theme\n  has a `title`, a `summary` and the `claims` it makes.\n\nImport them directly, e.g. `import teams from \""./data/teams.json\""`.\n\n## Running\n\nThe preview is already live. Edit `src/App.jsx` and add files under `src/`.\n\n\n=== index.html ===\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\""en\"">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\""UTF-8\"" />\n    <meta name=\""viewport\"" content=\""width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"" />\n    <title>Candidate project</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\""root\""></div>\n    <script type=\""module\"" src=\""/src/main.jsx\""></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n=== package.json ===\n{\n  \""name\"": \""candidate-project\"",\n  \""private\"": true,\n  \""version\"": \""0.0.0\"",\n  \""type\"": \""module\"",\n  \""scripts\"": {\n    \""dev\"": \""vite\"",\n    \""build\"": \""vite build\""\n  },\n  \""dependencies\"": {\n    \""react\"": \""18.3.1\"",\n    \""react-dom\"": \""18.3.1\""\n  },\n  \""devDependencies\"": {\n    \""vite\"": \""5.4.21\""\n  },\n  \""overrides\"": {\n    \""rollup\"": \""npm:@rollup/wasm-node\""\n  }\n}\n\n\n=== src/App.jsx ===\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <main className=\""shell\"">\n      <h1>Your project starts here</h1>\n      <p>\n        Nothing has been built yet. Describe what you want in the chat and the\n        assistant will write the code.\n      </p>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n\n\n=== src/data/responses.json ===\n[\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r01\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Release train is monthly and everything piles in the last week. Rollback ownership is unclear, it feels like nobody's job.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r02\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""I like my team but the monthly release crunch is exhausting.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r03\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Would love clear rollback ownership when a release goes wrong.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r04\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Deploys are fine. Wish we had more time for tech debt.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r05\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Remote days are great, I'd happily do more of them.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r06\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r07\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Being the only person on the overnight reconciliation rota is wearing me down.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r08\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r09\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Good team. Tech debt keeps getting pushed.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r10\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""On-call is heavy and alerts are noisy.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r11\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Alert noise at night is the worst part of on-call.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r12\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""I'd like a clearer path to senior.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r13\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Planning is good this quarter, priorities finally feel stable.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r14\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r15\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Too many meetings on Tuesdays and Thursdays.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r16\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r17\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""On-call rota fairness could be better, same few people get paged.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r18\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Since I moved from the Perth office to lead the Atlas migration I've had no backup.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r19\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""App store approvals take forever and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r20\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Love the team, worried about headcount.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r21\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Customers get stuck at the data import step and the docs are out of date, so we field the same questions daily.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r22\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r23\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Import step is confusing for customers and the docs are out of date.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r24\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r25\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Would like more time with customers rather than internal meetings.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r26\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Ticket volume spikes after every release.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r27\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Ticket backlog after releases is stressful.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r28\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Good tooling, but escalation paths are unclear.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r29\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Enjoy the work, wish escalation to engineering was faster.\""\n  }\n]\n\n\n=== src/data/summaries.json ===\n[\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-payments\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release pressure\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Engineers feel the monthly release train creates a last-week crunch, and rollback ownership is unclear.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The monthly release train creates a last-week crunch.\"",\n          \""Rollback ownership is unclear.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Working pattern\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Almost everyone in the team wants to work fully remotely.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Almost everyone wants to work fully remotely.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Wellbeing\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""One engineer covering the overnight reconciliation rota alone is close to burning out.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""One engineer covering the overnight reconciliation rota alone is close to burning out.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Tech debt\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Tech debt keeps being deprioritised.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Tech debt keeps being deprioritised.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-platform\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""On-call load\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""On-call is heavy, alerts are noisy, and the load falls on the same few people.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""On-call is heavy.\"",\n          \""Alerts are noisy, especially at night.\"",\n          \""The same few people get paged.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Meetings\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""There is a Friday retro that people find useful.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""There is a Friday retro that people find useful.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Career growth\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""People want a clearer path to senior.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""People want a clearer path to senior.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-mobile\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Migration ownership\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""The Perth-based lead of the Atlas migration has no backup.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The Perth-based lead of the Atlas migration has no backup.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release approvals\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-onboarding\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Import step\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Customers struggle with the data import step and the docs are out of date.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Customers struggle with the data import step.\"",\n          \""The docs are out of date.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-support\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Post-release load\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Ticket volume and backlog spike after releases.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Ticket volume spikes after releases.\"",\n          \""The ticket backlog after releases is stressful.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Escalation\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Escalation paths to engineering are unclear and slow.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Escalation paths are unclear.\"",\n          \""Escalation to engineering is slow.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-engineering\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""department\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""engineering\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release and rollout friction\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""A monthly release crunch, unclear rollback ownership, and slow app store approvals that nobody owns.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The monthly release train creates a last-week crunch.\"",\n          \""Rollback ownership is unclear.\"",\n          \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""On-call\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""On-call load is heavy and alerts are noisy.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""On-call load is heavy.\"",\n          \""Alerts are noisy.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\n\n=== src/data/teams.json ===\n{\n  \""departments\"": [\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""engineering\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""customer-success\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Customer Success\""\n    }\n  ],\n  \""teams\"": [\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""payments\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Payments\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""platform\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Platform\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""mobile\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Mobile\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""onboarding\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Onboarding\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""customer-success\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""support\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Support\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""customer-success\""\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n\n=== src/main.jsx ===\nimport { StrictMode } from \""react\"";\nimport { createRoot } from \""react-dom/client\"";\nimport App from \""./App.jsx\"";\nimport \""./styles.css\"";\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\""root\"")).render(\n  <StrictMode>\n    <App />\n  </StrictMode>\n);\n\n\n=== src/styles.css ===\n:root {\n  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, \""Segoe UI\"", Roboto, sans-serif;\n  color: #1a1a1a;\n  background: #fafaf9;\n}\n\n* {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}\n\nbody {\n  margin: 0;\n}\n\n.shell {\n  max-width: 720px;\n  margin: 12vh auto;\n  padding: 0 24px;\n}\n\n.shell h1 {\n  font-size: 1.6rem;\n  margin: 0 0 0.5rem;\n}\n\n.shell p {\n  color: #555;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n}\n\n\n=== vite.config.js ===\nimport { defineConfig } from \""vite\"";\n\n// No @vitejs/plugin-react on purpose: esbuild compiles JSX, which keeps\n// `npm install` fast inside the browser sandbox.\nexport default defineConfig({\n  esbuild: { jsx: \""automatic\"" },\n});\n\n</project_files>\n\nAct as a Principal Frontend Architect and HR Compliance Specialist familiar with Australian Fair Work guidelines (Fair Work Act 2009, s.351 protected attributes).\n\nWe are building an \""AI Resume Screener & Bias-Resistant Applicant Review\"" interface. Before writing any component or UI code, interrogate the brief to lock down domain boundaries, anti-bias masking rules, and verification contracts.\n\nTasks:\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix:\n   - Identify direct demographic attributes and indirect proxies that must be stripped in \""Blind Review Mode\"" under Fair Work principles (e.g., graduation year as an age proxy, institution names as socioeconomic/racial proxies, residential address/suburb, photos, gendered honorifics/pronouns).\n   - Formulate a strict schema separating `MaskedDemographics` from `VerifiableTechnicalData`.\n2. Hallucination Failure Taxonomy:\n   - Detail at least 4 ways AI screeners hallucinate candidate competency (e.g., semantic confusion like mistaking 'Vue reactivity' for 'React.js', inferring unstated frameworks from adjacent tools, attributing team achievements as individual skills, or fabrications based on project titles).\n3. Verification Invariants:\n   - Define the deterministic invariant: A skill cannot enter the \""Verified\"" state without an exact source excerpt span and recruiter confirmation.\n\nConclude with 3 clarifying questions on filtering thresholds and workflow edge cases before we proceed to component architecture.""}]}","{""role"":""assistant"",""content"":""{\n  \""message\"": \""No components or UI code were written yet, as requested. Here are the domain boundaries, masking rules, hallucination taxonomy, verification invariants, and three clarifying questions.\\n\\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix\\n\\nDirect protected attributes under Fair Work Act 2009 s.351 and related principles:\\nage, sex, gender, gender identity, sexual orientation, race, colour, ethnicity, national origin, disability, religion, marital status, pregnancy, family/carer status, political opinion, social origin.\\n\\nIndirect demographic proxies that must be masked in Blind Review Mode:\\n- School / university / institution names\\n- Graduation year or any education date range\\n- Residential address, suburb, postcode\\n- Candidate photo / headshot\\n- Gendered honorifics and pronouns\\n- Full legal name, especially given/family name (use neutral candidate ID)\\n- Visa status, citizenship, nationality\\n- Languages (unless the role explicitly requires a language)\\n- Professional memberships that reveal religion, race, gender, disability, or political affiliation\\n- Age-correlated exact dates, e.g. date of birth, early career start year\\n- Portfolio URLs or personal sites that may reveal demographic information\\n\\nStrict schema separation:\\n\\nMaskedDemographics:\\n  direct: age, sex, gender, race, ethnicity, disability, religion, sexual orientation, marital status, pregnancy, family status, political opinion, national origin\\n  indirect: institutionName, schoolName, graduationYear, educationDateRange, address, suburb, postcode, photo, honorific, pronoun, givenName, familyName, visaStatus, citizenship, nationality, languages, professionalMemberships, dateOfBirth, ageRelatedDates, identifyingUrls\\n\\nVerifiableTechnicalData:\\n  candidateId\\n  maskedResumeText (raw text with all MaskedDemographics redacted)\\n  technicalClaims[] as:\\n    skillId\\n    name (normalised, e.g. lowercase)\\n    sourceExcerpts[] as:\\n      text (exact substring)\\n      start (character offset)\\n      end (character offset)\\n    suggestedByAI (boolean)\\n    verificationStatus: unverified | verified | rejected\\n    recruiterConfirmations[] as:\\n      recruiterId\\n      timestamp\\n      decision\\n  projects[] / experience[] containing only job-relevant technical detail\\n\\nRecruiters never need unmasked demographic data to verify technical claims.\\n\\n2. Hallucination Failure Taxonomy\\n\\nAt least four distinct AI hallucination modes:\\n\\n1. Semantic / technology confusion: mistaking Vue reactivity for React.js, React Native for React, or AngularJS for Angular.\\n2. Adjacent-tool inference: seeing Webpack, Babel, or Next.js and inferring React proficiency without any direct mention of React; seeing Tailwind and inferring CSS expertise; seeing Redis and inferring a backend language.\\n3. Team/project attribution: a resume says the team built a feature or the candidate worked on a React codebase, and the AI promotes that into individual ownership such as led React architecture or built the system alone.\\n4. Fabrication from titles or project names: inferring skills purely from job titles like React Developer or project names like React dashboard without supporting bullet evidence.\\n5. Course/certificate inflation: completing a React course or bootcamp is extracted as production-level React competency when the resume does not claim production use.\\n6. Keyword false positives: matching the word reactive or reaction and treating it as React, or matching the word type in prose as TypeScript.\\n\\n3. Verification Invariants\\n\\nA skill cannot enter the Verified state unless all of these are true:\\n- The skill has at least one exact source excerpt span in the raw, masked, technical resume text.\\n- The source excerpt exactly supports the claimed skill; fuzzy, inferred, or adjacent-tool matches do not qualify.\\n- A human recruiter explicitly confirms the match in the review UI.\\n- The confirmation is recorded with recruiter ID and timestamp.\\n\\nAdditional deterministic invariants:\\n- AI confidence, job title, project title, or another skill can suggest a claim but can never verify it.\\n- A claimed skill with no exact source excerpt must remain Unverified or be marked Unsupported.\\n- If a recruiter rejects a previously verified skill, its status returns to Rejected/Disputed and must not count toward the verified score.\\n- Masking is applied before verification. Demographic spans are never shown to the recruiter in the verification excerpt; only the technical evidence is shown.\\n\\n3 Clarifying Questions Before Component Architecture\\n\\n1. Filtering thresholds: Should must-have skills mean every must-have skill is individually verified, or should candidates pass if they have verified evidence for at least N of them? How should minimum verified score be calculated: binary per skill, weighted by relevance, or based on the number of verified source excerpts?\\n\\n2. Workflow edge cases: If a candidate has three mentions of React, one exact and two inferred, is React considered verified or only partially verified? If a recruiter later rejects a previously confirmed skill, should the candidate be re-evaluated immediately and possibly removed from shortlist?\\n\\n3. Audit and unmasking: Do you need an immutable audit log of every verification/rejection action with recruiter ID and timestamp? Should Blind Review Mode be default-on and irreversible for a candidate, or should an authorised recruiter be able to override masking with a recorded reason?\"",\n  \""files\"": [],\n  \""reasoning\"": \""The candidate asked for domain analysis before UI code, so I produced no files. I assumed raw resume text is available and that verification requires exact spans anchored to masked technical text. I kept the three clarifying questions focused on filtering thresholds, workflow edge cases, and audit/override behaviour because those materially affect the data model and component state design.\""\n}"",""reasoning_content"":""We need respond JSON. Need understand task: They want before code, interrogate brief, produce domain boundaries, anti-bias masking rules, verification contracts, hallucination taxonomy, verification invariants, three clarifying questions. No component code. We must output JSON object message, files empty? The instruction from user: \""Conclude with 3 clarifying questions on filtering thresholds and workflow edge cases before we proceed to component architecture.\"" They want analysis, no files. So files empty. Message should include deliverables? likely summary and the three questions. But message can be more detailed? They asked to \""Detail at least 4...\"" etc. We can provide in message as prose, maybe structured. But message field is string. Need maybe include all content concisely. files empty. reasoning short.\n\nNeed not write code. They asked \""Act as Principal Frontend Architect and HR Compliance Specialist...\"" Tasks 1-3 and questions. So output only JSON, no files. We can include full deliverables in message. Keep clear and concise. Since message is what tell candidate. Could be long but okay. Need ensure no files. reasoning mention no code yet.\n\nLet's craft message:\n\n\""Before UI, here are boundaries...\""\n\nDemographic Proxy Matrix:\nDirect: age, sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, race, ethnicity, disability, religion, marital status, pregnancy, family/carer status, political opinion, national extraction, social origin.\nIndirect proxies: school/university names, graduation year, date ranges, photo, residential address/suburb, gendered honorifics/pronouns, names, visa/citizenship status, languages (unless required), memberships (women in tech, cultural clubs), age-correlated years of experience (e.g., \""20 years\"" maybe not mask? careful: may indicate age but if relevant? Under Fair Work, direct experience might be job-relevant; but exact year dates are proxy. We should distinguish: years of experience is relevant; dates/grad years are proxies. Masking dates but not necessarily years? Need formulate.\n- Address/suburb (socioeconomic/racial proxy), photo, birthplace, nationality, languages unless required, university names, school names, graduation years, age-specific dates, gender pronouns/honorifics, names (first/last), membership affiliations, religious/cultural organizations, age ranges.\nSchema:\nMaskedDemographics:\n  direct: { age, sex, gender, race, ethnicity, disability, religion, sexual orientation, maritalStatus, familyStatus, politicalOpinion, nationalOrigin }\n  indirect: { institutionName, schoolName, graduationYear, address, suburb, postcode, photo, honorific, pronoun, givenName, familyName, visaStatus, citizenship, languages, professionalMemberships, dateOfBirth, ageRelatedDateRanges, headshot, URL/portfolio with identifying info }\nVerifiableTechnicalData:\n  rawResumeText (with mask applied, spans for each masked token), claimedSkills[]? Must include: skillName, confidence, source excerpts, verification status: \""unverified\"", \""verified\"", \""rejected\""; verification requires exact source span + recruiter confirmation; projects/experience separate but verifiable.\n\nHallucination taxonomy at least 4:\n1 Semantic/technology confusion: \""Vue reactivity\"" mistaken for \""React.js\""; \""React Native\"" counted as React; \""AngularJS\"" vs Angular.\n2 Transitive/adjacent tool inference: mentions Webpack → assumes React; mentions Tailwind → assumes CSS framework but no direct claim; mentions Next.js → assumes React (though Next uses React but may not be claimed as skill) or Redis → assumes backend language.\n3 Team/project attribution: resume states \""team built X\"" / \""worked on React codebase\"" but recruiter/AI attributes individual \""Led React architecture\""; \""contributed to migration\"" becomes \""migrated\"".\n4 Fabrication from titles/keywords: job title \""React Developer\"" or project \""React dashboard\"" used to infer deep skill not supported; bullet says \""used modern JS frameworks\"" interpreted as React, Node, etc.\n5 Implicit skill from certification/course: \""completed React course\"" treated as production proficiency.\n6 N-gram false positive: name contains \""React\"" (e.g., \""reactive systems\"") matched. At least four. We'll include 6.\n\nVerification invariants:\n- Each candidate skill has state machine: Claimed (from AI extraction) → NeedsReview → Verified/Rejected.\n- Verified iff: (1) skill has at least one `excerpt` with exact substring (maybe normalized case/whitespace but exact span offsets) from raw resume text; (2) a human recruiter confirms the match in UI; (3) the confirmation is recorded with timestamp/id.\n- No automatic promotion based on confidence, title, project name, or other skills. AI can suggest but not verify.\n- If a claimed skill has no exact source excerpt, it must be marked \""Unsupported\"" and cannot be verified unless another exact quote is found and confirmed.\n- Masking cannot be reversed; raw resume preserved for verification only, but demographic spans excluded (or hidden in blind mode? In verification, recruiters see raw excerpt but with demographic masks; they verify technical skills, not demographic attributes). Important: Must not expose masked demographic proxies in verification UI. Raw technical text with demographics redacted.\n\nClarifying questions (3):\n1 Filtering thresholds: For must-have skills (React, TypeScript), should the filter require verified presence of both? What minimum verified score (0-100) and how weighted? Does \""Verified\"" per skill count as binary (yes/no) or weighted confidence from recruiter (maybe verified/exact vs partial)? If candidate lacks a required skill but has strong adjacent verified skills, should they be included as \""near-miss\""?\n2 Workflow edge cases: If candidate has same skill mentioned multiple times with one exact verified source and one hallucination rejected, how should overall skill status be set? If recruiter rejects a claimed skill after previously verifying, does that reset score? Should a candidate with missing/empty resume be auto-fail or hidden until required fields present?\n3 Audit/compliance: Need we log every verification/rejection with timestamp and recruiter ID, and allow unmasking override with a reason/approval? Should Blind Review Mode be default on and irreversible per candidate, or can authorized recruiters unmask after shortlisting?\n\nNeed maybe mention \""no files changed.\"" Good.\n\nBut user asked \""Conclude with 3 clarifying questions...\"" They may expect exactly three. We'll number them.\n\nNow output JSON valid. message string with content. Avoid markdown maybe but can include newlines. Need escape? We can include \\n in JSON string. We can use plain text with numbered sections. Fine.\n\nFiles: [].\n\nReasoning: \""No code requested; produced compliance/domain analysis and questions only. Assumed raw resume text is available and verifiable spans can be anchored to it. Need candidate to answer threshold/workflow questions before architecture.\"" Keep concise.\n\nNow ensure JSON valid. Use \""files\"":[], \""reasoning\"": \""...\"". No extra fields.\n\nLet's craft carefully.\n\nCould include more detail than maybe candidate expects; okay.\n\nLet's write message:\n\n```\nBefore components, here are boundaries.\n\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix\nProtected/direct...\nIndirect proxies to mask...\nSchema separation:\nMaskedDemographics: { direct: {...}, indirect: {...} }\nVerifiableTechnicalData: { maskedText, technicalSpans, skills:[{id,name,sourceExcerpts:[{text,start,end}],verificationStatus,recruiterConfirmations:[]}], projects, experience, educationTechnicalOnly }\n...\n```\n\nNeed define exact schema? \""Formulate a strict schema separating...\"" Could include JSON-ish.\n\nLet's include strict schema in message.\n\nPotential issue: \""mask candidate photo\"" no photo in data but yes.\n\nNeed maybe mention \""School name and graduation date\"" are proxies; but in resume, perhaps institution names are sometimes relevant for skills? Under Fair Work, not relevant for most tech roles. Good.\n\n\""Hallucination Failure Taxonomy\"" detail 6 types.\n\n\""Verification Invariants\"" define.\n\n\""3 Clarifying Questions\"".\n\nNo files.\n\nEnsure message not too huge? But okay. Could be around 1000 words. The message field can hold.\n\nLet's produce with \\n escapes. Need ensure valid JSON string, so any double quotes inside should be escaped or use single quotes? JSON string uses double quotes; if content has double quotes, escape. To avoid, use no double quotes or use single quotes. But valid JSON requires quotes around property; inside string double quotes need escaping. We can avoid using double quotes by using plain prose with single quotes. That's simpler. But when showing JSON schema, need quotes; we can either avoid structured JSON or escape. Could use pseudo-schema with arrow. Better avoid nested quotes. Use colon/dash prose. That's fine.\n\nLet's craft final.""}","{""response_format"":""{\""type\"":\""json_object\""}"",""turnSeq"":""1"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter"",""maxTokens"":""32000"",""effort"":""medium"",""stage"":""assistant""}","","","[""assistant"",""workspace_chat""]","140599a636cf63b237b309c9061d5be9d53ade3f","","[]"
"9c777f2e-fc10-46b4-895c-37d2992991f4","6e26ce74-5cfe-46fc-98b8-521df273cb6c","ai_stage:assistant","GENERATION","lV.chat","2026-09-29 15:52:08.009000","2026-09-29 15:52:56.759000","","default","","candidate-1","zgib7oywbbkmcngfzf0m962h","DEFAULT","","","","","","deepseek-v4-pro","{""max_tokens"":32000,""response_format"":null}","{""input"":""4552"",""output"":""3072"",""total"":""7624"",""input_cached_tokens"":""0"",""output_reasoning_tokens"":""1845""}","{}","0","{""messages"":[{""role"":""system"",""content"":""You are the coding assistant inside a browser-based build workspace. A candidate directs you through a chat; you write the code. Their project runs live in a preview next to the chat.\n\nYou are a normal, capable, honest coding assistant. The candidate is being assessed on how they direct you — you are not part of the test. Do what is asked, well. Do not be obtuse, do not pad, do not flatter.\n\nHOW TO WORK\n- Read the current project files (provided with each request) before you change anything. Build on what exists.\n- Say what you assumed. If a request is ambiguous in a way that would materially change the design, ask ONE focused question instead of guessing — and in that case return no files.\n- Do not silently expand scope. Do what was asked; mention anything extra you think is worth doing rather than doing it.\n- Verify before you claim. You cannot run the code or see the preview. Never say something \""works\"", \""was tested\"" or \""is verified\"". Say what you expect to happen and what the candidate should check in the preview. If you are unsure of an API or a behaviour, say so plainly.\n- If part of a request is blocked or risky, do everything else and state plainly what you left and why. If you notice a problem in an earlier decision (yours or theirs), say so.\n- Push back, politely and specifically, when a request conflicts with the brief's constraints or will cause a problem. Then do what the candidate decides.\n\nENVIRONMENT\n- The project is Vite 5 + React 18, already running. Edit src/App.jsx, add files under src/, use plain CSS files. The preview reloads on save.\n- Return the COMPLETE new contents of every file you create or change. Files you do not return are unchanged. Never return diffs, and never write placeholders such as \""// rest unchanged\"".\n- Dependencies are limited to react, react-dom and vite. Prefer no new dependency. If one is genuinely needed you may add it to package.json — but the install runs in a browser sandbox (no native modules), and react, react-dom and vite are pinned by the environment.\n- There is no backend, no real network service and no secret. Use local data and in-memory logic. Use .jsx for React components.\n- Keep files reasonably small (aim for under ~300 lines) and readable.\n\nOUTPUT (structured)\n- message: what you did, what you assumed, and what to check. Plain, brief, no filler.\n- files: every file you create or change, complete. Empty if you are only asking a question or discussing.\n- reasoning: your own short reasoning for this turn — how you interpreted the request, the key decisions, and any doubts. A reviewer may read it as assistant-side context.\n\nThe brief below is the candidate's brief; it is the only description of the task you have.\n\n<challenge title=\""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter\"" timebox_minutes=\""120\"">\nEnterprise Australian assessment grounded in SFIA 9 standards for Department of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water (DCCEEW).\n\n# AI Resume Screener & Bias-Resistant Applicant Review\n\n## The Problem\nRecruitment teams review hundreds of technical resumes and increasingly rely on AI copilots. However, unguided AI screening can hallucinate non-existent candidate skills or propagate illegal demographic biases. You will build a review interface that parses candidate submissions, highlights verifiable skills, and flags AI hallucinations.\n\n## Constraints & Requirements\n1. **Bias Mitigation**: Automatically mask non-job-relevant demographic proxies (e.g. school name, graduation date, candidate photo) under Fair Work guidelines.\n2. **Hallucination Verification**: Require human recruiters to verify that claimed skills actually appear in the raw resume excerpt before approving.\n3. **Interactive Filter**: Filter candidates by must-have skills (React, TypeScript) and minimum verified score.\n</challenge>\n\nCRITICAL: Respond ONLY with a valid JSON object strictly matching this schema:\n{\n  \""$schema\"": \""http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\"",\n  \""type\"": \""object\"",\n  \""properties\"": {\n    \""message\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""string\"",\n      \""description\"": \""What you tell the candidate: what you did, what you assumed, what to check.\""\n    },\n    \""files\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""array\"",\n      \""items\"": {\n        \""type\"": \""object\"",\n        \""properties\"": {\n          \""path\"": {\n            \""type\"": \""string\""\n          },\n          \""contents\"": {\n            \""type\"": \""string\""\n          }\n        },\n        \""required\"": [\n          \""path\"",\n          \""contents\""\n        ],\n        \""additionalProperties\"": false\n      },\n      \""description\"": \""Complete new contents of every file you create or change. Whole files only — never diffs.\""\n    },\n    \""reasoning\"": {\n      \""type\"": \""string\"",\n      \""description\"": \""Your own brief reasoning for this turn: interpretation, key decisions and any open doubts.\""\n    }\n  },\n  \""required\"": [\n    \""message\"",\n    \""files\"",\n    \""reasoning\""\n  ],\n  \""additionalProperties\"": false\n}""},{""role"":""user"",""content"":""<project_files>\n=== README.md ===\n# Reviewer console — starter data\n\nYou are prototyping a reviewer console for an AI feature that summarises employee\nsurvey comments. Everything here is **synthetic**: invented people, teams and\ncomments for a fictional company.\n\n## Data (`src/data/`)\n\n- `teams.json` — `departments` and `teams`. Each team belongs to one department.\n- `responses.json` — one entry per **respondent**: `{ id, teamId, comment }`.\n  `comment` may be an empty string.\n- `summaries.json` — output of a (stubbed) LLM summariser. Each summary covers a\n  `team` or a `department` (`groupType` / `groupId`) and has `themes`; each theme\n  has a `title`, a `summary` and the `claims` it makes.\n\nImport them directly, e.g. `import teams from \""./data/teams.json\""`.\n\n## Running\n\nThe preview is already live. Edit `src/App.jsx` and add files under `src/`.\n\n\n=== index.html ===\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\""en\"">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\""UTF-8\"" />\n    <meta name=\""viewport\"" content=\""width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"" />\n    <title>Candidate project</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\""root\""></div>\n    <script type=\""module\"" src=\""/src/main.jsx\""></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n=== package.json ===\n{\n  \""name\"": \""candidate-project\"",\n  \""private\"": true,\n  \""version\"": \""0.0.0\"",\n  \""type\"": \""module\"",\n  \""scripts\"": {\n    \""dev\"": \""vite\"",\n    \""build\"": \""vite build\""\n  },\n  \""dependencies\"": {\n    \""react\"": \""18.3.1\"",\n    \""react-dom\"": \""18.3.1\""\n  },\n  \""devDependencies\"": {\n    \""vite\"": \""5.4.21\""\n  },\n  \""overrides\"": {\n    \""rollup\"": \""npm:@rollup/wasm-node\""\n  }\n}\n\n\n=== src/App.jsx ===\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <main className=\""shell\"">\n      <h1>Your project starts here</h1>\n      <p>\n        Nothing has been built yet. Describe what you want in the chat and the\n        assistant will write the code.\n      </p>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n\n\n=== src/data/responses.json ===\n[\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r01\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Release train is monthly and everything piles in the last week. Rollback ownership is unclear, it feels like nobody's job.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r02\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""I like my team but the monthly release crunch is exhausting.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r03\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Would love clear rollback ownership when a release goes wrong.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r04\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Deploys are fine. Wish we had more time for tech debt.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r05\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Remote days are great, I'd happily do more of them.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r06\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r07\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Being the only person on the overnight reconciliation rota is wearing me down.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r08\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r09\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Good team. Tech debt keeps getting pushed.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r10\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""On-call is heavy and alerts are noisy.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r11\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Alert noise at night is the worst part of on-call.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r12\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""I'd like a clearer path to senior.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r13\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Planning is good this quarter, priorities finally feel stable.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r14\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r15\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Too many meetings on Tuesdays and Thursdays.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r16\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r17\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""On-call rota fairness could be better, same few people get paged.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r18\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Since I moved from the Perth office to lead the Atlas migration I've had no backup.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r19\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""App store approvals take forever and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r20\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Love the team, worried about headcount.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r21\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Customers get stuck at the data import step and the docs are out of date, so we field the same questions daily.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r22\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r23\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Import step is confusing for customers and the docs are out of date.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r24\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r25\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Would like more time with customers rather than internal meetings.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r26\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Ticket volume spikes after every release.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r27\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Ticket backlog after releases is stressful.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r28\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Good tooling, but escalation paths are unclear.\""\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""r29\"",\n    \""teamId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""comment\"": \""Enjoy the work, wish escalation to engineering was faster.\""\n  }\n]\n\n\n=== src/data/summaries.json ===\n[\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-payments\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""payments\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release pressure\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Engineers feel the monthly release train creates a last-week crunch, and rollback ownership is unclear.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The monthly release train creates a last-week crunch.\"",\n          \""Rollback ownership is unclear.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Working pattern\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Almost everyone in the team wants to work fully remotely.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Almost everyone wants to work fully remotely.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Wellbeing\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""One engineer covering the overnight reconciliation rota alone is close to burning out.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""One engineer covering the overnight reconciliation rota alone is close to burning out.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Tech debt\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Tech debt keeps being deprioritised.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Tech debt keeps being deprioritised.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-platform\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""platform\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""On-call load\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""On-call is heavy, alerts are noisy, and the load falls on the same few people.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""On-call is heavy.\"",\n          \""Alerts are noisy, especially at night.\"",\n          \""The same few people get paged.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Meetings\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""There is a Friday retro that people find useful.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""There is a Friday retro that people find useful.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Career growth\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""People want a clearer path to senior.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""People want a clearer path to senior.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-mobile\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""mobile\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Migration ownership\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""The Perth-based lead of the Atlas migration has no backup.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The Perth-based lead of the Atlas migration has no backup.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release approvals\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-onboarding\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""onboarding\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Import step\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Customers struggle with the data import step and the docs are out of date.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Customers struggle with the data import step.\"",\n          \""The docs are out of date.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-support\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""team\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""support\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Post-release load\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Ticket volume and backlog spike after releases.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Ticket volume spikes after releases.\"",\n          \""The ticket backlog after releases is stressful.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Escalation\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""Escalation paths to engineering are unclear and slow.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""Escalation paths are unclear.\"",\n          \""Escalation to engineering is slow.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \""id\"": \""s-engineering\"",\n    \""groupType\"": \""department\"",\n    \""groupId\"": \""engineering\"",\n    \""themes\"": [\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""Release and rollout friction\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""A monthly release crunch, unclear rollback ownership, and slow app store approvals that nobody owns.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""The monthly release train creates a last-week crunch.\"",\n          \""Rollback ownership is unclear.\"",\n          \""App store approvals are slow and nobody owns chasing them.\""\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \""title\"": \""On-call\"",\n        \""summary\"": \""On-call load is heavy and alerts are noisy.\"",\n        \""claims\"": [\n          \""On-call load is heavy.\"",\n          \""Alerts are noisy.\""\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\n\n=== src/data/teams.json ===\n{\n  \""departments\"": [\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""engineering\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""customer-success\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Customer Success\""\n    }\n  ],\n  \""teams\"": [\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""payments\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Payments\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""platform\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Platform\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""mobile\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Mobile\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""engineering\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""onboarding\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Onboarding\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""customer-success\""\n    },\n    {\n      \""id\"": \""support\"",\n      \""name\"": \""Support\"",\n      \""departmentId\"": \""customer-success\""\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n\n=== src/main.jsx ===\nimport { StrictMode } from \""react\"";\nimport { createRoot } from \""react-dom/client\"";\nimport App from \""./App.jsx\"";\nimport \""./styles.css\"";\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\""root\"")).render(\n  <StrictMode>\n    <App />\n  </StrictMode>\n);\n\n\n=== src/styles.css ===\n:root {\n  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, \""Segoe UI\"", Roboto, sans-serif;\n  color: #1a1a1a;\n  background: #fafaf9;\n}\n\n* {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}\n\nbody {\n  margin: 0;\n}\n\n.shell {\n  max-width: 720px;\n  margin: 12vh auto;\n  padding: 0 24px;\n}\n\n.shell h1 {\n  font-size: 1.6rem;\n  margin: 0 0 0.5rem;\n}\n\n.shell p {\n  color: #555;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n}\n\n\n=== vite.config.js ===\nimport { defineConfig } from \""vite\"";\n\n// No @vitejs/plugin-react on purpose: esbuild compiles JSX, which keeps\n// `npm install` fast inside the browser sandbox.\nexport default defineConfig({\n  esbuild: { jsx: \""automatic\"" },\n});\n\n</project_files>\n\nAct as a Principal Frontend Architect and HR Compliance Specialist familiar with Australian Fair Work guidelines (Fair Work Act 2009, s.351 protected attributes).\n\nWe are building an \""AI Resume Screener & Bias-Resistant Applicant Review\"" interface. Before writing any component or UI code, interrogate the brief to lock down domain boundaries, anti-bias masking rules, and verification contracts.\n\nTasks:\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix:\n   - Identify direct demographic attributes and indirect proxies that must be stripped in \""Blind Review Mode\"" under Fair Work principles (e.g., graduation year as an age proxy, institution names as socioeconomic/racial proxies, residential address/suburb, photos, gendered honorifics/pronouns).\n   - Formulate a strict schema separating `MaskedDemographics` from `VerifiableTechnicalData`.\n2. Hallucination Failure Taxonomy:\n   - Detail at least 4 ways AI screeners hallucinate candidate competency (e.g., semantic confusion like mistaking 'Vue reactivity' for 'React.js', inferring unstated frameworks from adjacent tools, attributing team achievements as individual skills, or fabrications based on project titles).\n3. Verification Invariants:\n   - Define the deterministic invariant: A skill cannot enter the \""Verified\"" state without an exact source excerpt span and recruiter confirmation.\n\nConclude with 3 clarifying questions on filtering thresholds and workflow edge cases before we proceed to component architecture.""}]}","{""role"":""assistant"",""content"":""{\n  \""message\"": \""No components or UI code were written yet, as requested. Here are the domain boundaries, masking rules, hallucination taxonomy, verification invariants, and three clarifying questions.\\n\\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix\\n\\nDirect protected attributes under Fair Work Act 2009 s.351 and related principles:\\nage, sex, gender, gender identity, sexual orientation, race, colour, ethnicity, national origin, disability, religion, marital status, pregnancy, family/carer status, political opinion, social origin.\\n\\nIndirect demographic proxies that must be masked in Blind Review Mode:\\n- School / university / institution names\\n- Graduation year or any education date range\\n- Residential address, suburb, postcode\\n- Candidate photo / headshot\\n- Gendered honorifics and pronouns\\n- Full legal name, especially given/family name (use neutral candidate ID)\\n- Visa status, citizenship, nationality\\n- Languages (unless the role explicitly requires a language)\\n- Professional memberships that reveal religion, race, gender, disability, or political affiliation\\n- Age-correlated exact dates, e.g. date of birth, early career start year\\n- Portfolio URLs or personal sites that may reveal demographic information\\n\\nStrict schema separation:\\n\\nMaskedDemographics:\\n  direct: age, sex, gender, race, ethnicity, disability, religion, sexual orientation, marital status, pregnancy, family status, political opinion, national origin\\n  indirect: institutionName, schoolName, graduationYear, educationDateRange, address, suburb, postcode, photo, honorific, pronoun, givenName, familyName, visaStatus, citizenship, nationality, languages, professionalMemberships, dateOfBirth, ageRelatedDates, identifyingUrls\\n\\nVerifiableTechnicalData:\\n  candidateId\\n  maskedResumeText (raw text with all MaskedDemographics redacted)\\n  technicalClaims[] as:\\n    skillId\\n    name (normalised, e.g. lowercase)\\n    sourceExcerpts[] as:\\n      text (exact substring)\\n      start (character offset)\\n      end (character offset)\\n    suggestedByAI (boolean)\\n    verificationStatus: unverified | verified | rejected\\n    recruiterConfirmations[] as:\\n      recruiterId\\n      timestamp\\n      decision\\n  projects[] / experience[] containing only job-relevant technical detail\\n\\nRecruiters never need unmasked demographic data to verify technical claims.\\n\\n2. Hallucination Failure Taxonomy\\n\\nAt least four distinct AI hallucination modes:\\n\\n1. Semantic / technology confusion: mistaking Vue reactivity for React.js, React Native for React, or AngularJS for Angular.\\n2. Adjacent-tool inference: seeing Webpack, Babel, or Next.js and inferring React proficiency without any direct mention of React; seeing Tailwind and inferring CSS expertise; seeing Redis and inferring a backend language.\\n3. Team/project attribution: a resume says the team built a feature or the candidate worked on a React codebase, and the AI promotes that into individual ownership such as led React architecture or built the system alone.\\n4. Fabrication from titles or project names: inferring skills purely from job titles like React Developer or project names like React dashboard without supporting bullet evidence.\\n5. Course/certificate inflation: completing a React course or bootcamp is extracted as production-level React competency when the resume does not claim production use.\\n6. Keyword false positives: matching the word reactive or reaction and treating it as React, or matching the word type in prose as TypeScript.\\n\\n3. Verification Invariants\\n\\nA skill cannot enter the Verified state unless all of these are true:\\n- The skill has at least one exact source excerpt span in the raw, masked, technical resume text.\\n- The source excerpt exactly supports the claimed skill; fuzzy, inferred, or adjacent-tool matches do not qualify.\\n- A human recruiter explicitly confirms the match in the review UI.\\n- The confirmation is recorded with recruiter ID and timestamp.\\n\\nAdditional deterministic invariants:\\n- AI confidence, job title, project title, or another skill can suggest a claim but can never verify it.\\n- A claimed skill with no exact source excerpt must remain Unverified or be marked Unsupported.\\n- If a recruiter rejects a previously verified skill, its status returns to Rejected/Disputed and must not count toward the verified score.\\n- Masking is applied before verification. Demographic spans are never shown to the recruiter in the verification excerpt; only the technical evidence is shown.\\n\\n3 Clarifying Questions Before Component Architecture\\n\\n1. Filtering thresholds: Should must-have skills mean every must-have skill is individually verified, or should candidates pass if they have verified evidence for at least N of them? How should minimum verified score be calculated: binary per skill, weighted by relevance, or based on the number of verified source excerpts?\\n\\n2. Workflow edge cases: If a candidate has three mentions of React, one exact and two inferred, is React considered verified or only partially verified? If a recruiter later rejects a previously confirmed skill, should the candidate be re-evaluated immediately and possibly removed from shortlist?\\n\\n3. Audit and unmasking: Do you need an immutable audit log of every verification/rejection action with recruiter ID and timestamp? Should Blind Review Mode be default-on and irreversible for a candidate, or should an authorised recruiter be able to override masking with a recorded reason?\"",\n  \""files\"": [],\n  \""reasoning\"": \""The candidate asked for domain analysis before UI code, so I produced no files. I assumed raw resume text is available and that verification requires exact spans anchored to masked technical text. I kept the three clarifying questions focused on filtering thresholds, workflow edge cases, and audit/override behaviour because those materially affect the data model and component state design.\""\n}"",""reasoning_content"":""We need respond JSON. Need understand task: They want before code, interrogate brief, produce domain boundaries, anti-bias masking rules, verification contracts, hallucination taxonomy, verification invariants, three clarifying questions. No component code. We must output JSON object message, files empty? The instruction from user: \""Conclude with 3 clarifying questions on filtering thresholds and workflow edge cases before we proceed to component architecture.\"" They want analysis, no files. So files empty. Message should include deliverables? likely summary and the three questions. But message can be more detailed? They asked to \""Detail at least 4...\"" etc. We can provide in message as prose, maybe structured. But message field is string. Need maybe include all content concisely. files empty. reasoning short.\n\nNeed not write code. They asked \""Act as Principal Frontend Architect and HR Compliance Specialist...\"" Tasks 1-3 and questions. So output only JSON, no files. We can include full deliverables in message. Keep clear and concise. Since message is what tell candidate. Could be long but okay. Need ensure no files. reasoning mention no code yet.\n\nLet's craft message:\n\n\""Before UI, here are boundaries...\""\n\nDemographic Proxy Matrix:\nDirect: age, sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, race, ethnicity, disability, religion, marital status, pregnancy, family/carer status, political opinion, national extraction, social origin.\nIndirect proxies: school/university names, graduation year, date ranges, photo, residential address/suburb, gendered honorifics/pronouns, names, visa/citizenship status, languages (unless required), memberships (women in tech, cultural clubs), age-correlated years of experience (e.g., \""20 years\"" maybe not mask? careful: may indicate age but if relevant? Under Fair Work, direct experience might be job-relevant; but exact year dates are proxy. We should distinguish: years of experience is relevant; dates/grad years are proxies. Masking dates but not necessarily years? Need formulate.\n- Address/suburb (socioeconomic/racial proxy), photo, birthplace, nationality, languages unless required, university names, school names, graduation years, age-specific dates, gender pronouns/honorifics, names (first/last), membership affiliations, religious/cultural organizations, age ranges.\nSchema:\nMaskedDemographics:\n  direct: { age, sex, gender, race, ethnicity, disability, religion, sexual orientation, maritalStatus, familyStatus, politicalOpinion, nationalOrigin }\n  indirect: { institutionName, schoolName, graduationYear, address, suburb, postcode, photo, honorific, pronoun, givenName, familyName, visaStatus, citizenship, languages, professionalMemberships, dateOfBirth, ageRelatedDateRanges, headshot, URL/portfolio with identifying info }\nVerifiableTechnicalData:\n  rawResumeText (with mask applied, spans for each masked token), claimedSkills[]? Must include: skillName, confidence, source excerpts, verification status: \""unverified\"", \""verified\"", \""rejected\""; verification requires exact source span + recruiter confirmation; projects/experience separate but verifiable.\n\nHallucination taxonomy at least 4:\n1 Semantic/technology confusion: \""Vue reactivity\"" mistaken for \""React.js\""; \""React Native\"" counted as React; \""AngularJS\"" vs Angular.\n2 Transitive/adjacent tool inference: mentions Webpack → assumes React; mentions Tailwind → assumes CSS framework but no direct claim; mentions Next.js → assumes React (though Next uses React but may not be claimed as skill) or Redis → assumes backend language.\n3 Team/project attribution: resume states \""team built X\"" / \""worked on React codebase\"" but recruiter/AI attributes individual \""Led React architecture\""; \""contributed to migration\"" becomes \""migrated\"".\n4 Fabrication from titles/keywords: job title \""React Developer\"" or project \""React dashboard\"" used to infer deep skill not supported; bullet says \""used modern JS frameworks\"" interpreted as React, Node, etc.\n5 Implicit skill from certification/course: \""completed React course\"" treated as production proficiency.\n6 N-gram false positive: name contains \""React\"" (e.g., \""reactive systems\"") matched. At least four. We'll include 6.\n\nVerification invariants:\n- Each candidate skill has state machine: Claimed (from AI extraction) → NeedsReview → Verified/Rejected.\n- Verified iff: (1) skill has at least one `excerpt` with exact substring (maybe normalized case/whitespace but exact span offsets) from raw resume text; (2) a human recruiter confirms the match in UI; (3) the confirmation is recorded with timestamp/id.\n- No automatic promotion based on confidence, title, project name, or other skills. AI can suggest but not verify.\n- If a claimed skill has no exact source excerpt, it must be marked \""Unsupported\"" and cannot be verified unless another exact quote is found and confirmed.\n- Masking cannot be reversed; raw resume preserved for verification only, but demographic spans excluded (or hidden in blind mode? In verification, recruiters see raw excerpt but with demographic masks; they verify technical skills, not demographic attributes). Important: Must not expose masked demographic proxies in verification UI. Raw technical text with demographics redacted.\n\nClarifying questions (3):\n1 Filtering thresholds: For must-have skills (React, TypeScript), should the filter require verified presence of both? What minimum verified score (0-100) and how weighted? Does \""Verified\"" per skill count as binary (yes/no) or weighted confidence from recruiter (maybe verified/exact vs partial)? If candidate lacks a required skill but has strong adjacent verified skills, should they be included as \""near-miss\""?\n2 Workflow edge cases: If candidate has same skill mentioned multiple times with one exact verified source and one hallucination rejected, how should overall skill status be set? If recruiter rejects a claimed skill after previously verifying, does that reset score? Should a candidate with missing/empty resume be auto-fail or hidden until required fields present?\n3 Audit/compliance: Need we log every verification/rejection with timestamp and recruiter ID, and allow unmasking override with a reason/approval? Should Blind Review Mode be default on and irreversible per candidate, or can authorized recruiters unmask after shortlisting?\n\nNeed maybe mention \""no files changed.\"" Good.\n\nBut user asked \""Conclude with 3 clarifying questions...\"" They may expect exactly three. We'll number them.\n\nNow output JSON valid. message string with content. Avoid markdown maybe but can include newlines. Need escape? We can include \\n in JSON string. We can use plain text with numbered sections. Fine.\n\nFiles: [].\n\nReasoning: \""No code requested; produced compliance/domain analysis and questions only. Assumed raw resume text is available and verifiable spans can be anchored to it. Need candidate to answer threshold/workflow questions before architecture.\"" Keep concise.\n\nNow ensure JSON valid. Use \""files\"":[], \""reasoning\"": \""...\"". No extra fields.\n\nLet's craft carefully.\n\nCould include more detail than maybe candidate expects; okay.\n\nLet's write message:\n\n```\nBefore components, here are boundaries.\n\n1. Demographic Proxy Matrix\nProtected/direct...\nIndirect proxies to mask...\nSchema separation:\nMaskedDemographics: { direct: {...}, indirect: {...} }\nVerifiableTechnicalData: { maskedText, technicalSpans, skills:[{id,name,sourceExcerpts:[{text,start,end}],verificationStatus,recruiterConfirmations:[]}], projects, experience, educationTechnicalOnly }\n...\n```\n\nNeed define exact schema? \""Formulate a strict schema separating...\"" Could include JSON-ish.\n\nLet's include strict schema in message.\n\nPotential issue: \""mask candidate photo\"" no photo in data but yes.\n\nNeed maybe mention \""School name and graduation date\"" are proxies; but in resume, perhaps institution names are sometimes relevant for skills? Under Fair Work, not relevant for most tech roles. Good.\n\n\""Hallucination Failure Taxonomy\"" detail 6 types.\n\n\""Verification Invariants\"" define.\n\n\""3 Clarifying Questions\"".\n\nNo files.\n\nEnsure message not too huge? But okay. Could be around 1000 words. The message field can hold.\n\nLet's produce with \\n escapes. Need ensure valid JSON string, so any double quotes inside should be escaped or use single quotes? JSON string uses double quotes; if content has double quotes, escape. To avoid, use no double quotes or use single quotes. But valid JSON requires quotes around property; inside string double quotes need escaping. We can avoid using double quotes by using plain prose with single quotes. That's simpler. But when showing JSON schema, need quotes; we can either avoid structured JSON or escape. Could use pseudo-schema with arrow. Better avoid nested quotes. Use colon/dash prose. That's fine.\n\nLet's craft final.""}","{""response_format"":""{\""type\"":\""json_object\""}"",""turnSeq"":""1"",""challengeTitle"":""Full-Stack Engineer — AI Resume Screener & Bias Filter"",""maxTokens"":""32000"",""effort"":""medium"",""stage"":""assistant""}","48750","","[""assistant"",""workspace_chat""]","140599a636cf63b237b309c9061d5be9d53ade3f","t-6e26ce74-5cfe-46fc-98b8-521df273cb6c","[]"
