rm effectively in the organisation, with an appreciation of how own role contributes to the business.\"",\n    \""businessSkills\"": \""Demonstrates effective communication and negotiation skills, understands and uses appropriate methods, tools and applications, and takes initiative in personal development.\""\n  }\n}\n\nCompany: Unknown\n\nJob Description:\nAbout the job\nI am partnering with a leading financial services organisation to find a highly skilled Senior AI Engineer to join a high-impact AI Enablement team.\nThis team builds the foundational platforms that empower other business areas to deploy advanced AI agents. They are specifically looking for a candidate who comes from a traditional Software Engineering background rather than a pure Data Science or Machine Learning profile who has successfully transitioned into an AI-focused engineering role. I am looking for someone with exceptionally strong core programming fundamentals who understands how to build the enterprise-grade infrastructure that makes AI integration possible.\nKey Responsibilities\nPlatform Engineering: Design and build scalable backend systems that enable the enterprise-wide deployment of AI agents.\nIntegration & Optimisation: Architect AI/ML pipelines to integrate seamlessly into existing enterprise software, writing robust code in Python and Java.\nData Utilisation: Leverage Graph technologies to structure and optimise complex data relationships for AI applications.\nModern Tooling: Utilise Large Language Models (LLMs) and state-of-the-art AI coding tools to solve complex engineering bottlenecks.\n\nRequired Skills & Experience\nCareer Pathway: A strong foundational background in traditional Software Engineering, followed by a recent transition into an AI Engineering role.\nTechnical Stack: Extensive commercial development experience in Python and Java, alongside proven hands-on work with Graph databases.\nAI Integration: Practical knowledge of embedding AI/ML pipelines into software architectures, rather than building or training models from scratch.\nProblem Solving: Exceptional analytical capability to troubleshoot and scale infrastructure in a complex, high-availability environment.""}]}","{""role"":""assistant"",""content"":""{\n  \""title\"": \""Compliant AI Transaction Categorization Service\"",\n  \""companyName\"": \""Australis Bank\"",\n  \""briefMarkdown\"": \""## Context\\nAustralis Bank, a leading Australian financial services organisation, is building an AI Enablement platform to help internal teams deploy AI agents safely. As part of this initiative, you are tasked with implementing a **Transaction Categorization Microservice** that leverages a Large Language Model (LLM) to classify bank transaction records into spending categories.\\n\\nThe service will be used by our retail banking division to enrich transaction data for downstream analytics and customer insights. It must operate within our strict regulatory environment, adhering to the **Privacy Act 1988 (Cth)**, **APRA CPS 234**, and **Consumer Data Right (CDR) rules** where applicable.\\n\\n## Task\\nDesign and implement a stateless microservice in TypeScript (Node.js) that:\\n1. Accepts a batch of raw transaction records via REST API.\\n2. For each transaction, calls an LLM (mock or real) to predict a spending category and confidence score.\\n3. Stores the categorized transactions in a graph database (simulate with in-memory or interface) while maintaining relationships (e.g., account -> transactions -> categories).\\n4. Produces an audit log of all actions, ensuring that all personally identifiable information (PII) is masked.\\n5. Aggregates totals per category by summing individual transaction amounts (in cents).\\n\\nThe solution must be production-ready, emphasising correctness, reliability, and compliance.\\n\\n## Requirements\\n### Input\\n- `POST /categorize` with JSON body containing an array of `TransactionRecord` objects (see starterSchemas).\\n- Each transaction has: unique `id`, `accountId`, `amountInCents` (integer, always positive), `description` (free text), and `timestamp` (ISO 8601 string in UTC).\\n\\n### LLM Integration\\n- Use a mocked LLM function that returns `{ category: string, confidence: number }` for a given description. You may implement a simple deterministic mock (e.g., keyword matching) but the code must be designed to easily swap in a real LLM API client.\\n- **Important**: The LLM may return unpredictable output (e.g., confidence as a float between 0 and 1, or category not in allowed list). You must validate and sanitize the response before persisting.\\n\\n### Compliance and Business Rules\\n1. **Monetary amounts** must be handled as integers (cents) at all times. Never use floating-point numbers for money.\\n2. **Category list** is fixed: `Groceries`, `Dining`, `Transport`, `Utilities`, `Entertainment`, `Other`. Any unknown category from LLM must fallback to `Other` and flag for review.\\n3. **Confidence score** must be stored as an integer 0-100. If LLM returns a float between 0 and 1, convert correctly (multiply by 100 and round). If outside range, clamp"",""reasoning_content"":""We need to produce JSON with fields: title, companyName, briefMarkdown, technicalInvariants, starterSchemas. The SFIA level is 3, primary skills PROG, DESN, DBDS. Company is Unknown, but job description says \""leading financial services organisation\"" in Australia (implied by Australian statutory context). We need to embed Australian regulatory/industry constraints, inject AI traps, define TypeScript interfaces. The brief should be for a work-sample assessment for a Senior AI Engineer role, but SFIA level 3 seems mid-level? Actually Senior AI Engineer might be higher, but SFIA level 3 is described as \""applies standard methods, works under general direction, resolves issues, may supervise\"". We'll align with that. The company is financial services, so Australian regulations: Privacy Act 1988, APRA prudential standards, maybe CDR (Consumer Data Right) / Open Banking. AI traps: floating-point arithmetic for currency, logging unmasked sensitive fields, aggregate gross calculation instead of statutory breakdowns (like STP Phase 2 for payroll, but this is financial services maybe more relevant: GST calculation? superannuation contributions? Or incorrect handling of dates/timezones? We need to integrate AI in financial services. The job is about AI enablement platform. So the work sample could be building a component that uses LLM to process financial documents? Or building a pipeline that integrates AI for classification with regulatory compliance.\n\nWe need to create a realistic assessment brief. The brief should ask the candidate to design/build a small system or component that demonstrates their ability to build AI integration with constraints. It should include TypeScript interfaces (starter schemas) and technical invariants (like no floats for money, data masking, etc).\n\nWe need to inject 2-3 AI traps: examples where naive AI generation would be wrong. Common traps: using floating-point for currency, not handling timezones properly (e.g., AEST vs AEST/AEDT and AWST), using aggregate totals instead of breakdowns (like if calculating tax, need each component), unmasked sensitive fields in logs, incorrect handling of PHI/PII, etc.\n\nSince financial services and AI, maybe the task is: Build a compliant transaction categorization service using LLM, with data masking and audit logging. Or build an API endpoint that integrates an LLM to extract financial entities, but with strict data handling. Use Graph database for relationships? Could involve Neo4j or similar. But TypeScript? The job mentions Python and Java, but starterSchemas are TypeScript interfaces. So maybe the assessment is in TypeScript (Node.js) to evaluate core programming, but they can use Python if they want? The brief should specify TypeScript for interfaces.\n\nWe need to create a valid JSON object with exact fields. Let's structure:\n\n- title: \""AI-Enabled Financial Document Processing Service\"" or something.\n- companyName: \""A leading Australian financial services organisation\"" or a specific fictional name? The prompt says company is Unknown, but we can create a plausible name: \""Australis Financial Group\"" or similar. But since it's unknown, maybe we can say \""Confidential Financial Services Client\"" but better to give a name for authenticity. Use \""Southern Cross Financial Group\"".\n- briefMarkdown: A markdown string describing the task, context, requirements, constraints, Definition of Done, etc. Include Australian regulatory references. Include AI traps as subtle hints? Actually traps are part of the scenario; we need to embed them so that naive LLM output would be wrong. We should not explicitly warn, but the technical invariants should capture the correct approach.\n- technicalInvariants: An array or object listing strict rules that must be followed, e.g., \""Use integer cents for all monetary amounts\"", \""Never log unmasked customer identifiers\"", \""Calculate GST by component, not on aggregate\"", \""Handle timezone conversion using IANA timezone database, not fixed offsets\"", etc.\n- starterSchemas: Object containing TypeScript interfaces or types relevant to the task. Could be multiple interfaces.\n\nWe need to make it Level 3 SFIA: complexity moderate, work reviewed at milestones. So task should be scoped for a few hours/days.\n\nDomain: financial services, AI enablement team building platforms for AI agents. So the candidate might be asked to design a service that integrates an LLM for document classification, but with compliance.\n\nLet's define the task: Build a microservice that ingests bank transaction records, uses an LLM to categorize them, and stores the result in a graph database for fraud detection or customer insights. Must comply with Privacy Act, APRA CPS 234 (information security), and CDR if applicable. Data masking, audit logging, monetary amounts in cents, Australian timezone handling. AI traps: using floats for currency, logging raw data, not handling daylight saving, using aggregate amounts for regulatory reporting.\n\nBut we need to be specific: maybe the service is for \""Financial Document Analysis\"" where we extract key data from invoices using LLM, and then compute GST. GST in Australia is 10% and must be calculated per line item, not on total. That's a trap: naive LLM might compute GST on total, but if some items are GST-free or have different codes, that's wrong. Also floating point issue.\n\nAnother trap: using LLM to extract dates and interpreting them in wrong timezone.\n\nAlternatively, the task could be: \""Design an API that takes a loan application document, uses LLM to extract fields, and calculates loan repayments.\"" But that involves interest calculations, where floating point is a trap.\n\nGiven the job is about AI enablement, the candidate is building infrastructure, not necessarily the AI model itself. So they might build a pipeline that orchestrates LLM calls, applies business rules, and enforces compliance. The work sample could be to implement a TypeScript middleware that integrates with an LLM API (like OpenAI) to categorize transactions, but with strict validation, masking, and logging.\n\nWe need starterSchemas: define TypeScript interfaces for input, output, configuration, etc. For example:\n\ninterface TransactionRecord { id: string; amountInCents: number; description: string; timestamp: string; /* ISO 8601 with timezone */ }\ninterface CategorizationResult { category: string; confidence: number; }\ninterface AuditLogEntry { timestamp: string; userId: string; action: string; maskedData: string; } etc.\n\nTechnical invariants list:\n- Monetary values must be represented as integers (cents) and never as floating-point numbers.\n- All date/time values must be stored in UTC and converted to local Australian timezones (AEST/AEDT) for display, using IANA timezone names (e.g., 'Australia/Sydney'), never fixed offsets.\n- Personally identifiable information (PII) must be masked in all logs and audit trails, in accordance with Privacy Act 1988 (APP 6 and 11).\n- LLM outputs must be validated against a schema; any field failing validation must trigger fallback rules and be flagged for human review.\n- The service must be idempotent; retrying the same request must not duplicate records.\n- For any aggregated reporting (e.g., total amount categorized), the aggregation must be computed from individual categorized line items, not from an LLM-provided summary.\n- Graph database relationships must be lossless; no information from the original record may be dropped without explicit transformation rules.\n\nNow the briefMarkdown: Write a realistic scenario. Perhaps: \""As part of our AI Enablement team, you are tasked with building a transaction categorization microservice for our retail banking division. The service will receive bank transaction records, use an LLM to classify them into spending categories, and store results in a graph database for downstream analytics. You will need to integrate with our existing LLM gateway, implement data masking, audit logging, and ensure compliance with Australian regulations. The service will be deployed in our Kubernetes cluster and must be stateless. You are to deliver a TypeScript implementation with interfaces provided in starterSchemas. The solution will be evaluated on correctness of the implementation, adherence to technical invariants, and code quality.\""\n\nThen include detailed requirements: input format, API contract, LLM integration (mock or real), validation rules, error handling, logging requirements, etc.\n\nDefinition of Done: list of criteria.\n\nInject AI traps subtly: In the scenario, mention that the LLM returns confidence scores as float but you must convert to a fixed-point scale? Or that the LLM sometimes returns amount as number, but you must ensure it's in cents. Or that you must compute total spending per category by summing line items, not using the LLM's total.\n\nWe need to ensure the JSON is valid and only output JSON. No extra text.\n\nLet's craft each field.\n\nTitle: \""Compliant AI Transaction Categorization Service\""\n\nCompanyName: \""Southern Cross Financial Group\"" (fictional, but plausible). Or \""Australis Bank\"". Use \""Australis Bank\"".\n\nBriefMarkdown: I'll write a concise but thorough markdown. Include sections: Context, Task, Requirements, Constraints (with Australian regs), Evaluation Criteria, Definition of Done. Use markdown formatting.\n\nTechnicalInvariants: array of strings.\n\nStarterSchemas: object with multiple TypeScript interface definitions as strings? Or as actual type definitions? The instruction says \""starterSchemas\"" so likely an object where each key is a schema name and value is the TypeScript code as a string, or a structured type? To be safe, provide as string containing the code. Or as object with interface definitions? The prompt doesn't specify structure exactly, but typical would be an object with keys and string values containing TS code. I'll do that: e.g., \""TransactionRecord\"": \""export interface TransactionRecord { ... }\"".\n\nLet's define interfaces:\n\n- TransactionRecord: id: string; accountId: string; amountInCents: number; description: string; timestamp: string; // ISO 8601 UTC\n- CategorizationResult: transactionId: string; category: string; subcategory?: string; confidence: number; // 0-100 integer\n- CategorySummary: category: string; totalAmountInCents: number; count: number;\n- AuditLogEntry: timestamp: string; actorId: string; action: string; resourceId: string; maskedData: string; outcome: 'SUCCESS' | 'FAILURE';\n- LLMResponse: maybe raw response shape.\n- ServiceConfig: includes LLM gateway URL, masking patterns, etc.\n\nWe'll include a few.\n\nNow build JSON. Ensure no newline issues, use proper escaping for markdown (backticks, etc). In JSON, markdown with backticks must be escaped or use single quotes? Actually double quotes in markdown need escaping. We'll keep it simple, avoid excessive quotes. Use plain text with line breaks represented as \\n.\n\nLet's write the briefMarkdown as a single string with \\n for newlines. Use backticks for code snippets; need to escape backticks in JSON string with backslash? Actually JSON string can contain backticks without escaping, they are not special. Only double quote and backslash need escaping. So we can include backticks as is.\n\nBut for readability, I'll produce the JSON with proper escaping in the final output.\n\nNow produce the JSON object.""}","{""response_format"":""{\""type\"":\""json_object\""}"",""maxTokens"":""3000"",""stage"":""challenge""}","","","[""challenge""]","140599a636cf63b237b309c9061d5be9d53ade3f","","[]"
